// Hebrew word definitions (hover/tap glossary)
// Extracted from SWRV_Genesis_Study monolith.

window.DEFINITIONS = {"beginning":{"hebrew":"בְּרֵאשִׁית","translit":"bereishit","strongs":"H7225","def":"From rosh (head, chief). Absolute starting point AND primacy of rank. Not just 'first in time' — first in importance, source.","ane":"In ANE creation accounts, the 'beginning' is the moment a god establishes order from chaos. Genesis claims YHWH alone is the prime cause.","cross":"John 1:1 (en arche); Proverbs 8:22","plain":"The very start of everything — and the source it all flows from."},"God":{"hebrew":"אֱלֹהִים","translit":"Elohim","strongs":"H430","def":"Plural noun (-im) used with singular verbs. Plural of majesty/fullness, NOT polytheism. Encompasses divine power, sovereignty, transcendence.","ane":"Other ANE peoples used 'el' for their gods. Hebrew Elohim is the same root but uniquely paired with singular verbs — declaring this is the ONE God who contains all divine power.","cross":"Genesis 1:26 (Let US make...)","plain":"The one true God — all-powerful, the maker of everything."},"LORD":{"hebrew":"יְהוָה","translit":"YHWH","strongs":"H3068","def":"The Tetragrammaton — the Self-Existent One. Connected to 'I AM THAT I AM' (Ex 3:14). Personal covenant name of God.","ane":"Pagan gods had functional names (Baal=master, Ra=sun). YHWH's name is being itself — not a function. He is the source of existence.","cross":"Exodus 3:14","plain":"God's personal name. It means 'the One who always exists.'"},"Lord":{"hebrew":"אֲדֹנָי","translit":"Adonai","strongs":"H136","def":"Master, lord, sovereign. Spoken aloud in place of YHWH out of reverence. Literally 'my Lord' (plural of majesty).","ane":"Standard ANE term for a king or master. Applied to YHWH it acknowledges Him as supreme Sovereign over all.","plain":"Master and ruler — a title of honor for God."},"created":{"hebrew":"בָּרָא","translit":"bara","strongs":"H1254","root":"Possibly cognate with 'to cut, separate.' But its theological usage is unique.","senses":["To create — uniquely DIVINE action; subject is ALWAYS God in the Tanakh","To bring into being something genuinely new — not reshape existing material","To create ex nihilo (out of nothing) — at least at the absolute beginning","To create as an expression of sovereign will — speech-act creation in Genesis 1"],"visual":"Something coming into existence that was NOT there before. Not the potter shaping clay (that's yatzar). Not the carpenter building (that's banah). Bara is the act only God can do — calling reality forth.","ane":"In ANE creation myths, gods shape pre-existing chaotic matter. The Babylonian Enuma Elish has Marduk forming the world from the slain body of the chaos-goddess Tiamat. Genesis declares something radically different: YHWH bara'd. He didn't fight chaos; He spoke and there was no chaos.","kingdom":"Only the King creates ex nihilo. Vice-regents (humans) make and shape (asah, yatzar) — but only YHWH creates (bara). Our creativity reflects His but never replicates the absolute origination.","greek":"LXX: ἐποίησεν (epoiēsen) — 'made.' This is a TRANSLATION LOSS. Greek had no equivalent for divine ex nihilo creation, so they used the general 'made.' Hebrew preserves the unique theological force.","cross":"Genesis 1:1, 1:21, 1:27, 2:3, 5:1; Isaiah 40:26, 41:20, 43:1, 65:17; Romans 4:17 ('calleth those things which be not as though they were'); Hebrews 11:3","plain":"Made something brand-new that never existed before — something only God can do."},"create":{"hebrew":"בָּרָא","translit":"bara","strongs":"H1254","def":"Same as 'created' — divine creation ex nihilo.","plain":"To make something brand-new out of nothing — only God does this."},"made":{"hebrew":"עָשָׂה","translit":"asah","strongs":"H6213","def":"To do, fashion, make. More general than bara — describes God shaping or humans crafting.","ane":"The pairing of bara and asah in Genesis 1: God both originates AND shapes.","plain":"Shaped or put together. More general than 'created.'"},"make":{"hebrew":"עָשָׂה","translit":"asah","strongs":"H6213","def":"Same as 'made.' To fashion, accomplish.","plain":"To shape, build, or put something together."},"formed":{"hebrew":"יָצַר","translit":"yatzar","strongs":"H3335","def":"To form, fashion (as a potter forms clay). Gen 2:7 — God formed the man from dust.","ane":"Pottery imagery: the maker has total authority over the made. Isaiah picks this up: 'Shall the clay say to the potter?' (Isa 29:16).","plain":"Shaped by hand, like a potter shaping clay. God formed the first man from dust."},"form":{"hebrew":"יָצַר","translit":"yatzar","strongs":"H3335","def":"Same as 'formed' — potter language.","plain":"To shape by hand, the way a potter shapes clay."},"dominion":{"hebrew":"רָדָה","translit":"radah","strongs":"H7287","root":"Primary meaning: to tread, trample (as a wine-press). Extended to 'rule' because rulers metaphorically 'tread upon' their domain.","senses":["To rule, exercise sovereignty","To tread, trample (as treading grapes in a winepress)","To subjugate (use of force in some contexts)","Royal kingship terminology — what kings do"],"visual":"A king walking through his territory, his authority felt by everyone he passes. The image of treading is concrete — feet making contact with the ground, marking it as YOURS.","ane":"Royal verb. In ANE texts only kings 'rdh.' For Genesis 1:26-28 to give this verb to ALL humanity is staggering — every human is given royal authority over creation.","warning":"Radah is NOT a license for exploitation. The same Adam given radah was placed in the garden 'to dress it and to keep it' (avad-shamar — to serve and guard). Kingly rule in the biblical pattern is SERVANT-RULE, not tyranny. Christ embodies this: 'the Son of Man came not to be served but to serve' (Mark 10:45).","kingdom":"This is the verb of vice-regency. Humans were created to RULE — not to be ruled by creation, not to flee from it, not to merely survive in it. We were made to GOVERN it as God's deputies.","greek":"LXX: ἄρχω (archō) — to rule, govern.","cross":"Genesis 1:26, 1:28; Psalm 8:6, 72:8, 110:2; 1 Peter 2:9 (royal priesthood); Revelation 5:10 (we shall reign on the earth)","plain":"Being in charge — ruling over something, the way a king rules his land."},"subdue":{"hebrew":"כָּבַשׁ","translit":"kabash","strongs":"H3533","root":"Primary meaning: to tread down, to bring under foot.","senses":["To subjugate, bring under control","To conquer (used of military conquest, Num 32:22)","To bring into submission","To master (something wild or unruly)"],"visual":"Putting your foot on something to claim it. Bringing wild ground into cultivation. Taming what was untamed. Imposing order on what was chaotic but not evil — like a farmer turning fallow ground into a field.","ane":"Conquest language. The earth in Genesis 1 was created GOOD but not yet EDEN. Eden was a garden within the world. Humanity's job was to extend Eden's order over the rest of the earth — to KABASH it. To make it all sacred space.","kingdom":"Together with radah (rule), kabash (subdue) is the kingdom mandate. Not rape of the earth — extension of order, beauty, fruitfulness, the King's reign. The wild becomes garden under the vice-regent's hands.","greek":"LXX: κατακυριεύω (katakyrieuō) — to lord over, master.","cross":"Genesis 1:28; Joshua 18:1; 1 Chronicles 22:18; Micah 7:19 ('he will subdue our iniquities')","plain":"To bring something wild under control and put it to good use."},"image":{"hebrew":"צֶלֶם","translit":"tselem","strongs":"H6754","root":"From a root meaning 'to shade' or 'represent.' A tselem is a statue or representation that stands in for the original.","senses":["Statue, idol — physical representation of a deity (Num 33:52)","Image — concrete likeness, not abstract resemblance","Representative — one who stands in place of another","Royal authority symbol — a king's tselem placed in conquered cities declared HIS rule there"],"visual":"A standing statue, the kind a king set up in distant provinces. The statue represented the king's PERSON and his AUTHORITY. To deface the statue was to attack the king. To honor the statue was to honor him. Now imagine: HUMANITY is God's tselem — His standing representation on the earth.","ane":"ANE kings were called 'tselem' of their gods. Pharaoh was tselem of Ra. The Assyrian king was tselem of Marduk. Only the king. Genesis 1:26-28 DEMOCRATIZES this radically — every human is tselem of YHWH. Every person you meet is a king's statue. THIS is the foundation of human dignity.","kingdom":"Tselem is the basis of vice-regency. We are not subjects of the King — we are His statues, placed on earth to declare 'YHWH rules here.' Our calling is to represent His character, exercise His authority, and extend His order. Sin is treason against this calling. Redemption is restoration of the image.","theology":"Genesis 9:6 makes tselem the basis of the prohibition against murder: kill a human, you've attacked the King's representation. Colossians 1:15 says CHRIST is 'the image (eikon) of the invisible God' — He is tselem in fullness. We are conformed to HIS image (Rom 8:29).","greek":"LXX: εἰκών (eikon). NT picks this up to describe Christ as the perfect image (Col 1:15; 2 Cor 4:4) and believers as being conformed to His image (Rom 8:29; 2 Cor 3:18).","cross":"Genesis 1:26-27, 5:1, 9:6; Colossians 1:15, 3:10; 2 Corinthians 3:18, 4:4; Romans 8:29; James 3:9","plain":"A stand-in that represents someone. Humans are made to represent God on earth."},"likeness":{"hebrew":"דְּמוּת","translit":"demut","strongs":"H1823","root":"From damah (to resemble)","senses":["Likeness, similarity","Pattern or model","Functional correspondence (not just appearance)"],"visual":"A pattern transferred — a master's design replicated in the apprentice's work","ane":"Demut paired with tselem (image) = FULL REPRESENTATIONAL IDENTITY. The two together communicate: humans are not just SHAPED LIKE God, we FUNCTION as His representatives — ruling, creating, naming, blessing, deciding.","kingdom":"Gen 1:26 pairs the words deliberately. The kingdom mandate is built on this dual identity. We are statue (tselem) AND functioning replica (demut). Not idols (passive). Not gods (autonomous). Vice-regents (deputies with delegated power).","cross":"Genesis 1:26; 5:1, 3; James 3:9","plain":"Made to resemble — patterned after God."},"kingdom":{"meaning":"The reign and rule of God. Not primarily a place but a presence — wherever God's will is done.","hebrew":"malkut (מַלְכוּת) — 'kingdom, reign.'","greek":"basileia (βασιλεία) — 'kingdom, reign.'","kingdom":"The central theme of Jesus' teaching. 'Thy kingdom come, thy will be done, on earth as it is in heaven' (Matt 6:10) — the prayer for kingdom-realization.","plain":"God's rule — wherever God is in charge and His will is done."},"king":{"hebrew":"מֶלֶךְ","translit":"melek","strongs":"H4428","root":"From malak (to reign, possess)","senses":["King — the supreme human ruler","Used of YHWH as Sovereign over creation","Used of conquered earthly kings as deputies (sometimes ironically)"],"visual":"A figure on a throne, crown on head, scepter in hand — focal point of all authority","ane":"ANE peoples saw their king as divine or semi-divine — Pharaoh was 'son of Ra,' Mesopotamian kings spoke for their gods. Tanakh refuses this: there is ONE King in heaven; earthly kings are His servants. When Israel demands a king 'like the nations,' YHWH says they have rejected HIM as King (1 Sam 8:7).","kingdom":"The ULTIMATE Kingdom title belongs to YHWH (Melek HaOlam = 'King of the World'). Earthly kingship is derivative and accountable. The Davidic line is preserved precisely because the Messiah-King comes through it.","theology":"Christ is named 'King of kings and Lord of lords' (Rev 19:16) — finally bringing all earthly authority under YHWH's reign through Himself.","cross":"1 Samuel 8:7; Psalm 24:7-10; Daniel 7:14; Revelation 19:16","plain":"A ruler with full authority over his people and land."},"kings":{"hebrew":"מְלָכִים","translit":"melakim","strongs":"H4428","def":"Plural of melek — kings.","plain":"More than one king."},"reign":{"hebrew":"מָלַךְ","translit":"malak","strongs":"H4427","root":"Active verb form of melek","senses":["To be king, to act as king","To exercise royal authority","To take the throne"],"visual":"Hands on the scepter — the active exercise of power","ane":"Royal proclamations: 'X has begun to reign' — formal coronation announcement throughout ANE.","kingdom":"YHWH 'reigns' — the perpetual present tense of His throne (Ps 93:1; 97:1). His reign was never interrupted by the Fall; humanity's exercise of delegated reign was.","cross":"1 Samuel 8:11; Psalm 93:1; Revelation 11:17","plain":"To rule as king — to actively hold and use power."},"throne":{"hebrew":"כִּסֵּא","translit":"kisse","strongs":"H3678","root":"Possibly from 'to cover, conceal'","senses":["A throne — seat of royal/judicial authority","Metaphor for the rule itself","Used of YHWH's heavenly throne"],"visual":"An elevated chair surrounded by attendants — center of power, source of decree","ane":"Coronation = the king actually SITTING on the throne for the first time. The throne was sacred space — to approach without invitation was death. Esther 4:11 reflects this even in late Persia.","kingdom":"YHWH's throne is in the heavens (Ps 11:4; Isa 66:1). Eden was its earthly extension. The Ark of the Covenant later represented His throne among His people. The future restoration brings God's throne back to earth (Rev 22:3).","theology":"Christ at the right hand of the Father (Heb 1:3) is enthroned co-regent — the human King now seated where Adam was meant to be.","cross":"Psalm 11:4; 89:14; Hebrews 1:8; Revelation 22:3","plain":"A king's royal seat — the center of his power."},"crown":{"hebrew":"עֲטָרָה","translit":"atarah","strongs":"H5850","def":"Crown, wreath. Symbol of royalty, victory, honor.","cross":"Psalm 8:5 (crowned him with glory and honor)","plain":"The headpiece a king wears — a sign of royalty and honor."},"rule":{"hebrew":"מָשַׁל","translit":"mashal","strongs":"H4910","root":"To rule, govern","senses":["To rule, have dominion","To govern with wisdom (subtly different from radah)","To compare, speak in parables (related root — wisdom that compares)"],"visual":"A wise judge weighing scales — governance through discernment","ane":"Where radah emphasizes the act of dominion (conquering), mashal emphasizes GOVERNANCE — administration, wisdom, the day-to-day work of rule.","kingdom":"Gen 1:18 — the lights of heaven 'rule' the day and night. Even cosmic objects have a governance role. Gen 4:7 — sin desires to 'rule' over Cain, but he must rule over it.","cross":"Genesis 1:16-18; 4:7; Proverbs 16:32; 22:7","plain":"To govern and make decisions for others."},"ruler":{"hebrew":"מוֹשֵׁל","translit":"moshel","strongs":"H4910","def":"One who rules, governor.","plain":"A person in charge — one who governs."},"prince":{"hebrew":"שַׂר","translit":"sar","strongs":"H8269","root":"To rule, be high","senses":["Prince, chief, ruler","Military captain","High official (under the king)"],"visual":"A noble at the king's right hand — second in command","ane":"Sarim (plural) were the high officials of ANE courts. They executed the king's commands and represented him to the people.","kingdom":"Gen 17:6 — 'kings shall come out of thee.' Same root as Sarah's name (Princess). Abraham's lineage was always meant to produce royalty.","cross":"Genesis 17:6; Isaiah 9:6 ('Prince of Peace'); Daniel 10:13","plain":"A high official just below the king — second in command."},"fill":{"hebrew":"מָלֵא","translit":"male","strongs":"H4390","root":"To be full, to fill","senses":["To fill up, complete","To consecrate (fill the hand — formal priestly term)","To fulfill (a promise, a time period)"],"visual":"A vessel filled to overflowing — completion, sufficiency","ane":"ANE kings boasted 'I filled the land with my glory.' Genesis subverts this royal claim: humanity collectively fills the earth WITH GOD'S GLORY by being His image. The land doesn't fill with our glory; it fills with His through us.","kingdom":"Third word of the Royal Mandate (Gen 1:28). 'Fill the earth' = extend the King's reign by inhabiting/representing every corner of His domain.","theology":"Christ commissions His disciples to FILL all nations with His name (Matt 28:19). Same mandate, new phase.","cross":"Genesis 1:28; Numbers 14:21; Isaiah 11:9; Matthew 28:19","plain":"To completely fill something up."},"multiply":{"hebrew":"רָבָה","translit":"rabah","strongs":"H7235","root":"To be/become great, increase","senses":["To increase in number","To increase in quality, greatness, magnitude","To exalt, to make great"],"visual":"A small flame becoming a wildfire — exponential expansion","ane":"Sumerian/Babylonian rulers boasted 'I made the people MANY.' Genesis flips it: humans are blessed to multiply BY GOD HIMSELF — not by royal decree from below, but by Royal Decree from above.","kingdom":"Second word of the Royal Mandate. The Abrahamic covenant carries this forward — 'I will multiply thee exceedingly' (Gen 17:2). Multiplication is the means of kingdom expansion.","cross":"Genesis 1:28; 17:2; 22:17","plain":"To grow rapidly in number — to greatly increase."},"fruitful":{"hebrew":"פָּרָה","translit":"parah","strongs":"H6509","root":"To bear, produce","senses":["To bear fruit, be productive","To prosper, expand","Source of Ephraim's name (Gen 41:52)"],"visual":"A laden fruit tree bowing under its own abundance","ane":"ANE creation stories had gods scared of human multiplication (Atrahasis sends a flood to control human noise). Genesis INVERTS this: God BLESSES humanity with multiplication — it is His plan, not His fear.","kingdom":"First word of the Royal Mandate (Gen 1:28). Be fruitful = expand the King's image across the earth. Reproduction is itself a kingdom act — making more bearers of the divine image.","cross":"Genesis 1:28; 9:1; 17:6; 35:11","plain":"Producing lots of offspring or results — full of life and increase."},"garden":{"hebrew":"גַּן","translit":"gan","strongs":"H1588","root":"Enclosed, protected place","senses":["Garden, walled enclosure","Royal park or pleasure-ground","Sacred space"],"visual":"Walls around lush green abundance — order amid wilderness","ane":"ANE royal gardens (Persian 'pairi-daeza' = paradise) were SACRED EXTENSIONS OF THE PALACE. The king walked there with his queen. They were both pleasure and political display. Eden = YHWH's royal garden, where He walks (Gen 3:8) with His image-bearers. The TEMPLE later replicates Eden — sacred space where God dwells.","kingdom":"Eden was the original throne-room of YHWH's earthly kingdom. Adam and Eve were the royal couple in residence. The whole story of redemption is about REGAINING THE GARDEN — and in Rev 22, the garden returns, this time spread across the whole new earth.","theology":"Christ rises from the dead in a garden (John 19:41; 20:15) — Mary mistakes Him for the gardener. Not by accident: the New Adam is restoring the garden vocation.","cross":"Genesis 2:8-15; 3:8, 23-24; Song of Songs 4:12; John 20:15; Revelation 22:1-5","plain":"An enclosed, cared-for place full of life and abundance."},"Eden":{"hebrew":"עֵדֶן","translit":"ʿEden","strongs":"H5731","root":"From a verb meaning 'to be soft, delicate, pleasant.' Cognate with Sumerian edin (plain, fertile land) and Akkadian edinu.","senses":["Delight, pleasure, luxury — the primary meaning of the word itself","Geographic place — 'eastward in Eden' (Gen 2:8); located near four rivers (Pishon, Gihon, Tigris, Euphrates)","Sacred / divine space — 'the garden of God' (Ezek 28:13)","The mountain of God — 'thou wast upon the holy mountain of God' (Ezek 28:14)","Source — 'a river went out of Eden to water the garden' (Gen 2:10)","God's manifest presence — 'they heard the voice of YHWH God walking in the garden' (Gen 3:8)","Royal estate / pleasure-garden of the King — LXX renders as παράδεισος (paradise), the Persian pairi-daeza ('walled-around')"],"visual":"A walled, fertile, abundant garden on a HIGH place — Ezekiel calls it 'the holy mountain of God.' Four rivers flowing OUT of it (it is the source, not just a recipient). Lush trees, including the central Tree of Life and Tree of the Knowledge of Good and Evil. Animals living peacefully. A walking-place where YHWH Himself appeared at 'the cool of the day' (Gen 3:8). Walled and guarded by cherubim after the Fall (Gen 3:24). NOT a primitive jungle — a cultivated, ordered, royal estate.","ane":"In ANE thought, gods had heavenly estates and earthly sanctuaries that mirrored them. Mesopotamian temples were built on raised platforms (ziggurats) to imitate the divine mountain. Egyptian temples had sacred gardens. Eden is the PROTOTYPE: the garden where heaven and earth meet, where God walks with humanity, and from which divine order flows out into the world.","kingdom":"Eden was the original THRONE ROOM. Adam was placed there as priest-king. The Hebrew verbs 'to dress and keep' (avad and shamar) are the same words later used of Levitical priests serving in the tabernacle (Num 3:7-8). The kingdom mandate (Gen 1:28 — be fruitful, multiply, fill, subdue, have dominion) was EDEN EXPANDING. Humanity was to carry God's manifest presence outward until ALL the earth became Eden. After the Fall this mission was disrupted, not cancelled. The Tabernacle, then the Temple, then Christ, then the Church are stages of Eden being re-established. Revelation 22 shows it complete: river of life, tree of life, no more curse, God's throne with humanity dwelling in His presence forever.","theology":"Eden is not merely a past geographic location — it encodes humanity's TRUE VOCATION: to live in God's manifest presence, to bear His image, to extend His rule. The word means 'delight' because life with God IS delight. Sin exiles us from delight; redemption is the journey BACK. Genesis opens in Eden; Revelation closes with Eden restored. The whole biblical story is exile-and-return.","greek":"LXX: παράδεισος (paradeisos) — 'paradise.' Same word Jesus speaks to the thief on the cross: 'today shalt thou be with me in paradise' (Luke 23:43). Same word Paul uses for being caught up to 'paradise' (2 Cor 12:4). Revelation 2:7: 'the tree of life, which is in the midst of the paradise of God.'","cross":"Genesis 2-3; Isaiah 51:3 (Zion as Eden restored); Ezekiel 28:13-14, 31:8-9, 36:35; Joel 2:3; Luke 23:43; 2 Corinthians 12:4; Revelation 2:7, 22:1-5","plain":"The lush garden-home God made for the first humans — a place of perfect abundance."},"tree":{"hebrew":"עֵץ","translit":"ets","strongs":"H6086","root":"Tree, wood — both the living plant and the cut material.","senses":["Tree (living)","Wood, timber, lumber","Stick, staff","Wooden object (cross, gallows)"],"visual":"A standing living tree — but also the wood it becomes when cut. The duality matters: the Tree of Life in Eden (living) and the tree on which Christ hung at Calvary (dead) are the same word in Hebrew (and the LXX uses ξύλον for both). The story comes full circle from Eden's tree to Calvary's tree.","ane":"ANE iconography: sacred trees in temple complexes represented divine wisdom and life. Asherah poles were sacred trees co-opted into Canaanite cult. Genesis takes the symbol and makes it about COMMUNION with YHWH.","kingdom":"Eden's two trees are the King's choice given to humanity: Life with Him, or autonomous knowledge of good and evil. Adam chose autonomy. Christ on the tree at Calvary undoes Adam's choice — His death on a tree opens the way back to the Tree of Life (Rev 22).","greek":"LXX: ξύλον (xylon) for both 'tree' and 'wood.' Same word used in NT for the cross (Acts 5:30, 1 Peter 2:24 — 'his own self bare our sins in his own body on the tree/xylon').","cross":"Genesis 2:9, 3:6; Deuteronomy 21:23 ('cursed is everyone that hangeth on a tree'); Galatians 3:13; 1 Peter 2:24; Revelation 22:2, 14","plain":"A tree. In Eden, two mattered: the Tree of Life and the tree of knowing good and evil."},"trees":{"hebrew":"עֵצִים","translit":"etsim","strongs":"H6086","def":"Plural of ets — trees.","plain":"More than one tree."},"knowledge":{"hebrew":"דַּעַת","translit":"daat","strongs":"H1847","root":"From yada — to know.","senses":["Knowing by experience (NOT just intellectual data)","Intimate knowledge — used of sexual union (Gen 4:1: 'Adam knew Eve his wife')","Skill, expertise (Ex 31:3)","Recognition — as opposed to ignorance","Discernment between options","Relational knowing — God 'knows' His people"],"visual":"Not data in your head. Knowledge is intimate, lived, experiential. To know YHWH is to walk with Him. To know good and evil is to have moral experience of them. The Tree of Knowledge wasn't an information-tree — it was an experience-tree.","ane":"Hebrew thought is RELATIONAL, not abstract. To 'know' something is to have experiential acquaintance. Greek thought (later) develops abstract conceptual knowing. Hebrew daat is body-and-relationship knowing.","kingdom":"Vice-regents need daat — practical wisdom to rule well. Solomon asked for daat. The world breaks down without leaders who know YHWH and know His ways.","greek":"LXX: γνῶσις (gnōsis). NT preserves both senses but often emphasizes the relational/experiential.","cross":"Genesis 2:9, 4:1; Exodus 31:3; Hosea 4:6 ('My people are destroyed for lack of knowledge'); Proverbs 1:7, 2:6, 9:10; Philippians 3:10 ('that I may know him')","plain":"Knowing something deeply through experience — not just facts in your head."},"good":{"hebrew":"טוֹב","translit":"tov","strongs":"H2896","root":"Primary adjective for goodness in Hebrew.","senses":["Good, fitting, pleasing — what works as designed","Beautiful (aesthetic)","Prosperous, successful","Morally good, righteous","Useful, beneficial","Joyful, pleasant"],"visual":"When a craftsman steps back and sees his work as it should be — that's tov. When a meal tastes right, when a song lands, when a relationship is whole — tov. Not just 'morally good' (that's English narrowing) but 'good in every dimension at once.'","ane":"ANE craftsmen used similar terms for 'a job well done.' Genesis 1's seven 'tov' verdicts on creation declare God a satisfied Master Craftsman.","kingdom":"The King's verdict 'tov' is creation's title deed. The world IS good — not as a moral assessment but as the King's stamp. Sin damaged it but didn't unmake it. The creation was tov, and will be again.","greek":"LXX: καλός (kalos) — good, beautiful. Same dual aesthetic-moral sense.","cross":"Genesis 1:4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25, 31; Mark 10:18 ('there is none good but one, that is, God')","plain":"The way things are meant to be — right, beautiful, and working as designed."},"evil":{"hebrew":"רַע","translit":"ra","strongs":"H7451","def":"Evil, wrong, harmful, bad. The opposite of tov.","plain":"What is wrong, harmful, and broken — the opposite of good."},"Spirit":{"hebrew":"רוּחַ","translit":"ruach","strongs":"H7307","root":"From a verb meaning 'to blow, breathe.' All three meanings (wind/breath/spirit) flow from the same root act of moving air.","senses":["Wind — physical moving air (Gen 8:1 — 'God made a wind to pass over the earth')","Breath — the breath that moves in lungs (Job 12:10)","Spirit / animating life-force — what makes a body alive","Disposition / mood / state of mind — 'a hard spirit' (Deut 2:30); 'a spirit of jealousy' (Num 5:14)","The Spirit of God — YHWH's active power (Gen 1:2 — 'the Spirit of God moved upon the face of the waters')","Prophetic inspiration — the Spirit comes upon prophets, kings, craftsmen","Mind / intellect — 'whose ruach (mind/spirit) hath given Him counsel?' (Isa 40:13)"],"visual":"Invisible but powerful — like the wind. You don't see ruach itself; you see what it MOVES. Hovering over the deep at creation. Filling Bezalel for craftsmanship (Ex 31:3). Coming upon David at his anointing. Like a breath you can't grasp but that brings life.","ane":"In ANE worldview, ruach is what distinguishes living from dead, animate from inanimate. NOT a separate immaterial part of you — the breathing-life that fills you and CAN BE GIVEN OR TAKEN by God. When God 'gathers His ruach' (Job 34:14), creatures die. When He sends His ruach forth, things live (Ps 104:30).","warning":"RULE 07 — Greek philosophy splits body/soul/spirit into three separable layers. Hebrew unifies them. The Spirit, the breath, the wind — same word, same reality flowing in different scales. Don't impose Western three-part anthropology onto a Hebrew word that resists it.","greek":"LXX: πνεῦμα (pneuma). Same threefold range. Jesus exploits this in John 3:8 — 'the wind (pneuma) bloweth where it listeth… so is everyone that is born of the Spirit (pneuma).'","kingdom":"The Spirit of God is what equips humans to BE vice-regents. Kings of Israel were anointed by Spirit; prophets, judges, craftsmen all served by Spirit. Without ruach, dominion is impossible.","cross":"Genesis 1:2, 6:3, 8:1; Exodus 31:3; Numbers 11:25; Judges 14:6; 1 Samuel 10:6; Job 33:4; Psalm 104:30; Isaiah 11:2; Joel 2:28; John 3:8","plain":"God's invisible, powerful presence — like wind you can't see but can feel working."},"spirit":{"meaning":"Wind, breath, life-force, the immaterial part of a person. Also: 'the Spirit' = Holy Spirit. Context decides.","hebrew":"ruach (רוּחַ) — 'wind, breath, spirit.'","greek":"pneuma (πνεῦμα) — 'wind, breath, spirit.' Where 'pneumatic' and 'pneumonia' come from.","kingdom":"Genesis 1:2 — the Spirit of God hovered over the waters. John 3:8 — 'the wind bloweth where it listeth... so is every one that is born of the Spirit.'","plain":"Breath, life, or the inner part of a person. Capital 'S' means God's Holy Spirit."},"breath":{"hebrew":"נְשָׁמָה","translit":"neshamah","strongs":"H5397","root":"From nasham — to pant, blow.","senses":["Breath of life — specifically God's breath, distinct from mere air","Inspiration, divine spark — what God breathes uniquely into humanity (Gen 2:7)","The candle of YHWH — 'the spirit (neshamah) of man is the candle of YHWH' (Prov 20:27) — neshamah as God's lamp inside us","Breath of all the living — used poetically for life itself (Isa 42:5)"],"visual":"More intimate than ruach (wind). Neshamah is FACE-TO-FACE breath — like CPR. God breathes into Adam's nostrils (Gen 2:7) — the only being to receive this direct divine breath.","ane":"Other ANE creation stories have humans formed but NOT god-breathed. Hebrew Genesis adds this UNIQUE detail: only humans receive God's neshamah. Animals have ruach but not neshamah. This is what makes humans IMAGE-BEARING — the divine breath is the source of our representational capacity.","kingdom":"Neshamah is what qualifies humans to rule. It is divine investment — the King's own breath in His vice-regents.","greek":"LXX: πνοή (pnoē). NT picks this up in Acts 17:25 — God 'giveth to all life and breath (pnoē).'","cross":"Genesis 2:7, 7:22; Job 32:8, 33:4; Proverbs 20:27; Isaiah 42:5; Acts 17:25","plain":"Close, life-giving breath. God breathed life into the first man."},"soul":{"meaning":"The whole person, especially the inner life. Hebrew nephesh means 'breathing one' — the living self.","hebrew":"nephesh (נֶפֶשׁ) — 'soul, life, person.' Not separate from body — IS the living being.","greek":"psyche (ψυχή) — 'soul, life.' Where 'psychology' comes from.","kingdom":"Mark 8:36 — 'what shall it profit a man, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul?' The soul outranks the world.","plain":"The whole living person — especially your inner self."},"man":{"hebrew":"אִישׁ","translit":"ish","strongs":"H376","root":"Possibly from a verb root meaning 'to exist' — 'the existing/standing one.' Different from adam (humankind) — ish is a specific individual male person.","senses":["Adult male, individual man","Husband (in marital context)","A particular person (sometimes generic 'someone')","One who is somebody — has standing, identity, presence","Counterpart to ishah — the relational pair"],"visual":"ISH does NOT appear in Genesis 1, NOR at Genesis 2:7 when Adam is first formed. He is just ADAM (humankind, taken from adamah/ground). Ish only appears at Genesis 2:23 — when Adam sees the woman and recognizes her. THE MOMENT she exists, HE becomes ish — because he now has an ishah. Manhood is awakened by the presence of woman.","wordplay":"Hebrew preserves a wordplay that English destroys. Ish (man) and ishah (woman) are THE SAME WORD with a feminine ending added. Adam's first poem (Gen 2:23) declares: 'she shall be called ishah because she was taken out of ish.' This is a deliberate Hebrew construction announcing fundamental ESSENCE-SAMENESS with relational differentiation. NOT two separate species — one essence, two expressions.","ane":"Other ANE languages have separate, unrelated words for 'man' and 'woman.' Hebrew uniquely names them as variants of a single word — encoding into the very LANGUAGE the truth that man and woman are one humanity. This is theology built into vocabulary.","theology":"Adam's first recorded speech (Gen 2:23) is poetry. He doesn't merely identify her — he CELEBRATES her. 'This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh.' The Hebrew is ecstatic. The first words spoken by a human being are a love-song.","kingdom":"The ish is the household head, the covenant representative, the one who stands before YHWH on behalf of his ishah and children. This is not entitlement — it is responsibility. He answers for the household. When Adam fails, GOD calls for him first, not Eve (Gen 3:9: 'And YHWH God called unto the man, and said unto him, Where art thou?').","greek":"LXX: ἀνήρ (anēr) — adult male / husband. ἄνθρωπος (anthropos) — human being / mankind generally.","cross":"Genesis 2:23-24, 3:6, 3:16, 4:1; Ephesians 5:25-33","plain":"A human being. Early in Genesis it means humankind in general."},"men":{"hebrew":"אֲנָשִׁים","translit":"anashim","strongs":"H582","def":"Men, people, mortals. Plural of enosh.","plain":"More than one man; people."},"Adam":{"hebrew":"אָדָם","translit":"adam","strongs":"H120","root":"From adamah (אֲדָמָה) — ground, soil, especially red/cultivated earth. Wordplay: humans formed from humus, the red earth. The name itself means 'earth-creature' or 'red one' (some Hebrew suggests skin-color connection via the red earth).","senses":["Humankind, humanity (collective term, includes male and female together)","Human being (singular, sometimes used generically)","Adam (proper name of the first man, especially from Gen 4 onward)","Earth-creature — emphasizing material origin from soil"],"visual":"Adam is the SPECIES name first, the individual name second. In Genesis 1:26-27, 'adam' is used for HUMANITY — both male and female together: 'Let us make adam in our image… male and female created He THEM.' Only in Genesis 2 does adam become specifically the first individual man. He IS humankind, condensed into one body.","ane":"ANE creation accounts often portrayed humans as made from clay or earth — but typically as servants of the gods, made to do their work. Genesis radically subverts this: humans are made from earth NOT to serve the gods' labor, but to RULE as the King's image-bearing vice-regents. Same dust, totally different vocation.","theology":"Adam = earth-creature. From dust we came, to dust we return (Gen 3:19). BUT — God breathed neshamah into this dust, and dust became image-bearer. Humanity is dust-and-divine-breath unified. Christ as the second Adam (1 Cor 15:45) reverses the first Adam's failure — same flesh, same dust, but obedient where the first failed.","skin_note":"The Hebrew root behind adam (and adamah, 'red earth') has been connected by some to the Hebrew adom (אָדֹם) meaning 'red.' The first humans, formed from Levantine/Mesopotamian soil, would have had brown skin in the Semitic phenotype range. NOT pale/European — that's anachronistic projection. Sourced: Genesis 2:7 (formed from adamah); standard ANE archaeology of regional populations.","kingdom":"Adam was the first vice-regent. The kingdom mandate was given to him AND her. The image of God was placed in adam — male and female together. We are still adam — earth-creatures with divine breath, bearing His image, called to rule.","greek":"LXX: Ἀδάμ (Adam) as proper name; ἄνθρωπος (anthropos) as 'humankind / human being.'","cross":"Genesis 1:26-27, 2:7, 2:15-25, 3:9-19, 5:1-5; Romans 5:12-21; 1 Corinthians 15:22, 45-49","plain":"The first human — his name also means 'humankind.' Made from the ground."},"Eve":{"hebrew":"חַוָּה","translit":"chavvah","strongs":"H2332","root":"From chavah (חוה) — 'to live, declare life, give life.' Connected to chayah (to live) and chayyim (life).","senses":["Life, life-giver, mother of life","The mother of all living (Gen 3:20 — em kol-chai)","Counterpart to Adam (the earth-creature paired with the life-bringer)"],"visual":"Adam (earth-creature) is paired with Chavvah (life-bringer). His name is from soil; her name is from breath. Together they encode the human reality: matter and life, body and soul, dust and breath, EARTH and LIFE.","ane":"Goddesses of life, fertility, and motherhood populated every ANE pantheon (Asherah, Astarte, Ishtar, Hathor). They were worshipped. Hebrew refuses this entirely — Eve is not a goddess. She is a HUMAN, the first mother, honored without being deified. Genesis demythologizes the divine-feminine while elevating the human-feminine to image-bearer status.","theology":"Adam names her chavvah AFTER the curse — even with judgment pronounced, even with death entering the world, Adam's prophetic act is to name her LIFE. He sees that through her seed, life will overcome death. Genesis 3:20 is one of the most hopeful verses in the Bible — naming hope into a fallen world. The seed of the woman (Gen 3:15) is the line that leads to Christ.","kingdom":"Chavvah is co-regent. The kingdom continues through HER WOMB. Without her, the line dies. Every promise — to Abraham, to Judah, to David — depends on a woman bearing seed. The Messiah comes 'born of woman' (Gal 4:4). Mary echoes Eve as the second mother of life — bringing forth the Life Himself.","greek":"LXX: Ζωή (Zoe) — Life. Same word Jesus uses: 'I am the way, the truth, and the LIFE (zoe)' (John 14:6). And: 'in him was life (zoe)' (John 1:4).","cross":"Genesis 2:21-25, 3:1-20, 4:1-2; 1 Timothy 2:13-15; 2 Corinthians 11:3","plain":"The first woman — her name means 'life,' the mother of all living."},"ground":{"hebrew":"אֲדָמָה","translit":"adamah","strongs":"H127","root":"Connected to adom (red)","senses":["Tilled soil, arable ground","Earth as the source of humanity","The land (specifically Israel's land in later texts)"],"visual":"Reddish-brown earth held in a hand — the substance from which Adam was formed","ane":"ANE peoples saw soil as sacred — the source of fertility, the meeting place of gods and humans. Genesis takes this further: humanity ITSELF is made from adamah. Adam IS earth + divine breath. We are not visitors to creation; we are part of it.","kingdom":"Gen 2:7 — 'YHWH God formed man (adam) of the dust of the ground (adamah).' Wordplay locks human identity to the earth. Gen 3:17 — the ground is cursed because of Adam's sin. Gen 3:19 — 'to dust shalt thou return.' Adam came from adamah; adamah receives him back.","theology":"Christ as the 'second Adam' (1 Cor 15:45) breaks the cycle: He emerges from the tomb (returned to earth) as the firstfruit of a new humanity that won't return to dust forever.","cross":"Genesis 2:7; 3:17-19; 4:10; 1 Corinthians 15:45-49","plain":"The soil/earth — what the first human was formed from."},"rest":{"meaning":"Ceasing from labor. Both physical and spiritual. God rested on day seven.","hebrew":"shabbat, nuach (נוּחַ).","greek":"anapauo (ἀναπαύω).","kingdom":"Matthew 11:28 — 'Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.' Hebrews 4 — there remains a Sabbath rest for the people of God.","plain":"Stopping work. God rested on the seventh day."},"Sabbath":{"hebrew":"שַׁבָּת","translit":"shabbat","strongs":"H7676","root":"From shabath (to cease, desist)","senses":["Seventh day of cessation","A weekly Sabbath","Sabbatical year (every 7th year)","Jubilee (every 50th year)"],"visual":"An open hand laid down beside completed work — rest as satisfaction, not exhaustion","ane":"ANE creation accounts: gods rest because they made humans to do their work for them (Enuma Elish, Atrahasis). Genesis: YHWH rests because His work is COMPLETE, and He INVITES humanity into His rest as fellow image-bearers, not slaves.","kingdom":"Sabbath is the kingdom's WEEKLY DECLARATION OF SOVEREIGNTY. By stopping work, the worker confesses: 'I am not the king. The world keeps turning by Another's hand.' Sabbath-keeping is REBELLION against work-as-identity and the slave-economies that demand it.","theology":"Christ is the Lord of the Sabbath (Mark 2:28). Hebrews 4 describes the believer's life as entering God's seventh-day rest. The Sabbath was always pointing forward to fellowship with the King.","cross":"Genesis 2:2-3; Exodus 20:8-11; Mark 2:27-28; Hebrews 4:9-10","plain":"The day of rest — laying down your work and enjoying what's finished."},"covenant":{"meaning":"Binding agreement, often sealed in blood. Sacred contract. The Bible is one big covenant document.","hebrew":"berith (בְּרִית) — 'covenant.' Possibly from a root meaning 'to cut.'","greek":"diatheke (διαθήκη) — 'covenant, testament.' Why we call the parts of the Bible 'Old Testament' and 'New Testament.'","kingdom":"Jeremiah 31:31-34 — the promise of a New Covenant. Hebrews 8 quotes this in full and applies it to Christ.","plain":"A sacred, binding agreement — a promise sealed between two parties."},"blessing":{"hebrew":"בְּרָכָה","translit":"berakah","strongs":"H1293","def":"Blessing, gift, treaty of peace.","cross":"Genesis 12:2-3","plain":"Good given by God — favor, life, and increase."},"blessed":{"hebrew":"בָּרוּךְ","translit":"baruch","strongs":"H1288","root":"From barach — to kneel. The connection: blessing-receivers knelt before kings.","senses":["Blessed, favored, empowered","Endowed with God's covenant goodness","Source of further blessing — 'thou shalt be a blessing' (Gen 12:2)","Made fruitful, productive","Set apart for divine purpose"],"visual":"A kneeling vassal receiving a king's hand on his head — anointed, charged, sent. A field that yields harvest. A womb that bears children. A name that becomes a blessing in itself.","ane":"ANE kings 'blessed' subjects by formal proclamation, often laying hands. Patriarchs blessed sons by laying hands and pronouncing destiny (Gen 27, 48). The blessing was not a wish — it was a transfer of authority and power.","kingdom":"Blessing is how the King distributes His kingdom. Adam was blessed (Gen 1:28) and given dominion. Abraham was blessed and made a father of nations. We are blessed in Christ (Eph 1:3) — already given everything pertaining to life and godliness.","greek":"LXX: εὐλογημένος (eulogēmenos) — 'well-spoken-of.' Beatitudes use μακάριος (makarios) — 'happy, blessed.'","cross":"Genesis 1:28, 12:2-3, 14:19, 24:1, 27:27-29, 49; Psalm 1:1; Matthew 5:3-12; Ephesians 1:3","plain":"Given God's favor — set up to flourish."},"bless":{"meaning":"To speak good over. To invoke favor. From God: to give favor. From us: to praise.","hebrew":"barakh (בָּרַךְ) — 'to kneel, to bless.' Same root as berakhah (blessing).","greek":"eulogeo (εὐλογέω) — 'to speak well of, to bless.'","kingdom":"Genesis 12:2-3 — Abraham to be 'blessed' and 'a blessing.' Numbers 6:24-26 — the priestly blessing.","plain":"To speak good over someone and give them favor."},"cursed":{"hebrew":"אָרוּר","translit":"arur","strongs":"H779","def":"Bound under judgment. Opposite of blessed.","plain":"Placed under judgment — the opposite of blessed."},"curse":{"meaning":"Opposite of bless. To call down evil. In the OT, often: the consequences of breaking covenant.","hebrew":"arar (אָרַר), qalal (קָלַל).","greek":"katara (κατάρα).","kingdom":"Galatians 3:13 — 'Christ hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us.' Jesus took the curse so we could receive the blessing.","plain":"To call down harm — the consequences of breaking a covenant."},"promise":{"meaning":"God's spoken commitment. The whole storyline of scripture is promise → fulfillment.","greek":"epaggelia (ἐπαγγελία) — 'announcement, promise.'","kingdom":"Galatians 3:16 — the promises were made to Abraham 'and to his seed... which is Christ.' Every promise of God points to and is fulfilled in Jesus (2 Cor 1:20).","plain":"Something God commits to do. Scripture is a story of promises kept."},"name":{"hebrew":"שֵׁם","translit":"shem","strongs":"H8034","root":"Possibly from a root meaning 'mark, sign.' A name marks/identifies the bearer.","senses":["Name — identifier","Character — what one is known for","Reputation — how one is regarded","Authority — what one's name accomplishes","Memory — being 'remembered' through one's name","Renown, fame"],"visual":"Not a label. A name in Hebrew thought is a person's REALITY made visible. Calling someone's name = invoking their character. Giving a new name = transforming identity. Erasing a name = un-creating the person.","ane":"ANE peoples knew/used divine names for power. Egyptian magic invoked names of gods. YHWH gave His covenant name (Ex 3:14) as a GIFT, not a magic formula. To 'call upon the name of YHWH' (Gen 4:26) = to relate to Him as covenant partner.","kingdom":"Kings ruled BY THEIR NAME — proclamations went out 'in the name of the king.' YHWH's people pray, prophesy, and exercise authority 'in the name of YHWH.' To bear God's name (image-bearing humanity) is to carry His authority and accountability.","greek":"LXX: ὄνομα (onoma). NT 'name of Jesus' carries the full weight of Hebrew shem.","cross":"Genesis 4:26, 11:4, 12:2, 17:5, 32:28; Exodus 3:13-15, 20:7; Proverbs 22:1; Acts 4:12; Philippians 2:9-11","plain":"In the Bible, a name stands for a person's whole character and reality."},"seed":{"hebrew":"זֶרַע","translit":"zera","strongs":"H2233","root":"From zara (to sow)","senses":["Seed (agricultural)","Offspring, descendants (collective and individual)","Family line"],"visual":"A single seed held between thumb and finger — the future folded into the present","ane":"ANE royal lines were obsessed with 'seed' — preserving the king's lineage was the kingdom's continuity. Tanakh inherits this: the Davidic seed, the Abrahamic seed.","kingdom":"Gen 3:15 — 'the seed of the woman' will crush the serpent's head. This is the PROTOEVANGELIUM — the first gospel. The royal seed is identified from the moment after the Fall. Genesis 22:18 — 'in thy seed (singular!) shall all nations of the earth be blessed.' Paul reads this Messianically (Gal 3:16): 'He saith not seeds, as of many; but as of one, And to thy seed, which is Christ.'","theology":"The whole biblical narrative tracks 'the seed' — Seth, Noah, Shem, Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Judah, David... arriving at Christ as the Singular Seed who fulfills the promise.","cross":"Genesis 3:15; 12:7; 13:15; 22:18; Galatians 3:16","plain":"Offspring and descendants — the future carried in a single seed."},"Noah":{"hebrew":"נֹחַ","translit":"noach","strongs":"H5146","def":"Rest, comfort. Lamech named him expecting relief from cursed ground (Gen 5:29).","plain":"The man God rescued through the flood. His name means 'rest/comfort.'"},"Noe":{"hebrew":"נֹחַ","translit":"noach","strongs":"H5146","def":"Greek/Latin form of Noah.","plain":"Another spelling of Noah."},"ark":{"meaning":"TWO different boxes: (1) Noah's ark — the ship. (2) Ark of the Covenant — the chest containing the tablets, manna, Aaron's rod.","hebrew":"tevah (תֵּבָה) for Noah's; aron (אָרוֹן) for the Ark of Covenant.","kingdom":"Both are types of Christ — Noah's ark saves through the waters of judgment; the Ark of the Covenant has the mercy seat covering the law.","plain":"Either Noah's giant boat, or the sacred chest holding God's covenant items."},"Abraham":{"hebrew":"אַבְרָהָם","translit":"avraham","strongs":"H85","def":"Father of a multitude. Renamed from Abram (Gen 17:5) when YHWH adds the 'h' — the breath of YHWH's name into his.","ane":"Name change in ANE = identity transformation. New name from a king = new mission and authority.","plain":"Father of God's covenant people. His name means 'father of many.'"},"Abram":{"hebrew":"אַבְרָם","translit":"avram","strongs":"H87","def":"Exalted father (singular). His original name.","plain":"Abraham's original name, before God renamed him."},"Sarah":{"hebrew":"שָׂרָה","translit":"sarah","strongs":"H8283","def":"Princess. Renamed from Sarai. From sar (prince, ruler) — she is royal.","plain":"Abraham's wife and mother of the promised son. Her name means 'princess.'"},"Sarai":{"hebrew":"שָׂרַי","translit":"sarai","strongs":"H8297","def":"My princess. Original name.","plain":"Sarah's original name."},"Isaac":{"hebrew":"יִצְחָק","translit":"yitzchaq","strongs":"H3327","def":"He laughs. Born of Sarah's old-age laughter and YHWH's promise.","plain":"Abraham and Sarah's promised son. His name means 'he laughs.'"},"Ishmael":{"hebrew":"יִשְׁמָעֵאל","translit":"yishmael","strongs":"H3458","def":"God hears. Named because YHWH heard Hagar's affliction.","plain":"Abraham's first son, by Hagar. His name means 'God hears.'"},"Jacob":{"hebrew":"יַעֲקֹב","translit":"yaakov","strongs":"H3290","def":"Heel-grabber, supplanter. Renamed Israel after wrestling YHWH (Gen 32:28).","plain":"Isaac's son, later renamed Israel — father of the twelve tribes."},"Israel":{"meaning":"Two meanings: (1) Jacob's God-given name ('struggles with God'), (2) the nation of his twelve descendants.","hebrew":"Yisra'el (יִשְׂרָאֵל).","kingdom":"Romans 9-11 — Paul's most extended argument that God has not finished with Israel. Galatians 6:16 — 'the Israel of God.'","plain":"Jacob's God-given name ('struggles with God') — and the nation of his descendants."},"Esau":{"hebrew":"עֵשָׂו","translit":"esav","strongs":"H6215","def":"Hairy. Jacob's older twin.","plain":"Jacob's older twin brother."},"Edom":{"hebrew":"אֱדוֹם","translit":"edom","strongs":"H123","def":"Red. Esau's nickname — for the red lentil stew he sold his birthright for.","plain":"Esau's other name (means 'red') — and the nation descended from him."},"Joseph":{"hebrew":"יוֹסֵף","translit":"yosef","strongs":"H3130","def":"May He add. Rachel asked YHWH to add another son after his birth.","plain":"Jacob's favored son, sold into Egypt, who rose to save his family."},"Judah":{"hebrew":"יְהוּדָה","translit":"yehudah","strongs":"H3063","def":"Praise. Leah's fourth son. The royal/Messianic line.","cross":"Genesis 49:10 (scepter from Judah)","plain":"Jacob's son whose line leads to the kings and the Messiah. Means 'praise.'"},"Reuben":{"hebrew":"רְאוּבֵן","translit":"reuven","strongs":"H7205","def":"See, a son! Jacob's firstborn.","plain":"Jacob's firstborn son."},"Simeon":{"hebrew":"שִׁמְעוֹן","translit":"shimon","strongs":"H8095","def":"Hearing. Jacob's second son.","plain":"Jacob's second son."},"Levi":{"hebrew":"לֵוִי","translit":"levi","strongs":"H3878","def":"Joined. Jacob's third son. Becomes the priestly tribe.","plain":"Jacob's third son — ancestor of the priestly tribe."},"Benjamin":{"hebrew":"בִּנְיָמִין","translit":"binyamin","strongs":"H1144","def":"Son of the right hand. Rachel called him Ben-Oni ('son of my sorrow') as she died.","plain":"Jacob's youngest son. His name means 'son of the right hand.'"},"Dinah":{"hebrew":"דִּינָה","translit":"dinah","strongs":"H1783","def":"Judgment.","plain":"Jacob's daughter."},"Tamar":{"hebrew":"תָּמָר","translit":"tamar","strongs":"H8559","def":"Palm tree. Daughter-in-law of Judah; in the Messianic line.","plain":"Judah's daughter-in-law, in the family line of the Messiah."},"Hagar":{"hebrew":"הָגָר","translit":"hagar","strongs":"H1904","def":"Egyptian handmaid of Sarai, mother of Ishmael.","plain":"Sarah's servant and the mother of Ishmael."},"Rebekah":{"hebrew":"רִבְקָה","translit":"rivqah","strongs":"H7259","def":"Isaac's wife, mother of Jacob and Esau.","plain":"Isaac's wife, mother of Jacob and Esau."},"Rachel":{"hebrew":"רָחֵל","translit":"rachel","strongs":"H7354","def":"Ewe. Jacob's beloved wife.","plain":"Jacob's beloved wife, mother of Joseph and Benjamin."},"Leah":{"hebrew":"לֵאָה","translit":"leah","strongs":"H3812","def":"Jacob's first wife. Mother of 6 of the 12 tribes.","plain":"Jacob's first wife, mother of six of the twelve tribes."},"Cain":{"hebrew":"קַיִן","translit":"qayin","strongs":"H7014","def":"From qanah (to acquire). Eve said 'I have acquired (qaniti) a man with the help of YHWH.'","plain":"Adam and Eve's firstborn — who killed his brother Abel."},"Abel":{"hebrew":"הֶבֶל","translit":"hevel","strongs":"H1893","def":"Vapor, breath, vanity. Same word repeated 38× in Ecclesiastes ('vanity of vanities').","plain":"Adam and Eve's second son, killed by Cain. His name means 'breath/vapor.'"},"Enoch":{"hebrew":"חֲנוֹךְ","translit":"chanoch","strongs":"H2585","def":"Dedicated, initiated. Walked with God; God took him.","cross":"1 Enoch (in our library); Hebrews 11:5; Jude 14","plain":"A man who walked so closely with God that God took him without dying."},"Methuselah":{"hebrew":"מְתוּשֶׁלַח","translit":"metushelach","strongs":"H4968","def":"Possibly 'when he dies, it shall come' — he died the year of the Flood.","plain":"The longest-living man in the Bible; he died the year of the flood."},"Lamech":{"hebrew":"לֶמֶךְ","translit":"lemech","strongs":"H3929","def":"Father of Noah.","plain":"Noah's father."},"Seth":{"hebrew":"שֵׁת","translit":"shet","strongs":"H8352","def":"Appointed. Eve named him: 'God has APPOINTED me another seed instead of Abel.'","plain":"Adam and Eve's third son, given after Abel's death. Means 'appointed.'"},"Shem":{"hebrew":"שֵׁם","translit":"shem","strongs":"H8035","def":"Name. Noah's son; ancestor of the Semitic peoples (including Israel).","plain":"Noah's son — ancestor of the Hebrew people."},"Ham":{"hebrew":"חָם","translit":"cham","strongs":"H2526","def":"Hot. Noah's son.","plain":"Noah's son."},"Japheth":{"hebrew":"יֶפֶת","translit":"yefet","strongs":"H3315","def":"Enlargement. Noah's son.","plain":"Noah's son."},"flood":{"hebrew":"מַבּוּל","translit":"mabbul","strongs":"H3999","def":"Flood, deluge. Used only of the Genesis flood — a unique world-undoing event.","plain":"The world-covering flood in Noah's day."},"rainbow":{"hebrew":"קֶשֶׁת","translit":"qeshet","strongs":"H7198","def":"Bow (also a war bow). Set in clouds as covenant sign — God's bow hung up.","ane":"In ANE warrior imagery, hanging up the bow = ending warfare. YHWH disarms toward humanity in covenant.","plain":"The bow in the clouds — God's sign He'd never flood the whole earth again."},"Nephilim":{"hebrew":"נְפִילִים","translit":"nephilim","strongs":"H5303","def":"Fallen ones, giants. Offspring of 'sons of God' and 'daughters of men' in Gen 6:1-4. LXX: γίγαντες.","ane":"Direct parallel to 1 Enoch chs 6-11.","cross":"1 Enoch 6-11; Numbers 13:33","plain":"The 'fallen ones' — giant offspring in Genesis 6, before the flood."},"giants":{"hebrew":"נְפִילִים","translit":"nephilim","strongs":"H5303","def":"Same as Nephilim. The KJV translation.","plain":"Same as Nephilim — the giant figures of Genesis 6."},"Babel":{"hebrew":"בָּבֶל","translit":"bavel","strongs":"H894","root":"From balal (to mix, confuse)","senses":["Babel — the place where languages were confused","Babylon — same Hebrew word","Symbol of human autonomy and rebellion"],"visual":"A massive tower under construction, its top piercing the clouds — and the workers suddenly confused, scattering","ane":"Babylonian ziggurats were TEMPLE-TOWERS — built to bring the gods down or human worshippers up. Each ziggurat was named to capture this: 'House of the Foundation of Heaven and Earth' (Etemenanki, in Babylon itself). The Genesis Tower of Babel narrative is a POLEMIC against this Mesopotamian theology of ascent.","kingdom":"Gen 11:4 — 'Let us make us a name' (shem). The Tower of Babel is humanity's counter-kingdom attempt: united but without YHWH, ascending but without invitation. YHWH 'comes down' (11:5) — the King condescends — and SCATTERS them. The kingdom won't be built that way.","theology":"Pentecost (Acts 2) reverses Babel — the Spirit gives tongues, uniting people across languages by YHWH's initiative, not human ascent.","cross":"Genesis 10:10; 11:1-9; Isaiah 13-14; Revelation 17-18; Acts 2:1-13","plain":"The city and tower where God confused human language and scattered people."},"Egypt":{"meaning":"Land of slavery in Exodus narrative. Becomes a biblical symbol of bondage — the world, the flesh.","hebrew":"Mitzrayim (מִצְרַיִם).","kingdom":"Hosea 11:1 — 'I called my son out of Egypt.' Quoted of Jesus in Matt 2:15. The Exodus pattern recurs throughout Bible.","plain":"The powerful nation that later enslaved Israel — a symbol of bondage."},"Pharaoh":{"hebrew":"פַּרְעֹה","translit":"paroh","strongs":"H6547","root":"Egyptian per-aa ('great house')","senses":["Title of Egyptian kings","Used as a generic name for any Egyptian king","Represents earthly power claiming divine status"],"visual":"A crowned figure on a throne, hand raised in command — power claiming godhood","ane":"In Egyptian theology, Pharaoh WAS the image of the god Ra/Horus. He was the bridge between gods and humanity. Genesis confronts this directly: Pharaoh deals with the patriarchs as equals or as supplicants — never as a god. Gen 41 — Joseph interprets Pharaoh's dream because YHWH does what Pharaoh's gods cannot.","kingdom":"Pharaoh = the FALSE counterpart to YHWH's kingdom. He claims divine authority Adam was supposed to bear under YHWH. The Exodus narrative (which Genesis sets up) is the showdown: which King has the true authority over Israel?","theology":"Every false 'pharaoh' (Babylon, Rome, etc.) in biblical theology echoes this original counterfeit kingship.","cross":"Genesis 12:15-20; 41:1-44; Exodus 5:2; Revelation 17:18","plain":"The title for Egypt's king, who claimed to be a god."},"Canaan":{"hebrew":"כְּנַעַן","translit":"kenaan","strongs":"H3667","def":"The Promised Land. Means 'humbled' or 'lowland.'","plain":"The land God promised to Abraham's descendants."},"Ararat":{"hebrew":"אֲרָרָט","translit":"ararat","strongs":"H780","def":"Mountain region where the ark rested.","plain":"The mountains where Noah's ark came to rest."},"Sodom":{"hebrew":"סְדֹם","translit":"sedom","strongs":"H5467","def":"Sodom. City destroyed for wickedness.","plain":"A city destroyed for its wickedness."},"Gomorrah":{"hebrew":"עֲמֹרָה","translit":"amorah","strongs":"H6017","def":"Gomorrah. Destroyed alongside Sodom.","plain":"The city destroyed alongside Sodom."},"Bethel":{"hebrew":"בֵּית־אֵל","translit":"beth-el","strongs":"H1008","def":"House of God. Where Jacob saw the ladder.","plain":"A place meaning 'house of God,' where Jacob saw the stairway to heaven."},"Salem":{"hebrew":"שָׁלֵם","translit":"shalem","strongs":"H8004","def":"Peace, completeness. Ancient name of Jerusalem.","ane":"Melchizedek = 'king of righteousness' from Salem. Jerusalem was the original royal-priestly city.","plain":"The ancient name for Jerusalem. It means 'peace.'"},"Moriah":{"hebrew":"מֹרִיָּה","translit":"moriyyah","strongs":"H4179","def":"Mountain where Abraham bound Isaac. Later: Solomon's temple.","cross":"2 Chronicles 3:1","plain":"The mountain where Abraham was tested with Isaac; later the temple site."},"Lot":{"hebrew":"לוֹט","translit":"lot","strongs":"H3876","def":"Veil, covering. Abraham's nephew.","plain":"Abraham's nephew, rescued from Sodom."},"Melchizedek":{"hebrew":"מַלְכִּי־צֶדֶק","translit":"malki-tsedeq","strongs":"H4442","root":"Combination: melek (king) + tsedeq (righteousness) = 'My King is righteousness'","senses":["Personal name","Title: 'King of Righteousness'","Office: King-priest of Salem"],"visual":"A figure who appears once, gives bread and wine, blesses Abram, and vanishes from Genesis — but echoes through Scripture","ane":"ANE rarely combined king and priest in one figure — those offices were usually separated. Melchizedek's joint office is striking. Salem (later Jerusalem) was already a place of YHWH worship before Abraham — Melchizedek serves 'God Most High' (El Elyon).","kingdom":"Gen 14:18-20 — Melchizedek is the FIRST KING-PRIEST. He blesses Abraham; Abraham gives him a tithe. This is the prototype of the royal-priestly office that Israel was meant to be (Ex 19:6: 'kingdom of priests') and that Christ ultimately fulfills.","theology":"Hebrews 7 dedicates a whole chapter to Melchizedek as a type of Christ — 'a priest forever after the order of Melchizedek' (Heb 7:17, quoting Ps 110:4). Christ is BOTH King AND Priest, succeeding Melchizedek's order.","cross":"Genesis 14:18-20; Psalm 110:4; Hebrews 5:6, 10; 7:1-28","plain":"A mysterious king-priest who blessed Abraham — a picture of Christ."},"Hebron":{"hebrew":"חֶבְרוֹן","translit":"chevron","strongs":"H2275","def":"Association, league. City where Abraham buried Sarah.","plain":"The city where Abraham buried Sarah."},"Beersheba":{"hebrew":"בְּאֵר שֶׁבַע","translit":"beer-sheva","strongs":"H884","def":"Well of seven / well of the oath.","plain":"A place name meaning 'well of the oath.'"},"altar":{"meaning":"Place of sacrifice. Where heaven and earth meet through blood.","hebrew":"mizbeach (מִזְבֵּחַ) — from zabach, 'to slaughter for sacrifice.'","greek":"thusiasterion (θυσιαστήριον).","kingdom":"Genesis 12:7 — Abraham built an altar. Hebrews 13:10 — 'We have an altar' — the cross.","plain":"A raised place where sacrifices are offered to God."},"sacrifice":{"meaning":"Offering given to God, often the death of an animal as substitute for the sinner.","hebrew":"zebach (זֶבַח) — 'slaughter, sacrifice.' korban (קָרְבָּן) — 'offering, drawing-near.'","greek":"thusia (θυσία).","kingdom":"Hebrews 10:12 — 'this man, after he had offered one sacrifice for sins for ever, sat down on the right hand of God.' Christ ends the system.","plain":"An offering to God — often an animal's life given in place of the sinner's."},"offering":{"meaning":"Gift presented to God. Bread, animal, money, prayer, or self.","hebrew":"minchah (מִנְחָה) — grain offering; olah — burnt offering; shelem — peace offering.","kingdom":"Romans 12:1 — 'present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable unto God, which is your reasonable service.' We are now the offering.","plain":"A gift presented to God."},"circumcision":{"hebrew":"מוּל","translit":"mul","strongs":"H4135","root":"To cut around, cut off","senses":["To cut around (the foreskin)","The sign of the Abrahamic covenant","Metaphor: circumcision of the heart (Deut 30:6; Rom 2:29)"],"visual":"A blade and a body — cutting that marks identity into the flesh","ane":"Other ANE cultures practiced circumcision (Egyptians, some Canaanites) — usually as a FERTILITY OR INITIATION RITE, often at puberty. Genesis makes it COVENANTAL — performed on infants (Gen 17:12), marking household membership in YHWH's covenant from day 8 of life.","kingdom":"Gen 17 — the body itself bears the king's seal. Every male in Abraham's house is marked as belonging to YHWH's kingdom. The mark is irreversible, intimate, perpetual.","theology":"Romans 4:9-12 — Abraham's circumcision came AFTER his justification by faith (Gen 15:6 precedes Gen 17). The covenant sign confirms but doesn't create the covenant relationship.","cross":"Genesis 17:9-14; Romans 2:28-29; 4:9-12; Galatians 5:6; Colossians 2:11","plain":"The cutting that marked Abraham's family as God's covenant people."},"circumcised":{"hebrew":"מוּל","translit":"mul","strongs":"H4135","def":"Same as 'circumcision' — past tense.","plain":"Having received that covenant mark."},"heaven":{"meaning":"The dwelling of God. The realm of the spiritual. Also: the sky.","hebrew":"shamayim (שָׁמַיִם) — plural form, 'the heights, the sky.'","greek":"ouranos (οὐρανός).","kingdom":"Paul mentions 'the third heaven' (2 Cor 12:2). Revelation 21 — heaven and earth united in the New Creation.","plain":"Where God dwells — and also the sky above."},"heavens":{"hebrew":"שָׁמַיִם","translit":"shamayim","strongs":"H8064","def":"Same as 'heaven' — Hebrew is dual.","plain":"The sky and the spiritual realm above."},"earth":{"hebrew":"אֶרֶץ","translit":"eretz","strongs":"H776","def":"Land, ground, world.","plain":"The land and world we live on."},"land":{"hebrew":"אֶרֶץ","translit":"eretz","strongs":"H776","def":"Same as 'earth.' In covenant context: the Promised Land.","plain":"The ground or territory — often the Promised Land in covenant settings."},"deep":{"hebrew":"תְהוֹם","translit":"tehom","strongs":"H8415","def":"Primal sea, abyss. Cognate with Babylonian Tiamat — but in Genesis just water under God's command.","ane":"In Babylonian Enuma Elish, Tiamat is a chaos-monster god killed by Marduk. Genesis demythologizes.","plain":"The deep waters / ocean. In Genesis, just water under God's control."},"darkness":{"hebrew":"חֹשֶׁךְ","translit":"choshech","strongs":"H2822","def":"Darkness — physical and spiritual absence of light.","cross":"1 John 1:5","plain":"The absence of light — physical or spiritual."},"light":{"hebrew":"אוֹר","translit":"or","strongs":"H216","def":"Brightness, illumination. Metaphor for truth, life, knowledge of God.","cross":"John 8:12","plain":"Brightness. Also a picture of truth and life."},"water":{"hebrew":"מַיִם","translit":"mayim","strongs":"H4325","def":"Water. Always grammatically plural in Hebrew.","plain":"Water — the basic source of life. Hebrew always writes it as plural (mayim) because water is never truly just one thing."},"waters":{"hebrew":"מַיִם","translit":"mayim","strongs":"H4325","def":"Same as 'water.'","plain":"Water (Hebrew says it as plural)."},"sea":{"hebrew":"יָם","translit":"yam","strongs":"H3220","def":"Sea. In ANE myth, often a chaos-god. Genesis demythologizes.","ane":"Yam is the chaos-god in Ugaritic myth. Genesis makes him a created object.","plain":"The sea. Other nations feared it as a chaos-god; Genesis says God made it."},"seas":{"hebrew":"יָמִּים","translit":"yamim","strongs":"H3220","def":"Plural of yam — seas.","plain":"More than one sea."},"firmament":{"hebrew":"רָקִיעַ","translit":"raqia","strongs":"H7549","def":"Expanse, hammered-out space. Imagined as a beaten-out vault.","ane":"ANE cosmology: the sky was a solid dome holding back heavenly waters.","plain":"The expanse of sky — pictured as a vast dome overhead."},"day":{"hebrew":"יוֹם","translit":"yom","strongs":"H3117","def":"Day. Used in Genesis 1 for creation days.","plain":"A day. In Genesis 1 it marks each stage of creation."},"days":{"hebrew":"יָמִים","translit":"yamim","strongs":"H3117","def":"Plural of yom.","plain":"More than one day."},"night":{"hebrew":"לַיְלָה","translit":"laylah","strongs":"H3915","def":"Night.","plain":"Night — the dark part of the day."},"year":{"hebrew":"שָׁנָה","translit":"shanah","strongs":"H8141","def":"Year. From verb 'to repeat.'","plain":"A year — one full cycle of seasons. The Hebrew word comes from 'to repeat,' because every year is creation cycling again."},"years":{"hebrew":"שָׁנִים","translit":"shanim","strongs":"H8141","def":"Plural of shanah.","plain":"More than one year."},"righteous":{"hebrew":"צַדִּיק","translit":"tsaddiq","strongs":"H6662","def":"Just, righteous. Right standing with God. Used of Noah (6:9).","plain":"Living rightly and faithfully before God. Used of Noah."},"righteousness":{"meaning":"Right standing — being in proper relationship with God and others. Not 'moral perfection' but covenant faithfulness.","hebrew":"tzedakah (צְדָקָה) — also means 'justice, almsgiving.' In Jewish thought, righteousness IS justice IS care for the poor.","greek":"dikaiosune (δικαιοσύνη) — 'right standing, justification.' Where 'dike' (justice) comes from.","kingdom":"Romans 1:17 — 'The just (dikaios) shall live by faith.' We don't earn righteousness; we receive it as gift through faith in Christ.","plain":"Being in right relationship with God and others — faithfulness, not flawless perfection."},"faith":{"meaning":"Trust. Conviction. Not blind belief — confident reliance based on reason to trust.","hebrew":"emunah (אֱמוּנָה) — 'faithfulness, steadiness.' Same root as 'Amen.'","greek":"pistis (πίστις) — 'faith, trust, faithfulness.'","kingdom":"Hebrews 11:1 — 'Now faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.' The hall of faith follows.","plain":"Trust — confident reliance on God, with good reason to trust Him."},"believed":{"hebrew":"אָמַן","translit":"aman","strongs":"H539","def":"To be firm, faithful, trust. Foundation of biblical faith.","cross":"Romans 4:3; Galatians 3:6","plain":"Trusted and relied on — the root of biblical faith."},"wisdom":{"meaning":"Skill at living. Not just knowledge — knowing HOW to apply truth in real life.","hebrew":"chokmah (חָכְמָה) — 'wisdom, skill.' Originally used of craftsmen.","greek":"sophia (σοφία).","kingdom":"Proverbs 9:10 — 'The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom.' James 1:5 — 'If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God.'","plain":"Skill at living well — knowing how to apply truth to real life."},"fear":{"meaning":"TWO meanings: (1) terror (the bad kind), (2) reverent awe of God (the right kind). Context decides.","hebrew":"yir'ah (יִרְאָה) — can be either, often means awe.","kingdom":"Proverbs 9:10 — 'The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom.' Not terror — reverence. Yet 'perfect love casteth out fear' (1 John 4:18) — the bad kind.","plain":"Either being scared, or deep awe and respect (the right kind, toward God)."},"love":{"meaning":"Self-giving, willed commitment to another's good. Not feeling — choice.","hebrew":"ahavah (אַהֲבָה); hesed — covenant lovingkindness.","greek":"agape (ἀγάπη) — self-sacrificing love; philia — friendship love; eros — romantic; storge — family.","kingdom":"1 John 4:8 — 'God is love.' Not 'God is loving' — God IS love. John 3:16. Romans 8:39. The defining attribute of God.","plain":"Choosing to commit yourself to another's good — more than a feeling."},"kindness":{"hebrew":"חֶסֶד","translit":"hesed","strongs":"H2617","root":"Possibly from a root meaning 'to bend, bow' — to incline toward in mercy.","senses":["Loyal love — covenant kindness","Steadfast mercy","Faithful commitment","Lovingkindness","Grace — undeserved favor","Devotion — going beyond strict obligation"],"visual":"A spouse who keeps faith when the other has failed. A friend who shows up when the chips are down. A king who pardons when justice could condemn. Not sentiment — covenant LOYALTY embodied as action.","ane":"Hesed has NO English equivalent. Some translate 'lovingkindness,' others 'covenant love,' others just leave it untranslated. ANE treaty texts use comparable terms for the loyalty owed between covenant partners. YHWH's hesed is the bedrock of every covenant He makes.","kingdom":"The King's hesed is what makes covenant possible at all. Without hesed, covenants would collapse the first time a vassal failed. With hesed, the relationship endures. Israel's whole hope through 1500 years of failure was YHWH's hesed enduring forever (Ps 136 — 'his hesed endureth forever' repeated 26 times).","greek":"LXX: ἔλεος (eleos) — mercy. NT picks this up. χάρις (charis) — grace — also captures part of hesed's range.","cross":"Genesis 24:12, 39:21; Exodus 34:6-7; Psalm 23:6, 51:1, 136 (whole psalm); Hosea 6:6; Micah 6:8 ('love hesed'); Lamentations 3:22-23","plain":"Loyal love that keeps showing up even when it's undeserved."},"mercy":{"meaning":"Withholding the punishment someone deserves. Compassion in action. Grace gives good you didn't earn; mercy withholds bad you did.","hebrew":"racham (רָחַם) — root word means 'womb.' Mercy is womb-love. Also rachamim (compassions).","greek":"eleos (ἔλεος) — pity, compassion. Where 'eleemosynary' (charitable) comes from.","kingdom":"Matthew 5:7 — 'Blessed are the merciful: for they shall obtain mercy.' Mercy received → mercy given. The cycle of the Kingdom.","plain":"Holding back the punishment someone deserves; compassion in action."},"wife":{"hebrew":"אִשָּׁה","translit":"ishah","strongs":"H802","see":"woman","note":"Same Hebrew word as 'woman.' Hebrew uses ONE word for both. Marriage doesn't change WHAT she is — it changes WHOSE she is in covenant.","plain":"A married woman."},"woman":{"hebrew":"אִשָּׁה","translit":"ishah","strongs":"H802","root":"Feminine form of ish. Hebrew adds the feminine ending '-ah' to ish — declaring her essential SAMENESS with him while distinguishing her by the body and role she bears.","senses":["Woman (adult female)","Wife (Hebrew uses ONE WORD for woman and wife — there is no separate term)","Female counterpart of ish","Adam's gift from God (Gen 2:22)"],"visual":"Adam SAW her and named her ishah — recognizing instantly that she came from him (the rib/side, Gen 2:21-22) and was for him. She is ish-with-a-feminine-ending. Same essence. Different role. Different body. Equal dignity.","ane":"In most ANE cultures, women had legal status only through male relatives — fathers, husbands, sons. Genesis is unique in placing the woman alongside the man in the IMAGE OF GOD itself (1:27). Her dignity is not derivative. The Hebrew text does NOT teach female inferiority — it teaches functional differentiation within shared image-bearing dignity.","wordplay":"The deliberate ish-ishah pairing is theological architecture. It says: man without woman is incomplete (2:18 — 'it is not good that the man should be alone'). Woman without man is the literal source of his completion. The pair is the unit. The single is the deficit.","marriage":"Marriage in Hebrew is established by Gen 2:24: 'Therefore shall a man (ish) leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave (davaq) unto his wife (ishah): and they shall be one flesh (basar echad).' This is the founding covenant. Three movements: LEAVE prior household — CLEAVE to spouse — BECOME one flesh. The order matters.","kingdom":"Ishah is co-regent with ish. The rule of Genesis 1:28 was given to BOTH. She is not a sub-ruler — she is a co-ruler. Her body brings forth life; his body initiates it; together they image God's own creative relational community.","greek":"LXX: γυνή (gynē) — woman / wife (same dual meaning as Hebrew).","cross":"Genesis 2:22-24, 3:1-13, 3:15, 3:16, 3:20; Ephesians 5:22-33; 1 Peter 3:1-7","plain":"A female human. Adam called her ishah, taken from man (ish)."},"women":{"hebrew":"נָשִׁים","translit":"nashim","strongs":"H802","def":"Plural of ishah.","plain":"More than one woman."},"husband":{"hebrew":"אִישׁ","translit":"ish","strongs":"H376","see":"man","note":"Same Hebrew word as 'man.' Marriage doesn't change WHAT he is — it changes WHOSE he is in covenant. Also: baal (בַּעַל, H1167) is sometimes translated 'husband' but means 'master/owner.' Hebrew uses both words but ish is the marriage-covenant term, baal the property/headship term.","plain":"A married man."},"father":{"hebrew":"אָב","translit":"av","strongs":"H1","def":"Father.","plain":"A father — the head of the family, the one who passes on the name, blessing, and inheritance to the next generation."},"fathers":{"hebrew":"אָבוֹת","translit":"avot","strongs":"H1","def":"Plural — fathers, ancestors.","plain":"Fathers or ancestors."},"mother":{"hebrew":"אֵם","translit":"em","strongs":"H517","def":"Mother.","plain":"A mother — the one who gives life and nurtures it. In the Bible, mothers often name the children and shape their destinies."},"son":{"hebrew":"בֵּן","translit":"ben","strongs":"H1121","def":"Son. Continuation of family line, inheritance, name.","plain":"A son — who carries on the family name and inheritance."},"sons":{"hebrew":"בָּנִים","translit":"banim","strongs":"H1121","def":"Plural — sons. Also general 'children.'","plain":"Sons, or children in general."},"daughter":{"hebrew":"בַּת","translit":"bat","strongs":"H1323","def":"Daughter.","plain":"A daughter."},"daughters":{"hebrew":"בָּנוֹת","translit":"banot","strongs":"H1323","def":"Plural — daughters.","plain":"More than one daughter."},"brother":{"hebrew":"אָח","translit":"ach","strongs":"H251","def":"Brother. 'Am I my brother's keeper?' (Gen 4:9) echoes through Scripture.","plain":"A brother."},"brethren":{"hebrew":"אַחִים","translit":"achim","strongs":"H251","def":"Plural — brothers.","plain":"Brothers (the older word for the plural)."},"sister":{"hebrew":"אָחוֹת","translit":"achot","strongs":"H269","def":"Sister.","plain":"A sister — and sometimes used for any close female relative or companion."},"people":{"hebrew":"עַם","translit":"am","strongs":"H5971","def":"People, nation, kindred. The 'covenant people' = am Yisrael.","plain":"A group bound together — especially God's covenant people."},"nation":{"hebrew":"גּוֹי","translit":"goy","strongs":"H1471","def":"Nation, people-group. Plural goyim = 'the nations / Gentiles.'","plain":"A people-group. The plural often means the non-Jewish 'nations.'"},"nations":{"hebrew":"גּוֹיִם","translit":"goyim","strongs":"H1471","def":"Plural — nations.","plain":"Peoples and countries — often everyone outside Israel."},"tribe":{"hebrew":"שֵׁבֶט","translit":"shevet","strongs":"H7626","def":"Tribe, scepter, rod. Same word for 'scepter' (Gen 49:10) — the tribe and its ruling staff.","plain":"An extended family group. The same Hebrew word also means a ruler's staff."},"scepter":{"hebrew":"שֵׁבֶט","translit":"shevet","strongs":"H7626","root":"Possibly from 'to branch off'","senses":["A ruler's staff — symbol of authority","A tribe (the people under that staff)","A rod used for discipline or measuring"],"visual":"A wooden staff held by a king — power crystallized in a single object","ane":"In ANE coronation, the king received a shevet from the deity, signifying that all rule on earth flows from heaven. To 'wield the scepter' = to govern with delegated divine authority.","kingdom":"Genesis 49:10 declares the SCEPTER will not depart from Judah — the royal authority stays with that tribe until 'Shiloh comes.' This is the kingly Messianic prophecy: an unbroken royal line until the ultimate King arrives.","cross":"Genesis 49:10; Numbers 24:17; Psalm 45:6; Hebrews 1:8","plain":"A ruler's staff — a symbol of his authority."},"family":{"hebrew":"מִשְׁפָּחָה","translit":"mishpachah","strongs":"H4940","def":"Family, clan, kindred.","plain":"A family or clan."},"families":{"hebrew":"מִשְׁפָּחוֹת","translit":"mishpachot","strongs":"H4940","def":"Plural — families. Used in Gen 12:3 ('all families of the earth').","plain":"More than one family — as in the promise to bless 'all families of the earth.'"},"flesh":{"meaning":"Body — sometimes neutral (human nature). But in NT often: the sinful tendency in fallen humanity.","hebrew":"basar (בָּשָׂר) — 'flesh, body.'","greek":"sarx (σάρξ) — 'flesh.' Romans 7-8 contrasts flesh and spirit.","kingdom":"Galatians 5:17 — 'the flesh lusteth against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh.' Believers live by the Spirit.","plain":"The body or human nature. In the New Testament it can mean our sinful tendencies."},"bone":{"hebrew":"עֶצֶם","translit":"etsem","strongs":"H6106","def":"Bone, body, substance. 'Bone of my bones' (Gen 2:23) = covenant kinship language.","plain":"Bone. 'Bone of my bones' means deep, family-close kinship."},"bones":{"hebrew":"עֲצָמוֹת","translit":"atsamot","strongs":"H6106","def":"Plural — bones.","plain":"More than one bone."},"blood":{"hebrew":"דָּם","translit":"dam","strongs":"H1818","root":"Possibly connected to adom (red)","senses":["Blood — the substance","Life itself ('the life is in the blood,' Lev 17:11)","Guilt of bloodshed ('your blood be on your own head')"],"visual":"Red as the universal sign of life or death — the medium of both","ane":"Bloodshed in ANE created RITUAL POLLUTION that had to be addressed. Blood was sacred — life itself. Covenants were sealed with blood. Treaties were 'cut.' The ANE saw blood as carrying soul-substance.","kingdom":"Gen 4:10 — Abel's blood 'crieth' from the ground. Gen 9:6 — bloodshed of an image-bearer demands accountability because the image of God is in the human. Blood = the kingdom's most serious matter, because it carries LIFE.","theology":"All sacrificial blood points to Christ (Heb 9:22). His blood 'speaks better things than that of Abel' (Heb 12:24) — not crying for vengeance, but for mercy.","cross":"Genesis 4:10; 9:4-6; Leviticus 17:11; Hebrews 9:22; 12:24","plain":"Blood — the Bible's sign of life, and of life given in death."},"death":{"hebrew":"מָוֶת","translit":"mavet","strongs":"H4194","root":"From mut (to die)","senses":["Death — cessation of life","The realm of the dead (Sheol's neighbor)","Final separation"],"visual":"A body still — the absence of breath, the silence of the once-spoken","ane":"ANE peoples viewed death as the GREATEST ENEMY. The Mesopotamian gods feared it; only their immortal status protected them. Genesis takes a strikingly different view: death is the consequence of rebellion (Gen 2:17), and YHWH alone is its master.","warning":"Hebrew thought has NO concept of an immortal soul that survives death naturally. To be human = to be MORTAL FLESH. Death is real — REAL — not a transition. This is why resurrection (not soul-flight) is the Hebrew hope.","kingdom":"Death entered through Adam's rebellion (Rom 5:12 reflects Hebrew thought). The kingdom's enemy is death itself (1 Cor 15:26). Christ's resurrection defeats death FROM WITHIN — He dies and emerges through the other side as the firstfruits of a new humanity.","cross":"Genesis 2:17; 3:19; Romans 5:12, 17; 1 Corinthians 15:26, 54-57","plain":"Death — the end of breath and life."},"die":{"hebrew":"מוּת","translit":"mut","strongs":"H4191","def":"To die.","plain":"To die — to stop breathing, to return to the ground. Death entered creation through sin; resurrection is God's answer to it."},"live":{"hebrew":"חָיָה","translit":"chayah","strongs":"H2421","def":"To live. Source of chayyim and Chavvah.","plain":"To live — to be alive and breathing. The same Hebrew root gives us 'Eve' (Chavvah) and 'life' (chayyim)."},"life":{"hebrew":"חַיִּים","translit":"chayyim","strongs":"H2416","root":"From chayah (to live)","senses":["Life","Living things (plural form, always)","Vitality, animation, fullness"],"visual":"Pulsing — the rhythm of breath and blood and motion","ane":"Hebrew is plural: chayyim = 'lives.' Hebrew anthropology views life as ABUNDANT, multifaceted — not minimal existence but flourishing in all dimensions.","kingdom":"The Tree of LIFE (Gen 2:9) — the kingdom's signature gift. Life isn't earned; it's RECEIVED in fellowship with the King. The river of life (Rev 22:1) flows from God's throne — life as overflow of the King's presence.","theology":"Christ says 'I am the way, the TRUTH, and the LIFE' (John 14:6). The Greek 'zoe' translates chayyim — the same fullness Hebrew Scripture promised.","cross":"Genesis 2:9; 3:22; Deuteronomy 30:19; John 10:10; 14:6; Revelation 22:1-2","plain":"Life — breath, heartbeat, and motion."},"living":{"hebrew":"חַי","translit":"chai","strongs":"H2416","def":"Living, alive.","plain":"Alive and breathing. 'The living God' (El Chai) means He is active and present — not a dead idol carved from stone."},"serpent":{"hebrew":"נָחָשׁ","translit":"nachash","strongs":"H5175","def":"Serpent. Same root as 'shining one' or 'enchanter.' Identified with Satan in Rev 12:9.","ane":"In ANE iconography, serpents represented chaos and primordial enemies of order.","plain":"A snake. In Genesis, the creature the deceiver used; later linked to Satan."},"fish":{"hebrew":"דָּג","translit":"dag","strongs":"H1709","def":"Fish.","plain":"Fish — creatures of the sea, created on Day Five. In ancient cultures the sea was feared; God simply makes fish and calls them good."},"bird":{"hebrew":"עוֹף","translit":"of","strongs":"H5775","def":"Bird, flying creature.","plain":"A bird or flying creature."},"birds":{"hebrew":"עוֹף","translit":"of","strongs":"H5775","def":"Same as 'bird' — collective.","plain":"Birds (as a group)."},"cattle":{"hebrew":"בְּהֵמָה","translit":"behemah","strongs":"H929","def":"Beast, livestock.","plain":"Livestock — farm animals."},"lion":{"hebrew":"אַרְיֵה","translit":"aryeh","strongs":"H738","def":"Lion. Royal symbol. Judah called 'lion's whelp' in Gen 49:9.","plain":"A lion — a royal symbol, tied to the tribe of Judah."},"voice":{"hebrew":"קוֹל","translit":"qol","strongs":"H6963","root":"Sound, voice","senses":["Voice (of God, humans, animals, instruments)","Sound (thunder, water, music)","A command or word spoken"],"visual":"Sound waves emanating outward — the audible presence of the speaker","ane":"ANE peoples expected the gods to speak through omens, signs, ecstatic prophecy. Hebrew uniquely emphasizes the CLEAR VERBAL VOICE — YHWH speaks intelligibly, in covenant language, to His people.","kingdom":"Gen 3:8 — Adam and Eve heard 'the voice of YHWH God walking in the garden.' Even in their hiding, the King's voice penetrates. Gen 22:11 — the angel's voice from heaven stops Abraham's sacrifice. The kingdom is governed by HIS VOICE, not by impersonal force.","theology":"John 10:27 — Christ's sheep hear His voice. The kingdom remains a voice-governed reality.","cross":"Genesis 3:8, 10; 22:11; 1 Kings 19:12 (the still small voice); John 10:27","plain":"A voice or sound — the speaker made audible."},"word":{"hebrew":"דָּבָר","translit":"davar","strongs":"H1697","root":"From dabar (to speak, arrange)","senses":["Word, speech","Matter, affair (a thing spoken about)","Event (a thing that happens)"],"visual":"Spoken sound that becomes physical reality — speech that does work","ane":"ANE gods spoke and reality changed — but the line between magic and creation was blurry. Hebrew uniquely insists: YHWH'S WORD IS PERFORMATIVE. He says, and it is. There is no incantation, no ritual mediation — His word IS the act.","kingdom":"Gen 1 is structured around 'And God said... and it was so.' The kingdom is built by the King's word. Royal proclamation enacts what it announces. The same word-power is given to humans in proportion: humans speak names, commands, promises — small reflections of divine speech-power.","theology":"John 1:1 — 'In the beginning was the Word.' The eternal Davar/Logos through whom all things came into being. The kingdom's foundational reality is Word made flesh.","cross":"Genesis 1:3 (etc.); Psalm 33:6, 9; Isaiah 55:11; John 1:1-3, 14","plain":"A spoken word. In the Bible, God's word actually makes things happen."},"remember":{"meaning":"Active recollection — sometimes by God (which leads to action) or by us (which leads to obedience).","hebrew":"zakar (זָכַר).","kingdom":"Genesis 8:1 — 'God remembered Noah.' Doesn't mean He had forgotten — means He acted. Luke 22:19 — 'this do in remembrance of me.'","plain":"To actively call to mind — which leads God to act and us to obey."},"remembered":{"hebrew":"זָכַר","translit":"zachar","strongs":"H2142","def":"Same as 'remember' — past tense.","plain":"Called to mind and acted on (past tense)."},"see":{"meaning":"Perceive. In Hebrew often means understand, not just visual sight.","hebrew":"ra'ah (רָאָה).","kingdom":"Genesis 22:14 — 'YHWH yireh' — 'the LORD will see/provide.' Hagar named God 'El-roi' — 'the God who sees me' (Gen 16:13).","plain":"To perceive — in Hebrew often 'to understand,' not just to look."},"saw":{"hebrew":"רָאָה","translit":"raah","strongs":"H7200","def":"Past tense of 'see.'","plain":"Perceived or understood (past tense)."},"walk":{"meaning":"In biblical usage, 'walk' = live. How you walk = how you live.","hebrew":"halak (הָלַךְ).","greek":"peripateo (περιπατέω) — 'walk around, conduct one's life.'","kingdom":"Genesis 5:24 — 'Enoch walked with God.' Galatians 5:16 — 'walk in the Spirit.' 1 John 1:7 — 'walk in the light.'","plain":"In the Bible, how you 'walk' means how you live."},"walked":{"hebrew":"הָלַךְ","translit":"halach","strongs":"H1980","def":"Same as 'walk' — past tense.","plain":"Lived (past tense) — as in 'walked with God.'"},"way":{"hebrew":"דֶּרֶךְ","translit":"derek","strongs":"H1869","def":"Way, path, journey, manner.","plain":"A road, a path, or a way of living."},"house":{"hebrew":"בַּיִת","translit":"bayit","strongs":"H1004","def":"House, household, dynasty. The 'house of Abraham' = his lineage and authority.","plain":"A house — and also a household or family line."},"field":{"hebrew":"שָׂדֶה","translit":"sadeh","strongs":"H7704","def":"Field, open country.","plain":"Open country or farmland."},"stone":{"hebrew":"אֶבֶן","translit":"even","strongs":"H68","def":"Stone. Used of memorials, altars, foundations.","plain":"A stone — used for altars, memorials, and foundations."},"servant":{"meaning":"One who serves. In the ANE, often a slave — but in Bible language, often a position of HONOR (servant of the LORD).","hebrew":"ebed (עֶבֶד) — 'servant, slave.'","greek":"doulos (δοῦλος) — 'slave, bondservant.' diakonos — 'servant.'","kingdom":"Jesus calls himself the Servant of all (Mark 10:45). Isaiah 53 is 'the Suffering Servant.' Paul opens many letters: 'Paul, a servant (doulos) of Jesus Christ.'","plain":"One who serves. Often a title of honor — 'servant of the LORD.'"},"servants":{"hebrew":"עֲבָדִים","translit":"avadim","strongs":"H5650","def":"Plural of eved.","plain":"More than one servant."},"charge":{"hebrew":"מִשְׁמֶרֶת","translit":"mishmeret","strongs":"H4931","def":"Charge, watch, duty, responsibility. Mandate given to image-bearers.","plain":"A duty or responsibility you're given to keep."},"keep":{"hebrew":"שָׁמַר","translit":"shamar","strongs":"H8104","def":"To keep, watch, guard. Adam was to 'keep' (shamar) the garden — same word later used for guarding the temple.","ane":"Eden = original temple. Adam = original priest-king.","plain":"To guard, watch over, and protect."},"till":{"hebrew":"עָבַד","translit":"avad","strongs":"H5647","def":"To work, serve, cultivate, worship. Same word for 'work' the garden AND 'serve' YHWH.","ane":"Working the garden = worship. Adam's labor was liturgical.","plain":"To work and tend the ground (the same word also means to serve God)."},"tilled":{"hebrew":"עָבַד","translit":"avad","strongs":"H5647","def":"Past tense of 'till.'","plain":"Worked the ground (past tense)."},"work":{"hebrew":"מְלָאכָה","translit":"melachah","strongs":"H4399","def":"Work, occupation, business. Used of God's creative work (Gen 2:2-3).","plain":"Labor or occupation — including God's work of creating."},"works":{"hebrew":"מַעֲשֶׂה","translit":"maaseh","strongs":"H4639","def":"Work, deed, labor. Plural form.","plain":"Deeds or actions."},"great":{"hebrew":"גָּדוֹל","translit":"gadol","strongs":"H1419","def":"Great, large, mighty.","plain":"Big or mighty."},"mighty":{"hebrew":"גִּבּוֹר","translit":"gibbor","strongs":"H1368","def":"Mighty, valiant, hero. Used of Nimrod (Gen 10:8) as 'a mighty hunter before the LORD.'","plain":"Powerful, heroic, strong."},"strong":{"hebrew":"חָזָק","translit":"chazaq","strongs":"H2389","def":"Strong, mighty, firm.","plain":"Strong and firm."},"judgment":{"meaning":"God's verdict — declaring what is right and what is wrong. Justice rendered.","hebrew":"mishpat (מִשְׁפָּט) — 'judgment, justice.'","greek":"krisis (κρίσις) — 'judgment, decision.' Where 'crisis' comes from.","kingdom":"Genesis 18:25 — Abraham: 'Shall not the Judge of all the earth do right?' God's judgments are always righteous.","plain":"God's verdict — declaring what's right and wrong, and setting it right."},"justice":{"hebrew":"צֶדֶק","translit":"tsedek","strongs":"H6664","def":"Righteousness, justice. Foundation of YHWH's throne.","plain":"Doing what is right and fair — a foundation of God's rule."},"peace":{"meaning":"Wholeness, completeness, well-being. Not just 'absence of conflict' but presence of everything good.","hebrew":"shalom (שָׁלוֹם) — 'wholeness, completeness, well-being.' Still the Hebrew greeting.","greek":"eirene (εἰρήνη) — 'peace.'","kingdom":"Isaiah 9:6 — Christ is the Prince of Peace (Sar Shalom). John 14:27 — Jesus' parting gift: 'Peace I leave with you, my peace I give unto you.'","plain":"Wholeness and well-being — everything as it should be, not just 'no fighting.'"},"anger":{"hebrew":"אַף","translit":"af","strongs":"H639","def":"Anger, nostril, face.","plain":"Anger — literally 'nostrils' in Hebrew, because rage shows in flared nostrils and hot breath. God's anger is His settled response to evil, not a tantrum."},"wrath":{"hebrew":"חֵמָה","translit":"chemah","strongs":"H2534","def":"Heat, fury, wrath. God's covenant anger against unfaithfulness.","plain":"Burning anger — God's settled response to evil."},"sin":{"meaning":"Missing the mark. Falling short of God's standard. Not just bad actions — broken relationship.","hebrew":"chatat (חַטָּאת) — 'missing the mark.' Like an arrow that doesn't reach the target. Also pesha (rebellion) and avon (twisted).","greek":"hamartia (ἁμαρτία) — same image: missing the target.","kingdom":"Romans 3:23 — 'all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God.' Sin is the universal human condition. Romans 6:23 — 'the wages of sin is death.'","plain":"Missing the mark — falling short of God's standard and breaking relationship with Him."},"iniquity":{"hebrew":"עָוֹן","translit":"avon","strongs":"H5771","def":"Iniquity, guilt, perversity. Twisted/distorted action.","plain":"Twisted, crooked wrongdoing — and the guilt that comes with it."},"transgression":{"hebrew":"פֶּשַׁע","translit":"pesha","strongs":"H6588","def":"Rebellion, transgression, willful violation.","plain":"Crossing the line on purpose — open rebellion."},"evil_one":{"hebrew":"הַשָּׂטָן","translit":"ha-satan","strongs":"H7854","def":"The accuser, adversary. Not a name in Genesis but the conceptual figure behind the serpent.","plain":"The accuser and enemy — the figure behind the serpent."},"Spirit of God":{"hebrew":"רוּחַ אֱלֹהִים","translit":"ruach Elohim","strongs":"H7307+H430","def":"The Spirit of God hovering over the waters (Gen 1:2). Same Spirit who would later anoint kings, prophets, craftsmen.","cross":"Exodus 31:3","plain":"God's Spirit — present and active, hovering over the waters at creation."},"darkness_was":{"hebrew":"חֹשֶׁךְ הָיָה","translit":"choshech hayah","strongs":"-","def":"Pre-creation darkness — chaotic, unordered.","plain":"The dark, formless state before God brought order."},"Edomites":{"hebrew":"אֱדוֹמִים","translit":"edomim","strongs":"H130","def":"Descendants of Esau.","plain":"The descendants of Esau."},"Israelites":{"hebrew":"בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל","translit":"bnei yisrael","strongs":"-","def":"Sons of Israel — descendants of Jacob.","plain":"The descendants of Jacob (Israel)."},"King":{"hebrew":"מֶלֶךְ","translit":"melek","strongs":"H4428","root":"From malak — to reign, possess as ruler.","senses":["King — sovereign ruler","The supreme governing authority","YHWH as the Great King over all the earth","Earthly kings as deputies under YHWH (good and bad)","Messianic King — the ultimate Anointed Ruler"],"visual":"A throne. A crown. A scepter. A court. Subjects bowing. Decrees going out. Justice administered. War declared and ended. Wealth gathered and distributed. THIS is what melek meant in the ANE — and YHWH is repeatedly called Melek over all creation.","ane":"ANE kings were considered semi-divine. Egyptian Pharaoh was 'image of Ra.' Babylonian kings were sons of Marduk. Hebrew theology takes the SAME concept of kingship and applies it ONLY to YHWH at the highest level — and to humanity collectively as His vice-regent kings (Gen 1:26-28).","kingdom":"The whole biblical storyline is the kingdom storyline. YHWH is the true Melek. Humanity was made to be vice-regent kings (Gen 1:28). Israel was a 'kingdom of priests' (Ex 19:6). David's house was promised an eternal throne (2 Sam 7). Christ is born King of the Jews (Matt 2:2), enters Jerusalem as King (Matt 21:5), is crucified as King (Matt 27:37), reigns as King (Rev 19:16), and the saints reign with Him forever (Rev 22:5).","greek":"LXX: βασιλεύς (basileus). 'King of kings, Lord of lords' (Rev 19:16) = basileus basileōn.","cross":"Genesis 14:18, 17:6, 49:10; Exodus 15:18 ('YHWH shall reign for ever and ever'); 1 Samuel 8; 2 Samuel 7; Psalm 24:7-10, 47, 95-99; Isaiah 6:5, 9:6-7; Revelation 19:16","plain":"A ruler with full authority — throne, crown, and command. Ultimately God Himself."},"male":{"hebrew":"זָכָר","translit":"zakar","strongs":"H2145","root":"From the verb zakar (זכר) — 'to mark, remember, recall, bring to mind.' Same root as 'memorial' and 'remembrance.'","senses":["Male (biological sex) — the anatomical/biological designation","The remembered one — male as the carrier of family name and lineage","One who marks / one who is marked — through circumcision, the covenant sign was placed on the male body","The genealogical thread — genealogies trace through males because his name is 'remembered' through the line"],"visual":"In ANE patrilineal society, zakar is the one whose name is REMEMBERED through generations. The Genesis genealogies (chs 5, 10, 11, 25, 36, 46) trace through male lines because of this. To be zakar = to be the marker in the family scroll, the link who carries the name forward.","ane":"Throughout the ANE, male and female were biological-functional categories tied to procreation, lineage, and covenant continuity. The categories were binary, biological, and tied to anatomical reality — the Hebrew etymology makes this explicit. Genesis is not a modern social-construction text — it is an ANE text written in ANE categories.","theology":"Circumcision (Gen 17:10-14) was applied to 'every zakar' — the covenant sign was placed on male anatomy specifically because the male carried the seed (zera) of the covenant line. The seed of the woman / seed of Abraham promises (Gen 3:15, 12:7, 22:18) culminate in Christ — the ultimate zakar carrying redemption forward.","warning":"RULE 04 — NO OPINIONS. The text and the Hebrew lexicon use binary biological categories. Reporting that is not opinion; it is what the words say. Genesis predates modern category debates by ~3,500 years. To be faithful to the text, we use its categories.","greek":"LXX: ἄρσην (arsēn) — male. NT preserves the same biological category (Matt 19:4, where Jesus quotes Gen 1:27; Rom 1:27; Gal 3:28).","cross":"Genesis 1:27, 5:2, 6:19, 7:2-3, 7:9, 7:16, 17:10-14; Exodus 13:12, 13:15; Leviticus 12; Numbers 1; Matthew 19:4; Galatians 3:28","plain":"Male — the gender remembered through the family line."},"female":{"hebrew":"נְקֵבָה","translit":"neqebah","strongs":"H5347","root":"From the verb naqab (נקב) — 'to pierce, perforate, bore through, hollow out.' Direct anatomical etymology — the Hebrew is biologically descriptive without coyness.","senses":["Female (biological sex) — the anatomical designation referring to receptive anatomy","The bored/hollowed one — direct reference to female anatomy","Counterpart to zakar (male) — together they form the procreative pair"],"visual":"The Hebrew is direct and anatomical. Female = the one with receiving anatomy. The Bible is not coy about biological reality — God designed bodies and called them 'very good' (Gen 1:31). The neqebah is literally named for her sexed body. Honored, not hidden.","ane":"All Semitic languages use anatomically descriptive terms for male and female. Hebrew shares root forms with Akkadian, Aramaic, Ugaritic. The categories are biological-functional, not abstract. Both zakar and neqebah are equally created in God's image (Gen 1:27) — equality in dignity, distinction in body and function.","theology":"Genesis 1:27 is one of the most theologically dense verses in the Bible: 'In the image of God created He him; male and female created He them.' The IMAGE is shared equally. The bodies are distinct. Both are needed to fulfill the kingdom mandate (Gen 1:28 — be fruitful and multiply requires both).","kingdom":"Both zakar and neqebah are vice-regents. Both bear God's tselem. Both are commissioned to rule, subdue, fill the earth. Patriarchy in the biblical narrative is functional (covenant lineage) NOT a hierarchy of dignity. Sarah is consulted, Rebekah's consent is asked, Rachel is loved, Hannah's prayer changes Israel's history. Royal women run through the Messianic line — Tamar, Rahab, Ruth, Bathsheba (all named in Matt 1).","greek":"LXX: θῆλυς (thēlys) — female. Same biological-functional category. NT preserves it (Mark 10:6 quoting Gen 1:27; Rom 1:26).","cross":"Genesis 1:27, 5:2, 6:19, 7:2-3, 7:9, 7:16; Mark 10:6; Galatians 3:28","plain":"Female — God's deliberate design, equal partner with the male."},"helper":{"hebrew":"עֵזֶר","translit":"ezer","strongs":"H5828","root":"From azar (עזר) — to help, surround with help, support, deliver. Strong action word, NOT subordinate.","senses":["Helper, supporter, ally","Strong help, deliverer, rescuer","Used 21 times in the Tanakh — 16 of those of GOD as Israel's helper","Often paired with words for STRENGTH and DELIVERANCE"],"visual":"NOT a subordinate assistant. Ezer is a STRONG word. When Israel calls YHWH her ezer (Ps 33:20, 70:5, 115:9-11, 121:1-2, 124:8, 146:5), He is not a junior helper — He is the Mighty One who rescues. The same word applied to Eve in Gen 2:18 honors her as STRONG counterpart.","ane":"ANE cultures generally cast women as subordinate to men in legal status. Genesis 2:18 uses a word for the woman that elsewhere describes GOD HIMSELF helping His people. This is theological honor — Eve is named with a word otherwise reserved for divine rescue.","kenegdo":"The phrase is 'ezer kenegdo' — 'a helper corresponding to him.' Kenegdo means 'opposite him,' 'face-to-face with him,' 'corresponding to him.' Equality of rank, fitness for partnership. Not 'beneath him' — face-to-face with him.","warning":"Ezer kenegdo has been mistranslated and misused for centuries to suggest female subordination. The Hebrew does not teach this. Eve is co-image-bearer (1:27), co-ruler (1:28), and co-helper (2:18). Honor the text — don't reduce her with bad translation tradition.","theology":"Marriage in Genesis is partnership of equals with distinct roles. Both image-bearers, both vice-regents, complementary in body and function. The rest of the Bible, including Paul, builds on this Genesis foundation — Ephesians 5 calls husbands to LAY DOWN THEIR LIVES like Christ for the church, the highest possible standard of love.","greek":"LXX: βοηθός (boēthos) — helper, ally. Used of God in Heb 13:6: 'The Lord is my helper (boēthos), and I will not fear.'","cross":"Genesis 2:18, 2:20; Exodus 18:4; Deuteronomy 33:7, 26, 29; Psalm 33:20, 70:5, 115:9, 121:1-2, 124:8, 146:5; Hebrews 13:6","plain":"A strong rescuer and ally — the same word used of God helping His people. Not a lesser assistant."},"cleave":{"hebrew":"דָּבַק","translit":"davaq","strongs":"H1692","root":"Primary meaning: to cling, stick, adhere, glue, follow closely. Used of physical adherence AND covenant loyalty.","senses":["Cling, stick, adhere physically","Cleave to a person in covenant loyalty","Pursue closely, follow","Be joined together (used of Israel cleaving to YHWH and of marriage)"],"visual":"Glue. Welding. Two things made one and inseparable. Marriage in Gen 2:24 uses the same word God uses for Israel cleaving to Him (Deut 10:20). The marriage covenant is NOT a contract that can be terminated — it is a fusion that cannot be torn apart without damage.","ane":"ANE marriages often included contractual clauses for divorce. Hebrew marriage uses fusion-language. Yes, the Mosaic law later permits divorce 'because of hardness of heart' (Matt 19:8) — but Genesis sets the foundation: marriage is intended as davaq, permanent covenant adherence.","theology":"The husband-wife covenant in Genesis 2:24 mirrors the YHWH-Israel covenant. When Hosea speaks of Israel's idolatry, he uses adultery imagery — because Israel was supposed to davaq to YHWH the way a wife davaqs to her husband. The marriage covenant is icon of THE covenant.","greek":"LXX: προσκολλάω (proskollaō) — glue together. Same word Jesus uses in Matt 19:5/Mark 10:7 quoting Gen 2:24.","cross":"Genesis 2:24, 34:3; Deuteronomy 10:20, 11:22, 13:4, 30:20; Joshua 22:5, 23:8; Ruth 1:14; Matthew 19:5; Mark 10:7-8; Ephesians 5:31","plain":"To stick to and become one with — the bond of marriage."},"one":{"hebrew":"אֶחָד","translit":"echad","strongs":"H259","root":"Cardinal number: one. But carries deeper unity-meaning — composite oneness.","senses":["One in number","One unified whole — composite unity (multiple parts, one essence)","Used of God's oneness (Deut 6:4 — Shema: YHWH echad)","Used of marriage union (Gen 2:24 — basar echad, 'one flesh')","Used of unified actions ('with one accord')"],"visual":"Echad is not 'solitary one' (yachid is the word for solitary/only). Echad is UNITED one — like a cluster of grapes is 'one cluster' though many grapes (Num 13:23). The marriage echad makes two persons into one functional household, one inheritance line, one covenant unit — without erasing their distinctness.","theology":"The Shema (Deut 6:4) declares 'YHWH our God, YHWH echad' — God's own composite unity. Marriage echad (Gen 2:24) is the only other major echad in the Pentateuch — pointing to the deep parallel: the marriage union ICONS the divine unity. This is why marriage is sacred — it manifests God's own nature in the world.","kingdom":"The kingdom advances through echad units — covenant marriages producing covenant children. Babel was a counter-echad — humanity unified for self-exaltation. The true echad is humans aligned with God's purpose.","greek":"LXX: εἷς (heis). NT: 'one flesh' = sarx mia (Mark 10:8); 'God is one' = ho theos heis (Gal 3:20).","cross":"Genesis 2:24, 11:1, 11:6 (Babel); Deuteronomy 6:4 (Shema); Ezekiel 37:17, 22; Mark 10:7-9; Ephesians 4:4-6","plain":"United as one — many parts joined together, not just a lonely single."},"naked":{"hebrew":"עָרוֹם","translit":"arom","strongs":"H6174","root":"From arah (ערה) — to be bare, exposed, uncovered.","senses":["Physically naked, unclothed","Exposed, vulnerable","Without protection or covering","Innocent, unashamed (Gen 2:25) — the naked-without-shame state of pre-Fall"],"visual":"In Eden, naked carried no shame because nothing needed hiding — body and soul aligned, transparent before God and each other (Gen 2:25 — 'they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed'). After the Fall, naked becomes vulnerability and shame (3:7, 3:10) — they sew fig leaves, but God provides skin garments (3:21 — first death covers shame).","ane":"ANE cultures generally treated nakedness as either humiliation (slaves, captives) or fertility ritual. Genesis presents pre-Fall nakedness as INNOCENCE — neither erotic nor shameful, simply at-home in the body God designed.","theology":"The Fall introduces shame and the need for COVERING. From the skins of Genesis 3:21 to the priestly garments to Christ as our righteousness (Isa 61:10 — clothed in garments of salvation), the Bible's covering-narrative runs through Scripture. Final scene in Revelation: the saints clothed in white robes washed in the blood (Rev 7:14, 19:8). Eden's innocence restored.","greek":"LXX: γυμνός (gymnos) — naked. Source of 'gymnasium' (Greek athletes trained naked).","cross":"Genesis 2:25, 3:7, 3:10-11, 3:21; Job 1:21; Isaiah 61:10; Revelation 3:18, 7:14, 19:8","plain":"Bare. In Eden it meant total openness with no shame."},"begin":{"hebrew":"רֵאשִׁית","translit":"reshit","strongs":"H7225","see":"beginning","plain":"To start something. The very first word of the Bible (bereshit) comes from this root — everything begins with God."},"Beginning":{"hebrew":"בְּרֵאשִׁית","translit":"bereishit","strongs":"H7225","root":"From rosh (ראשׁ) — head, top, chief, first. Reshit = first-thing, head-thing, chief-thing.","senses":["Beginning in time — 'in the beginning' (Gen 1:1)","First-fruits, choice-portion — the BEST, the head of the harvest","Chief, principal, foremost","Source — that which starts a series","The HEBREW NAME OF THE BOOK — the book Christians call Genesis is called 'Bereishit' in Hebrew, named for its first word"],"visual":"Not just 'temporal start' — the HEAD/SOURCE/CHIEF. Reshit is the cornerstone, the headwater, the top of the mountain from which everything flows. The first verse declares: God is the reshit. He is the source from which all reality flows. Time, matter, energy, life, identity — all flow from His act of creation.","ane":"ANE creation myths generally start with primordial chaos or pre-existing material that the gods then shape. Genesis declares NO pre-existing condition. There was no battle, no chaos, no co-eternal matter — just God, who created. This is the most radical theological claim of the Tanakh: God is uncaused; everything else is caused.","theology":"Bereishit is a STATEMENT of divine sovereignty over time itself. Time is His creature. He is not in it; it is in Him. He sees beginning and end together (Isa 46:9-10). The whole biblical story unfolds from this starting point — and ends with a NEW beginning ('Behold, I make all things new,' Rev 21:5).","kingdom":"The Beginning = the King establishing His kingdom. Genesis 1-2 is the founding charter: cosmos as throne-room, earth as kingdom-territory, humanity as vice-regents, Eden as palace-garden. Genesis 3 is the rebellion. The rest of the Bible is reconquest. Revelation 22 is restoration complete.","greek":"LXX: ἐν ἀρχῇ (en archē). John 1:1 echoes this exact phrase: 'In the beginning was the Word.' John deliberately matches Genesis 1:1, declaring Christ as the divine Word who was THERE at the beginning, who IS reshit.","cross":"Genesis 1:1; Proverbs 8:22-31 (Wisdom at the beginning); John 1:1-3; Colossians 1:15-18; Hebrews 1:1-3; Revelation 21:5-6, 22:13","plain":"The starting point and the source from which everything flows."},"ish":{"hebrew":"אִישׁ","translit":"ish","strongs":"H376","root":"Possibly from a verb root meaning 'to exist' — 'the existing/standing one.' Different from adam (humankind) — ish is a specific individual male person.","senses":["Adult male, individual man","Husband (in marital context)","A particular person (sometimes generic 'someone')","One who is somebody — has standing, identity, presence","Counterpart to ishah — the relational pair"],"visual":"ISH does NOT appear in Genesis 1, NOR at Genesis 2:7 when Adam is first formed. He is just ADAM (humankind, taken from adamah/ground). Ish only appears at Genesis 2:23 — when Adam sees the woman and recognizes her. THE MOMENT she exists, HE becomes ish — because he now has an ishah. Manhood is awakened by the presence of woman.","wordplay":"Hebrew preserves a wordplay that English destroys. Ish (man) and ishah (woman) are THE SAME WORD with a feminine ending added. Adam's first poem (Gen 2:23) declares: 'she shall be called ishah because she was taken out of ish.' This is a deliberate Hebrew construction announcing fundamental ESSENCE-SAMENESS with relational differentiation. NOT two separate species — one essence, two expressions.","ane":"Other ANE languages have separate, unrelated words for 'man' and 'woman.' Hebrew uniquely names them as variants of a single word — encoding into the very LANGUAGE the truth that man and woman are one humanity. This is theology built into vocabulary.","theology":"Adam's first recorded speech (Gen 2:23) is poetry. He doesn't merely identify her — he CELEBRATES her. 'This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh.' The Hebrew is ecstatic. The first words spoken by a human being are a love-song.","kingdom":"The ish is the household head, the covenant representative, the one who stands before YHWH on behalf of his ishah and children. This is not entitlement — it is responsibility. He answers for the household. When Adam fails, GOD calls for him first, not Eve (Gen 3:9: 'And YHWH God called unto the man, and said unto him, Where art thou?').","greek":"LXX: ἀνήρ (anēr) — adult male / husband. ἄνθρωπος (anthropos) — human being / mankind generally.","cross":"Genesis 2:23-24, 3:6, 3:16, 4:1; Ephesians 5:25-33","_alias_of":"man","plain":"A human being. Early in Genesis it means humankind in general."},"ishah":{"hebrew":"אִשָּׁה","translit":"ishah","strongs":"H802","root":"Feminine form of ish. Hebrew adds the feminine ending '-ah' to ish — declaring her essential SAMENESS with him while distinguishing her by the body and role she bears.","senses":["Woman (adult female)","Wife (Hebrew uses ONE WORD for woman and wife — there is no separate term)","Female counterpart of ish","Adam's gift from God (Gen 2:22)"],"visual":"Adam SAW her and named her ishah — recognizing instantly that she came from him (the rib/side, Gen 2:21-22) and was for him. She is ish-with-a-feminine-ending. Same essence. Different role. Different body. Equal dignity.","ane":"In most ANE cultures, women had legal status only through male relatives — fathers, husbands, sons. Genesis is unique in placing the woman alongside the man in the IMAGE OF GOD itself (1:27). Her dignity is not derivative. The Hebrew text does NOT teach female inferiority — it teaches functional differentiation within shared image-bearing dignity.","wordplay":"The deliberate ish-ishah pairing is theological architecture. It says: man without woman is incomplete (2:18 — 'it is not good that the man should be alone'). Woman without man is the literal source of his completion. The pair is the unit. The single is the deficit.","marriage":"Marriage in Hebrew is established by Gen 2:24: 'Therefore shall a man (ish) leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave (davaq) unto his wife (ishah): and they shall be one flesh (basar echad).' This is the founding covenant. Three movements: LEAVE prior household — CLEAVE to spouse — BECOME one flesh. The order matters.","kingdom":"Ishah is co-regent with ish. The rule of Genesis 1:28 was given to BOTH. She is not a sub-ruler — she is a co-ruler. Her body brings forth life; his body initiates it; together they image God's own creative relational community.","greek":"LXX: γυνή (gynē) — woman / wife (same dual meaning as Hebrew).","cross":"Genesis 2:22-24, 3:1-13, 3:15, 3:16, 3:20; Ephesians 5:22-33; 1 Peter 3:1-7","_alias_of":"woman","plain":"A female human. Adam called her ishah, taken from man (ish)."},"zakar":{"hebrew":"זָכָר","translit":"zakar","strongs":"H2145","root":"From the verb zakar (זכר) — 'to mark, remember, recall, bring to mind.' Same root as 'memorial' and 'remembrance.'","senses":["Male (biological sex) — the anatomical/biological designation","The remembered one — male as the carrier of family name and lineage","One who marks / one who is marked — through circumcision, the covenant sign was placed on the male body","The genealogical thread — genealogies trace through males because his name is 'remembered' through the line"],"visual":"In ANE patrilineal society, zakar is the one whose name is REMEMBERED through generations. The Genesis genealogies (chs 5, 10, 11, 25, 36, 46) trace through male lines because of this. To be zakar = to be the marker in the family scroll, the link who carries the name forward.","ane":"Throughout the ANE, male and female were biological-functional categories tied to procreation, lineage, and covenant continuity. The categories were binary, biological, and tied to anatomical reality — the Hebrew etymology makes this explicit. Genesis is not a modern social-construction text — it is an ANE text written in ANE categories.","theology":"Circumcision (Gen 17:10-14) was applied to 'every zakar' — the covenant sign was placed on male anatomy specifically because the male carried the seed (zera) of the covenant line. The seed of the woman / seed of Abraham promises (Gen 3:15, 12:7, 22:18) culminate in Christ — the ultimate zakar carrying redemption forward.","warning":"RULE 04 — NO OPINIONS. The text and the Hebrew lexicon use binary biological categories. Reporting that is not opinion; it is what the words say. Genesis predates modern category debates by ~3,500 years. To be faithful to the text, we use its categories.","greek":"LXX: ἄρσην (arsēn) — male. NT preserves the same biological category (Matt 19:4, where Jesus quotes Gen 1:27; Rom 1:27; Gal 3:28).","cross":"Genesis 1:27, 5:2, 6:19, 7:2-3, 7:9, 7:16, 17:10-14; Exodus 13:12, 13:15; Leviticus 12; Numbers 1; Matthew 19:4; Galatians 3:28","_alias_of":"male","plain":"Male — the gender remembered through the family line."},"neqebah":{"hebrew":"נְקֵבָה","translit":"neqebah","strongs":"H5347","root":"From the verb naqab (נקב) — 'to pierce, perforate, bore through, hollow out.' Direct anatomical etymology — the Hebrew is biologically descriptive without coyness.","senses":["Female (biological sex) — the anatomical designation referring to receptive anatomy","The bored/hollowed one — direct reference to female anatomy","Counterpart to zakar (male) — together they form the procreative pair"],"visual":"The Hebrew is direct and anatomical. Female = the one with receiving anatomy. The Bible is not coy about biological reality — God designed bodies and called them 'very good' (Gen 1:31). The neqebah is literally named for her sexed body. Honored, not hidden.","ane":"All Semitic languages use anatomically descriptive terms for male and female. Hebrew shares root forms with Akkadian, Aramaic, Ugaritic. The categories are biological-functional, not abstract. Both zakar and neqebah are equally created in God's image (Gen 1:27) — equality in dignity, distinction in body and function.","theology":"Genesis 1:27 is one of the most theologically dense verses in the Bible: 'In the image of God created He him; male and female created He them.' The IMAGE is shared equally. The bodies are distinct. Both are needed to fulfill the kingdom mandate (Gen 1:28 — be fruitful and multiply requires both).","kingdom":"Both zakar and neqebah are vice-regents. Both bear God's tselem. Both are commissioned to rule, subdue, fill the earth. Patriarchy in the biblical narrative is functional (covenant lineage) NOT a hierarchy of dignity. Sarah is consulted, Rebekah's consent is asked, Rachel is loved, Hannah's prayer changes Israel's history. Royal women run through the Messianic line — Tamar, Rahab, Ruth, Bathsheba (all named in Matt 1).","greek":"LXX: θῆλυς (thēlys) — female. Same biological-functional category. NT preserves it (Mark 10:6 quoting Gen 1:27; Rom 1:26).","cross":"Genesis 1:27, 5:2, 6:19, 7:2-3, 7:9, 7:16; Mark 10:6; Galatians 3:28","_alias_of":"female","plain":"Female — God's deliberate design, equal partner with the male."},"ezer":{"hebrew":"עֵזֶר","translit":"ezer","strongs":"H5828","root":"From azar (עזר) — to help, surround with help, support, deliver. Strong action word, NOT subordinate.","senses":["Helper, supporter, ally","Strong help, deliverer, rescuer","Used 21 times in the Tanakh — 16 of those of GOD as Israel's helper","Often paired with words for STRENGTH and DELIVERANCE"],"visual":"NOT a subordinate assistant. Ezer is a STRONG word. When Israel calls YHWH her ezer (Ps 33:20, 70:5, 115:9-11, 121:1-2, 124:8, 146:5), He is not a junior helper — He is the Mighty One who rescues. The same word applied to Eve in Gen 2:18 honors her as STRONG counterpart.","ane":"ANE cultures generally cast women as subordinate to men in legal status. Genesis 2:18 uses a word for the woman that elsewhere describes GOD HIMSELF helping His people. This is theological honor — Eve is named with a word otherwise reserved for divine rescue.","kenegdo":"The phrase is 'ezer kenegdo' — 'a helper corresponding to him.' Kenegdo means 'opposite him,' 'face-to-face with him,' 'corresponding to him.' Equality of rank, fitness for partnership. Not 'beneath him' — face-to-face with him.","warning":"Ezer kenegdo has been mistranslated and misused for centuries to suggest female subordination. The Hebrew does not teach this. Eve is co-image-bearer (1:27), co-ruler (1:28), and co-helper (2:18). Honor the text — don't reduce her with bad translation tradition.","theology":"Marriage in Genesis is partnership of equals with distinct roles. Both image-bearers, both vice-regents, complementary in body and function. The rest of the Bible, including Paul, builds on this Genesis foundation — Ephesians 5 calls husbands to LAY DOWN THEIR LIVES like Christ for the church, the highest possible standard of love.","greek":"LXX: βοηθός (boēthos) — helper, ally. Used of God in Heb 13:6: 'The Lord is my helper (boēthos), and I will not fear.'","cross":"Genesis 2:18, 2:20; Exodus 18:4; Deuteronomy 33:7, 26, 29; Psalm 33:20, 70:5, 115:9, 121:1-2, 124:8, 146:5; Hebrews 13:6","_alias_of":"helper","plain":"A strong rescuer and ally — the same word used of God helping His people. Not a lesser assistant."},"davaq":{"hebrew":"דָּבַק","translit":"davaq","strongs":"H1692","root":"Primary meaning: to cling, stick, adhere, glue, follow closely. Used of physical adherence AND covenant loyalty.","senses":["Cling, stick, adhere physically","Cleave to a person in covenant loyalty","Pursue closely, follow","Be joined together (used of Israel cleaving to YHWH and of marriage)"],"visual":"Glue. Welding. Two things made one and inseparable. Marriage in Gen 2:24 uses the same word God uses for Israel cleaving to Him (Deut 10:20). The marriage covenant is NOT a contract that can be terminated — it is a fusion that cannot be torn apart without damage.","ane":"ANE marriages often included contractual clauses for divorce. Hebrew marriage uses fusion-language. Yes, the Mosaic law later permits divorce 'because of hardness of heart' (Matt 19:8) — but Genesis sets the foundation: marriage is intended as davaq, permanent covenant adherence.","theology":"The husband-wife covenant in Genesis 2:24 mirrors the YHWH-Israel covenant. When Hosea speaks of Israel's idolatry, he uses adultery imagery — because Israel was supposed to davaq to YHWH the way a wife davaqs to her husband. The marriage covenant is icon of THE covenant.","greek":"LXX: προσκολλάω (proskollaō) — glue together. Same word Jesus uses in Matt 19:5/Mark 10:7 quoting Gen 2:24.","cross":"Genesis 2:24, 34:3; Deuteronomy 10:20, 11:22, 13:4, 30:20; Joshua 22:5, 23:8; Ruth 1:14; Matthew 19:5; Mark 10:7-8; Ephesians 5:31","_alias_of":"cleave","plain":"To stick to and become one with — the bond of marriage."},"echad":{"hebrew":"אֶחָד","translit":"echad","strongs":"H259","root":"Cardinal number: one. But carries deeper unity-meaning — composite oneness.","senses":["One in number","One unified whole — composite unity (multiple parts, one essence)","Used of God's oneness (Deut 6:4 — Shema: YHWH echad)","Used of marriage union (Gen 2:24 — basar echad, 'one flesh')","Used of unified actions ('with one accord')"],"visual":"Echad is not 'solitary one' (yachid is the word for solitary/only). Echad is UNITED one — like a cluster of grapes is 'one cluster' though many grapes (Num 13:23). The marriage echad makes two persons into one functional household, one inheritance line, one covenant unit — without erasing their distinctness.","theology":"The Shema (Deut 6:4) declares 'YHWH our God, YHWH echad' — God's own composite unity. Marriage echad (Gen 2:24) is the only other major echad in the Pentateuch — pointing to the deep parallel: the marriage union ICONS the divine unity. This is why marriage is sacred — it manifests God's own nature in the world.","kingdom":"The kingdom advances through echad units — covenant marriages producing covenant children. Babel was a counter-echad — humanity unified for self-exaltation. The true echad is humans aligned with God's purpose.","greek":"LXX: εἷς (heis). NT: 'one flesh' = sarx mia (Mark 10:8); 'God is one' = ho theos heis (Gal 3:20).","cross":"Genesis 2:24, 11:1, 11:6 (Babel); Deuteronomy 6:4 (Shema); Ezekiel 37:17, 22; Mark 10:7-9; Ephesians 4:4-6","_alias_of":"one","plain":"United as one — many parts joined together, not just a lonely single."},"arom":{"hebrew":"עָרוֹם","translit":"arom","strongs":"H6174","root":"From arah (ערה) — to be bare, exposed, uncovered.","senses":["Physically naked, unclothed","Exposed, vulnerable","Without protection or covering","Innocent, unashamed (Gen 2:25) — the naked-without-shame state of pre-Fall"],"visual":"In Eden, naked carried no shame because nothing needed hiding — body and soul aligned, transparent before God and each other (Gen 2:25 — 'they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed'). After the Fall, naked becomes vulnerability and shame (3:7, 3:10) — they sew fig leaves, but God provides skin garments (3:21 — first death covers shame).","ane":"ANE cultures generally treated nakedness as either humiliation (slaves, captives) or fertility ritual. Genesis presents pre-Fall nakedness as INNOCENCE — neither erotic nor shameful, simply at-home in the body God designed.","theology":"The Fall introduces shame and the need for COVERING. From the skins of Genesis 3:21 to the priestly garments to Christ as our righteousness (Isa 61:10 — clothed in garments of salvation), the Bible's covering-narrative runs through Scripture. Final scene in Revelation: the saints clothed in white robes washed in the blood (Rev 7:14, 19:8). Eden's innocence restored.","greek":"LXX: γυμνός (gymnos) — naked. Source of 'gymnasium' (Greek athletes trained naked).","cross":"Genesis 2:25, 3:7, 3:10-11, 3:21; Job 1:21; Isaiah 61:10; Revelation 3:18, 7:14, 19:8","_alias_of":"naked","plain":"Bare. In Eden it meant total openness with no shame."},"bereshit":{"hebrew":"בְּרֵאשִׁית","translit":"bereishit","strongs":"H7225","root":"From rosh (ראשׁ) — head, top, chief, first. Reshit = first-thing, head-thing, chief-thing.","senses":["Beginning in time — 'in the beginning' (Gen 1:1)","First-fruits, choice-portion — the BEST, the head of the harvest","Chief, principal, foremost","Source — that which starts a series","The HEBREW NAME OF THE BOOK — the book Christians call Genesis is called 'Bereishit' in Hebrew, named for its first word"],"visual":"Not just 'temporal start' — the HEAD/SOURCE/CHIEF. Reshit is the cornerstone, the headwater, the top of the mountain from which everything flows. The first verse declares: God is the reshit. He is the source from which all reality flows. Time, matter, energy, life, identity — all flow from His act of creation.","ane":"ANE creation myths generally start with primordial chaos or pre-existing material that the gods then shape. Genesis declares NO pre-existing condition. There was no battle, no chaos, no co-eternal matter — just God, who created. This is the most radical theological claim of the Tanakh: God is uncaused; everything else is caused.","theology":"Bereishit is a STATEMENT of divine sovereignty over time itself. Time is His creature. He is not in it; it is in Him. He sees beginning and end together (Isa 46:9-10). The whole biblical story unfolds from this starting point — and ends with a NEW beginning ('Behold, I make all things new,' Rev 21:5).","kingdom":"The Beginning = the King establishing His kingdom. Genesis 1-2 is the founding charter: cosmos as throne-room, earth as kingdom-territory, humanity as vice-regents, Eden as palace-garden. Genesis 3 is the rebellion. The rest of the Bible is reconquest. Revelation 22 is restoration complete.","greek":"LXX: ἐν ἀρχῇ (en archē). John 1:1 echoes this exact phrase: 'In the beginning was the Word.' John deliberately matches Genesis 1:1, declaring Christ as the divine Word who was THERE at the beginning, who IS reshit.","cross":"Genesis 1:1; Proverbs 8:22-31 (Wisdom at the beginning); John 1:1-3; Colossians 1:15-18; Hebrews 1:1-3; Revelation 21:5-6, 22:13","_alias_of":"Beginning","plain":"The starting point and the source from which everything flows."},"bara":{"hebrew":"בָּרָא","translit":"bara","strongs":"H1254","root":"Possibly cognate with 'to cut, separate.' But its theological usage is unique.","senses":["To create — uniquely DIVINE action; subject is ALWAYS God in the Tanakh","To bring into being something genuinely new — not reshape existing material","To create ex nihilo (out of nothing) — at least at the absolute beginning","To create as an expression of sovereign will — speech-act creation in Genesis 1"],"visual":"Something coming into existence that was NOT there before. Not the potter shaping clay (that's yatzar). Not the carpenter building (that's banah). Bara is the act only God can do — calling reality forth.","ane":"In ANE creation myths, gods shape pre-existing chaotic matter. The Babylonian Enuma Elish has Marduk forming the world from the slain body of the chaos-goddess Tiamat. Genesis declares something radically different: YHWH bara'd. He didn't fight chaos; He spoke and there was no chaos.","kingdom":"Only the King creates ex nihilo. Vice-regents (humans) make and shape (asah, yatzar) — but only YHWH creates (bara). Our creativity reflects His but never replicates the absolute origination.","greek":"LXX: ἐποίησεν (epoiēsen) — 'made.' This is a TRANSLATION LOSS. Greek had no equivalent for divine ex nihilo creation, so they used the general 'made.' Hebrew preserves the unique theological force.","cross":"Genesis 1:1, 1:21, 1:27, 2:3, 5:1; Isaiah 40:26, 41:20, 43:1, 65:17; Romans 4:17 ('calleth those things which be not as though they were'); Hebrews 11:3","_alias_of":"created","plain":"Made something brand-new that never existed before — something only God can do."},"Elohim":{"hebrew":"אֱלֹהִים","translit":"Elohim","strongs":"H430","def":"Plural noun (-im) used with singular verbs. Plural of majesty/fullness, NOT polytheism. Encompasses divine power, sovereignty, transcendence.","ane":"Other ANE peoples used 'el' for their gods. Hebrew Elohim is the same root but uniquely paired with singular verbs — declaring this is the ONE God who contains all divine power.","cross":"Genesis 1:26 (Let US make...)","_alias_of":"God","plain":"The one true God — all-powerful, the maker of everything."},"tselem":{"hebrew":"צֶלֶם","translit":"tselem","strongs":"H6754","root":"From a root meaning 'to shade' or 'represent.' A tselem is a statue or representation that stands in for the original.","senses":["Statue, idol — physical representation of a deity (Num 33:52)","Image — concrete likeness, not abstract resemblance","Representative — one who stands in place of another","Royal authority symbol — a king's tselem placed in conquered cities declared HIS rule there"],"visual":"A standing statue, the kind a king set up in distant provinces. The statue represented the king's PERSON and his AUTHORITY. To deface the statue was to attack the king. To honor the statue was to honor him. Now imagine: HUMANITY is God's tselem — His standing representation on the earth.","ane":"ANE kings were called 'tselem' of their gods. Pharaoh was tselem of Ra. The Assyrian king was tselem of Marduk. Only the king. Genesis 1:26-28 DEMOCRATIZES this radically — every human is tselem of YHWH. Every person you meet is a king's statue. THIS is the foundation of human dignity.","kingdom":"Tselem is the basis of vice-regency. We are not subjects of the King — we are His statues, placed on earth to declare 'YHWH rules here.' Our calling is to represent His character, exercise His authority, and extend His order. Sin is treason against this calling. Redemption is restoration of the image.","theology":"Genesis 9:6 makes tselem the basis of the prohibition against murder: kill a human, you've attacked the King's representation. Colossians 1:15 says CHRIST is 'the image (eikon) of the invisible God' — He is tselem in fullness. We are conformed to HIS image (Rom 8:29).","greek":"LXX: εἰκών (eikon). NT picks this up to describe Christ as the perfect image (Col 1:15; 2 Cor 4:4) and believers as being conformed to His image (Rom 8:29; 2 Cor 3:18).","cross":"Genesis 1:26-27, 5:1, 9:6; Colossians 1:15, 3:10; 2 Corinthians 3:18, 4:4; Romans 8:29; James 3:9","_alias_of":"image","plain":"A stand-in that represents someone. Humans are made to represent God on earth."},"demut":{"hebrew":"דְּמוּת","translit":"demut","strongs":"H1823","def":"Likeness, similarity, pattern, model. Functional and relational correspondence.","ane":"Together with tselem (image) = full representational identity. We don't just resemble God, we FUNCTION as His representatives.","cross":"James 3:9","_alias_of":"likeness","plain":"Made to resemble — patterned after God."},"radah":{"hebrew":"רָדָה","translit":"radah","strongs":"H7287","root":"Primary meaning: to tread, trample (as a wine-press). Extended to 'rule' because rulers metaphorically 'tread upon' their domain.","senses":["To rule, exercise sovereignty","To tread, trample (as treading grapes in a winepress)","To subjugate (use of force in some contexts)","Royal kingship terminology — what kings do"],"visual":"A king walking through his territory, his authority felt by everyone he passes. The image of treading is concrete — feet making contact with the ground, marking it as YOURS.","ane":"Royal verb. In ANE texts only kings 'rdh.' For Genesis 1:26-28 to give this verb to ALL humanity is staggering — every human is given royal authority over creation.","warning":"Radah is NOT a license for exploitation. The same Adam given radah was placed in the garden 'to dress it and to keep it' (avad-shamar — to serve and guard). Kingly rule in the biblical pattern is SERVANT-RULE, not tyranny. Christ embodies this: 'the Son of Man came not to be served but to serve' (Mark 10:45).","kingdom":"This is the verb of vice-regency. Humans were created to RULE — not to be ruled by creation, not to flee from it, not to merely survive in it. We were made to GOVERN it as God's deputies.","greek":"LXX: ἄρχω (archō) — to rule, govern.","cross":"Genesis 1:26, 1:28; Psalm 8:6, 72:8, 110:2; 1 Peter 2:9 (royal priesthood); Revelation 5:10 (we shall reign on the earth)","_alias_of":"dominion","plain":"Being in charge — ruling over something, the way a king rules his land."},"kabash":{"hebrew":"כָּבַשׁ","translit":"kabash","strongs":"H3533","root":"Primary meaning: to tread down, to bring under foot.","senses":["To subjugate, bring under control","To conquer (used of military conquest, Num 32:22)","To bring into submission","To master (something wild or unruly)"],"visual":"Putting your foot on something to claim it. Bringing wild ground into cultivation. Taming what was untamed. Imposing order on what was chaotic but not evil — like a farmer turning fallow ground into a field.","ane":"Conquest language. The earth in Genesis 1 was created GOOD but not yet EDEN. Eden was a garden within the world. Humanity's job was to extend Eden's order over the rest of the earth — to KABASH it. To make it all sacred space.","kingdom":"Together with radah (rule), kabash (subdue) is the kingdom mandate. Not rape of the earth — extension of order, beauty, fruitfulness, the King's reign. The wild becomes garden under the vice-regent's hands.","greek":"LXX: κατακυριεύω (katakyrieuō) — to lord over, master.","cross":"Genesis 1:28; Joshua 18:1; 1 Chronicles 22:18; Micah 7:19 ('he will subdue our iniquities')","_alias_of":"subdue","plain":"To bring something wild under control and put it to good use."},"nephesh":{"hebrew":"נֶפֶשׁ","translit":"nephesh","strongs":"H5315","root":"From a primitive root meaning 'to breathe; to be refreshed.' Connected to the throat (the breathing organ) as the literal physical seat.","senses":["Throat / neck — 'the waters are come in unto my soul/throat' (Ps 69:1); 'iron entered into his soul/neck' (Ps 105:18)","Breath — the active principle of breathing","Living being / creature — used of animals AND humans (Gen 1:20-21, 2:7)","Person — 'the souls that they had gotten in Haran' (Gen 12:5) means the PEOPLE they had acquired","Self — 'I love you with all my soul' = with my whole self","Will / desire / appetite — 'if it be your soul (your will) that I should bury my dead' (Gen 23:8); 'whatsoever thy soul desireth' (Deut 14:26)","Emotion / feeling — 'my soul cleaveth to thee' (Ps 63:8); 'him whom my soul loveth' (Song 3:1)","Mind, intellect, intent — 'with all your soul' = full mental engagement (Deut 6:5)","Life itself — 'his soul shall live' (1 Kgs 19:4)","The whole inner person — MIND, WILL, EMOTION unified — your popular framework names this accurately","The seat of life in the blood — 'the life (nephesh) of the flesh is in the blood' (Lev 17:11)"],"visual":"NOT a ghost-like inner thing trapped in a body. Nephesh is the WHOLE LIVING PERSON viewed from the inside — the breathing, feeling, thinking, willing, desiring self. When you look in someone's eyes and see 'them' looking back — that is nephesh. The throat connection is concrete: your nephesh is what catches your breath when you're afraid, what swallows when you're moved, what thirsts and hungers and longs.","ane":"Hebrew thought is RADICALLY different from Greek philosophy here. Greek (Plato → some Christian streams) sees the soul as an immaterial entity trapped in a body, escaping at death to a higher realm. Hebrew thought has NO concept of disembodied soul-life. Nephesh is the unified living person — body and breath together. When body dies, the nephesh ceases as a living entity (Ezek 18:4: 'the soul that sinneth, it shall die'). The Hebrew hope is RESURRECTION — the body raised whole — not soul-flight to heaven.","warning":"RULE 07 — NO PLATONISM. Do not read Greek dualism into nephesh. The Bible does NOT teach 'you are a soul who has a body.' It teaches you ARE a living, breathing, embodied nephesh — body and breath unified.","psychology":"The popular 'mind, will, emotion' framework (Watchman Nee and others) accurately captures the inner faculties of nephesh — AS LONG AS you remember nephesh ALSO includes physical breath, throat, appetite, and the whole living self. The framework is right; just don't shrink nephesh to fit it.","greek":"LXX: ψυχή (psyche). In Greek-philosophy contexts the word can drift toward the dualist sense, but Hebrew-rooted NT writers preserve nephesh meaning. Jesus says 'fear him who can destroy both soul (psyche) AND body in hell' (Matt 10:28) — distinguishing them but not Platonically separating.","kingdom":"Image-bearers are NEPHESH UNITS — the whole living self as God's vice-regent. We rule with our entire being, not just our minds.","cross":"Genesis 2:7, 12:5; Leviticus 17:11; Deuteronomy 6:5; 1 Kings 19:4; Psalm 42:1, 63:1, 69:1; Ezekiel 18:4; Matthew 10:28; Hebrews 4:12","_alias_of":"soul","plain":"The whole living person — especially your inner self."},"neshamah":{"hebrew":"נְשָׁמָה","translit":"neshamah","strongs":"H5397","root":"From nasham — to pant, blow.","senses":["Breath of life — specifically God's breath, distinct from mere air","Inspiration, divine spark — what God breathes uniquely into humanity (Gen 2:7)","The candle of YHWH — 'the spirit (neshamah) of man is the candle of YHWH' (Prov 20:27) — neshamah as God's lamp inside us","Breath of all the living — used poetically for life itself (Isa 42:5)"],"visual":"More intimate than ruach (wind). Neshamah is FACE-TO-FACE breath — like CPR. God breathes into Adam's nostrils (Gen 2:7) — the only being to receive this direct divine breath.","ane":"Other ANE creation stories have humans formed but NOT god-breathed. Hebrew Genesis adds this UNIQUE detail: only humans receive God's neshamah. Animals have ruach but not neshamah. This is what makes humans IMAGE-BEARING — the divine breath is the source of our representational capacity.","kingdom":"Neshamah is what qualifies humans to rule. It is divine investment — the King's own breath in His vice-regents.","greek":"LXX: πνοή (pnoē). NT picks this up in Acts 17:25 — God 'giveth to all life and breath (pnoē).'","cross":"Genesis 2:7, 7:22; Job 32:8, 33:4; Proverbs 20:27; Isaiah 42:5; Acts 17:25","_alias_of":"breath","plain":"Close, life-giving breath. God breathed life into the first man."},"ruach":{"hebrew":"רוּחַ","translit":"ruach","strongs":"H7307","root":"From a verb meaning 'to blow, breathe.' All three meanings (wind/breath/spirit) flow from the same root act of moving air.","senses":["Wind — physical moving air (Gen 8:1 — 'God made a wind to pass over the earth')","Breath — the breath that moves in lungs (Job 12:10)","Spirit / animating life-force — what makes a body alive","Disposition / mood / state of mind — 'a hard spirit' (Deut 2:30); 'a spirit of jealousy' (Num 5:14)","The Spirit of God — YHWH's active power (Gen 1:2 — 'the Spirit of God moved upon the face of the waters')","Prophetic inspiration — the Spirit comes upon prophets, kings, craftsmen","Mind / intellect — 'whose ruach (mind/spirit) hath given Him counsel?' (Isa 40:13)"],"visual":"Invisible but powerful — like the wind. You don't see ruach itself; you see what it MOVES. Hovering over the deep at creation. Filling Bezalel for craftsmanship (Ex 31:3). Coming upon David at his anointing. Like a breath you can't grasp but that brings life.","ane":"In ANE worldview, ruach is what distinguishes living from dead, animate from inanimate. NOT a separate immaterial part of you — the breathing-life that fills you and CAN BE GIVEN OR TAKEN by God. When God 'gathers His ruach' (Job 34:14), creatures die. When He sends His ruach forth, things live (Ps 104:30).","warning":"RULE 07 — Greek philosophy splits body/soul/spirit into three separable layers. Hebrew unifies them. The Spirit, the breath, the wind — same word, same reality flowing in different scales. Don't impose Western three-part anthropology onto a Hebrew word that resists it.","greek":"LXX: πνεῦμα (pneuma). Same threefold range. Jesus exploits this in John 3:8 — 'the wind (pneuma) bloweth where it listeth… so is everyone that is born of the Spirit (pneuma).'","kingdom":"The Spirit of God is what equips humans to BE vice-regents. Kings of Israel were anointed by Spirit; prophets, judges, craftsmen all served by Spirit. Without ruach, dominion is impossible.","cross":"Genesis 1:2, 6:3, 8:1; Exodus 31:3; Numbers 11:25; Judges 14:6; 1 Samuel 10:6; Job 33:4; Psalm 104:30; Isaiah 11:2; Joel 2:28; John 3:8","_alias_of":"Spirit","plain":"God's invisible, powerful presence — like wind you can't see but can feel working."},"adam":{"hebrew":"אָדָם","translit":"adam","strongs":"H120","root":"From adamah (אֲדָמָה) — ground, soil, especially red/cultivated earth. Wordplay: humans formed from humus, the red earth. The name itself means 'earth-creature' or 'red one' (some Hebrew suggests skin-color connection via the red earth).","senses":["Humankind, humanity (collective term, includes male and female together)","Human being (singular, sometimes used generically)","Adam (proper name of the first man, especially from Gen 4 onward)","Earth-creature — emphasizing material origin from soil"],"visual":"Adam is the SPECIES name first, the individual name second. In Genesis 1:26-27, 'adam' is used for HUMANITY — both male and female together: 'Let us make adam in our image… male and female created He THEM.' Only in Genesis 2 does adam become specifically the first individual man. He IS humankind, condensed into one body.","ane":"ANE creation accounts often portrayed humans as made from clay or earth — but typically as servants of the gods, made to do their work. Genesis radically subverts this: humans are made from earth NOT to serve the gods' labor, but to RULE as the King's image-bearing vice-regents. Same dust, totally different vocation.","theology":"Adam = earth-creature. From dust we came, to dust we return (Gen 3:19). BUT — God breathed neshamah into this dust, and dust became image-bearer. Humanity is dust-and-divine-breath unified. Christ as the second Adam (1 Cor 15:45) reverses the first Adam's failure — same flesh, same dust, but obedient where the first failed.","skin_note":"The Hebrew root behind adam (and adamah, 'red earth') has been connected by some to the Hebrew adom (אָדֹם) meaning 'red.' The first humans, formed from Levantine/Mesopotamian soil, would have had brown skin in the Semitic phenotype range. NOT pale/European — that's anachronistic projection. Sourced: Genesis 2:7 (formed from adamah); standard ANE archaeology of regional populations.","kingdom":"Adam was the first vice-regent. The kingdom mandate was given to him AND her. The image of God was placed in adam — male and female together. We are still adam — earth-creatures with divine breath, bearing His image, called to rule.","greek":"LXX: Ἀδάμ (Adam) as proper name; ἄνθρωπος (anthropos) as 'humankind / human being.'","cross":"Genesis 1:26-27, 2:7, 2:15-25, 3:9-19, 5:1-5; Romans 5:12-21; 1 Corinthians 15:22, 45-49","_alias_of":"Adam","plain":"The first human — his name also means 'humankind.' Made from the ground."},"adamah":{"hebrew":"אֲדָמָה","translit":"adamah","strongs":"H127","def":"Tilled soil, ground, earth. Source of Adam's name.","_alias_of":"ground","plain":"The soil/earth — what the first human was formed from."},"yatzar":{"hebrew":"יָצַר","translit":"yatzar","strongs":"H3335","def":"To form, fashion (as a potter forms clay). Gen 2:7 — God formed the man from dust.","ane":"Pottery imagery: the maker has total authority over the made. Isaiah picks this up: 'Shall the clay say to the potter?' (Isa 29:16).","_alias_of":"formed","plain":"Shaped by hand, like a potter shaping clay. God formed the first man from dust."},"asah":{"hebrew":"עָשָׂה","translit":"asah","strongs":"H6213","def":"To do, fashion, make. More general than bara — describes God shaping or humans crafting.","ane":"The pairing of bara and asah in Genesis 1: God both originates AND shapes.","_alias_of":"made","plain":"Shaped or put together. More general than 'created.'"},"shabbat":{"hebrew":"שָׁבַת","translit":"shabbat","strongs":"H7673","def":"To cease, desist. Source of 'Sabbath.' NOT exhaustion but completed satisfaction.","ane":"In ANE creation accounts, the gods rest because they made humans to do their work. YHWH rests because His work is COMPLETE — and INVITES humanity into His rest as image-bearers, not slaves.","cross":"Hebrews 4:9-10","_alias_of":"rest","plain":"Stopping work. God rested on the seventh day."},"berit":{"hebrew":"בְּרִית","translit":"berit","strongs":"H1285","root":"Possibly from barah (to cut) or from an Akkadian root meaning 'between.' The Hebrew idiom is 'karet berit' — to CUT a covenant.","senses":["Covenant — solemn binding agreement","Treaty (between nations or kings)","Pledge, oath, alliance","Marriage as covenant (Mal 2:14)","Friendship covenant (Jonathan and David, 1 Sam 18:3)"],"visual":"Animals cut in halves, laid opposite each other forming a corridor of blood. The covenant parties walk between the pieces. Their walk says: 'so let me be cut in pieces if I break this.' This is what 'cutting a covenant' means concretely. Genesis 15:17 shows YHWH alone (a smoking pot and torch) walking between — taking ALL covenant obligations on Himself.","ane":"Standard ANE treaty form. Suzerain-vassal treaties were 'cut' with this animal ritual. Hittite treaties from the 2nd millennium BC follow the exact pattern. The Hebrew Bible doesn't invent covenant — it uses the cultural form to express divine relationship. YHWH is the suzerain; His people are the vassals; the covenant binds both. Jeremiah 34:18-20 describes the practice still active in the 6th century BC.","kingdom":"Every kingdom is built on covenants. The Adamic covenant (implied in Eden), the Noahic, Abrahamic, Mosaic, Davidic, and New Covenants are stages of God establishing His kingdom on earth. Berit is the legal instrument of His reign.","greek":"LXX: διαθήκη (diathēkē) — usually 'will/testament' in Greek, but used here to translate berit. NT uses the same word; 'New Testament/Covenant' = kainē diathēkē.","cross":"Genesis 6:18, 9:9-17, 15:18, 17:2-21; Exodus 19-24; 2 Samuel 7; Jeremiah 31:31-34, 34:18-20; Malachi 2:14; Hebrews 8-9","_alias_of":"covenant","plain":"A sacred, binding agreement — a promise sealed between two parties."},"baruch":{"hebrew":"בָּרוּךְ","translit":"baruch","strongs":"H1288","root":"From barach — to kneel. The connection: blessing-receivers knelt before kings.","senses":["Blessed, favored, empowered","Endowed with God's covenant goodness","Source of further blessing — 'thou shalt be a blessing' (Gen 12:2)","Made fruitful, productive","Set apart for divine purpose"],"visual":"A kneeling vassal receiving a king's hand on his head — anointed, charged, sent. A field that yields harvest. A womb that bears children. A name that becomes a blessing in itself.","ane":"ANE kings 'blessed' subjects by formal proclamation, often laying hands. Patriarchs blessed sons by laying hands and pronouncing destiny (Gen 27, 48). The blessing was not a wish — it was a transfer of authority and power.","kingdom":"Blessing is how the King distributes His kingdom. Adam was blessed (Gen 1:28) and given dominion. Abraham was blessed and made a father of nations. We are blessed in Christ (Eph 1:3) — already given everything pertaining to life and godliness.","greek":"LXX: εὐλογημένος (eulogēmenos) — 'well-spoken-of.' Beatitudes use μακάριος (makarios) — 'happy, blessed.'","cross":"Genesis 1:28, 12:2-3, 14:19, 24:1, 27:27-29, 49; Psalm 1:1; Matthew 5:3-12; Ephesians 1:3","_alias_of":"blessed","plain":"Given God's favor — set up to flourish."},"barach":{"hebrew":"בָּרַךְ","translit":"barach","strongs":"H1288","def":"To kneel; to bless. The blesser empowers; the blessed kneels.","_alias_of":"bless","plain":"To speak good over someone and give them favor."},"shem":{"hebrew":"שֵׁם","translit":"shem","strongs":"H8034","root":"Possibly from a root meaning 'mark, sign.' A name marks/identifies the bearer.","senses":["Name — identifier","Character — what one is known for","Reputation — how one is regarded","Authority — what one's name accomplishes","Memory — being 'remembered' through one's name","Renown, fame"],"visual":"Not a label. A name in Hebrew thought is a person's REALITY made visible. Calling someone's name = invoking their character. Giving a new name = transforming identity. Erasing a name = un-creating the person.","ane":"ANE peoples knew/used divine names for power. Egyptian magic invoked names of gods. YHWH gave His covenant name (Ex 3:14) as a GIFT, not a magic formula. To 'call upon the name of YHWH' (Gen 4:26) = to relate to Him as covenant partner.","kingdom":"Kings ruled BY THEIR NAME — proclamations went out 'in the name of the king.' YHWH's people pray, prophesy, and exercise authority 'in the name of YHWH.' To bear God's name (image-bearing humanity) is to carry His authority and accountability.","greek":"LXX: ὄνομα (onoma). NT 'name of Jesus' carries the full weight of Hebrew shem.","cross":"Genesis 4:26, 11:4, 12:2, 17:5, 32:28; Exodus 3:13-15, 20:7; Proverbs 22:1; Acts 4:12; Philippians 2:9-11","_alias_of":"name","plain":"In the Bible, a name stands for a person's whole character and reality."},"chayyim":{"hebrew":"חַיִּים","translit":"chayyim","strongs":"H2416","def":"Life. Always plural in Hebrew (-im) — life is ABUNDANCE.","_alias_of":"life","plain":"Life — breath, heartbeat, and motion."},"chayah":{"hebrew":"חָיָה","translit":"chayah","strongs":"H2421","def":"To live. Source of chayyim and Chavvah.","_alias_of":"live","plain":"To live, to be alive, to survive. The verb behind 'life' (chayyim), 'Eve' (Chavvah — the life-giver), and every living creature."},"mavet":{"hebrew":"מָוֶת","translit":"mavet","strongs":"H4194","def":"Death. The wages of sin.","cross":"Romans 5:12","_alias_of":"death","plain":"Death — the end of breath and life."},"tov":{"hebrew":"טוֹב","translit":"tov","strongs":"H2896","root":"Primary adjective for goodness in Hebrew.","senses":["Good, fitting, pleasing — what works as designed","Beautiful (aesthetic)","Prosperous, successful","Morally good, righteous","Useful, beneficial","Joyful, pleasant"],"visual":"When a craftsman steps back and sees his work as it should be — that's tov. When a meal tastes right, when a song lands, when a relationship is whole — tov. Not just 'morally good' (that's English narrowing) but 'good in every dimension at once.'","ane":"ANE craftsmen used similar terms for 'a job well done.' Genesis 1's seven 'tov' verdicts on creation declare God a satisfied Master Craftsman.","kingdom":"The King's verdict 'tov' is creation's title deed. The world IS good — not as a moral assessment but as the King's stamp. Sin damaged it but didn't unmake it. The creation was tov, and will be again.","greek":"LXX: καλός (kalos) — good, beautiful. Same dual aesthetic-moral sense.","cross":"Genesis 1:4, 10, 12, 18, 21, 25, 31; Mark 10:18 ('there is none good but one, that is, God')","_alias_of":"good","plain":"The way things are meant to be — right, beautiful, and working as designed."},"ra":{"hebrew":"רַע","translit":"ra","strongs":"H7451","def":"Evil, wrong, harmful, bad. The opposite of tov.","_alias_of":"evil","plain":"What is wrong, harmful, and broken — the opposite of good."},"daat":{"hebrew":"דַּעַת","translit":"daat","strongs":"H1847","root":"From yada — to know.","senses":["Knowing by experience (NOT just intellectual data)","Intimate knowledge — used of sexual union (Gen 4:1: 'Adam knew Eve his wife')","Skill, expertise (Ex 31:3)","Recognition — as opposed to ignorance","Discernment between options","Relational knowing — God 'knows' His people"],"visual":"Not data in your head. Knowledge is intimate, lived, experiential. To know YHWH is to walk with Him. To know good and evil is to have moral experience of them. The Tree of Knowledge wasn't an information-tree — it was an experience-tree.","ane":"Hebrew thought is RELATIONAL, not abstract. To 'know' something is to have experiential acquaintance. Greek thought (later) develops abstract conceptual knowing. Hebrew daat is body-and-relationship knowing.","kingdom":"Vice-regents need daat — practical wisdom to rule well. Solomon asked for daat. The world breaks down without leaders who know YHWH and know His ways.","greek":"LXX: γνῶσις (gnōsis). NT preserves both senses but often emphasizes the relational/experiential.","cross":"Genesis 2:9, 4:1; Exodus 31:3; Hosea 4:6 ('My people are destroyed for lack of knowledge'); Proverbs 1:7, 2:6, 9:10; Philippians 3:10 ('that I may know him')","_alias_of":"knowledge","plain":"Knowing something deeply through experience — not just facts in your head."},"hesed":{"hebrew":"חֶסֶד","translit":"hesed","strongs":"H2617","root":"Possibly from a root meaning 'to bend, bow' — to incline toward in mercy.","senses":["Loyal love — covenant kindness","Steadfast mercy","Faithful commitment","Lovingkindness","Grace — undeserved favor","Devotion — going beyond strict obligation"],"visual":"A spouse who keeps faith when the other has failed. A friend who shows up when the chips are down. A king who pardons when justice could condemn. Not sentiment — covenant LOYALTY embodied as action.","ane":"Hesed has NO English equivalent. Some translate 'lovingkindness,' others 'covenant love,' others just leave it untranslated. ANE treaty texts use comparable terms for the loyalty owed between covenant partners. YHWH's hesed is the bedrock of every covenant He makes.","kingdom":"The King's hesed is what makes covenant possible at all. Without hesed, covenants would collapse the first time a vassal failed. With hesed, the relationship endures. Israel's whole hope through 1500 years of failure was YHWH's hesed enduring forever (Ps 136 — 'his hesed endureth forever' repeated 26 times).","greek":"LXX: ἔλεος (eleos) — mercy. NT picks this up. χάρις (charis) — grace — also captures part of hesed's range.","cross":"Genesis 24:12, 39:21; Exodus 34:6-7; Psalm 23:6, 51:1, 136 (whole psalm); Hosea 6:6; Micah 6:8 ('love hesed'); Lamentations 3:22-23","_alias_of":"kindness","plain":"Loyal love that keeps showing up even when it's undeserved."},"ahavah":{"hebrew":"אַהֲבָה","translit":"ahavah","strongs":"H160","def":"Love. Used of God's love AND human/marital love.","cross":"Genesis 24:67","_alias_of":"love","plain":"Choosing to commit yourself to another's good — more than a feeling."},"emunah":{"hebrew":"אֱמוּנָה","translit":"emunah","strongs":"H530","def":"Firmness, faithfulness, trust.","cross":"Hebrews 11","_alias_of":"faith","plain":"Trust — confident reliance on God, with good reason to trust Him."},"aman":{"hebrew":"אָמַן","translit":"aman","strongs":"H539","def":"To be firm, faithful, trust. Foundation of biblical faith.","cross":"Romans 4:3; Galatians 3:6","_alias_of":"believed","plain":"Trusted and relied on — the root of biblical faith."},"tsedaqah":{"hebrew":"צְדָקָה","translit":"tsedaqah","strongs":"H6666","root":"From tsadaq — to be straight, right, just.","senses":["Right standing with God","Justice — doing right by the vulnerable (widows, orphans, sojourners)","Covenant faithfulness — keeping one's covenant obligations","Right relationship — with God, with others","Acquittal in legal context","The fruit of faith (Gen 15:6 — 'reckoned to him as righteousness')"],"visual":"A plumb line — straight when it should be straight. A scale — balanced when goods are exchanged. A path — straight when it leads where it should. NOT 'sinless perfection' but 'rightly oriented.'","ane":"ANE kings boasted of their tsedaqah — establishing justice for the poor was a king's duty. YHWH the Great King fulfills this perfectly; human kings under Him share this calling.","kingdom":"Tsedaqah is the moral fabric of God's kingdom. A king is JUDGED by his tsedaqah — does he establish right relationships? Does he protect the vulnerable? Christ the King does this perfectly and gives His tsedaqah to His people (2 Cor 5:21).","greek":"LXX: δικαιοσύνη (dikaiosynē). NT centerpiece of Pauline soteriology (Rom 1:17, 3:21-26).","cross":"Genesis 15:6, 18:19; Deuteronomy 6:25; Psalm 15:2, 24:5, 72:1-7; Isaiah 1:17, 51:5-6; Amos 5:24; Romans 4:3-5; 2 Corinthians 5:21","_alias_of":"righteousness","plain":"Being in right relationship with God and others — faithfulness, not flawless perfection."},"tsaddiq":{"hebrew":"צַדִּיק","translit":"tsaddiq","strongs":"H6662","def":"Just, righteous. Right standing with God. Used of Noah (6:9).","_alias_of":"righteous","plain":"Living rightly and faithfully before God. Used of Noah."},"yirah":{"hebrew":"יִרְאָה","translit":"yirah","strongs":"H3374","def":"Fear, reverence, awe. The 'fear of the Lord' = covenant reverence.","cross":"Proverbs 1:7","_alias_of":"fear","plain":"Either being scared, or deep awe and respect (the right kind, toward God)."},"chokmah":{"hebrew":"חָכְמָה","translit":"chokmah","strongs":"H2451","def":"Wisdom, skill, practical insight.","_alias_of":"wisdom","plain":"Skill at living well — knowing how to apply truth to real life."},"basar":{"hebrew":"בָּשָׂר","translit":"basar","strongs":"H1320","def":"Flesh, meat, body. 'They two shall be one flesh' (Gen 2:24) — total covenant union.","_alias_of":"flesh","plain":"The body or human nature. In the New Testament it can mean our sinful tendencies."},"etsem":{"hebrew":"עֶצֶם","translit":"etsem","strongs":"H6106","def":"Bone, body, substance. 'Bone of my bones' (Gen 2:23) = covenant kinship language.","_alias_of":"bone","plain":"Bone. 'Bone of my bones' means deep, family-close kinship."},"dam":{"hebrew":"דָּם","translit":"dam","strongs":"H1818","def":"Blood. Symbol of life. 'The life is in the blood' (Lev 17:11).","cross":"Genesis 4:10","_alias_of":"blood","plain":"Blood — the Bible's sign of life, and of life given in death."},"nachash":{"hebrew":"נָחָשׁ","translit":"nachash","strongs":"H5175","def":"Serpent. Same root as 'shining one' or 'enchanter.' Identified with Satan in Rev 12:9.","ane":"In ANE iconography, serpents represented chaos and primordial enemies of order.","_alias_of":"serpent","plain":"A snake. In Genesis, the creature the deceiver used; later linked to Satan."},"gan":{"hebrew":"גַּן","translit":"gan","strongs":"H1588","def":"Garden, enclosed/protected place. LXX: παράδεισος (paradise) — same word the Persians used for royal walled gardens.","ane":"Royal gardens in ANE were sacred space — extensions of the king's palace. Eden = YHWH's royal garden, given to humanity.","_alias_of":"garden","plain":"An enclosed, cared-for place full of life and abundance."},"eden":{"hebrew":"עֵדֶן","translit":"ʿEden","strongs":"H5731","root":"From a verb meaning 'to be soft, delicate, pleasant.' Cognate with Sumerian edin (plain, fertile land) and Akkadian edinu.","senses":["Delight, pleasure, luxury — the primary meaning of the word itself","Geographic place — 'eastward in Eden' (Gen 2:8); located near four rivers (Pishon, Gihon, Tigris, Euphrates)","Sacred / divine space — 'the garden of God' (Ezek 28:13)","The mountain of God — 'thou wast upon the holy mountain of God' (Ezek 28:14)","Source — 'a river went out of Eden to water the garden' (Gen 2:10)","God's manifest presence — 'they heard the voice of YHWH God walking in the garden' (Gen 3:8)","Royal estate / pleasure-garden of the King — LXX renders as παράδεισος (paradise), the Persian pairi-daeza ('walled-around')"],"visual":"A walled, fertile, abundant garden on a HIGH place — Ezekiel calls it 'the holy mountain of God.' Four rivers flowing OUT of it (it is the source, not just a recipient). Lush trees, including the central Tree of Life and Tree of the Knowledge of Good and Evil. Animals living peacefully. A walking-place where YHWH Himself appeared at 'the cool of the day' (Gen 3:8). Walled and guarded by cherubim after the Fall (Gen 3:24). NOT a primitive jungle — a cultivated, ordered, royal estate.","ane":"In ANE thought, gods had heavenly estates and earthly sanctuaries that mirrored them. Mesopotamian temples were built on raised platforms (ziggurats) to imitate the divine mountain. Egyptian temples had sacred gardens. Eden is the PROTOTYPE: the garden where heaven and earth meet, where God walks with humanity, and from which divine order flows out into the world.","kingdom":"Eden was the original THRONE ROOM. Adam was placed there as priest-king. The Hebrew verbs 'to dress and keep' (avad and shamar) are the same words later used of Levitical priests serving in the tabernacle (Num 3:7-8). The kingdom mandate (Gen 1:28 — be fruitful, multiply, fill, subdue, have dominion) was EDEN EXPANDING. Humanity was to carry God's manifest presence outward until ALL the earth became Eden. After the Fall this mission was disrupted, not cancelled. The Tabernacle, then the Temple, then Christ, then the Church are stages of Eden being re-established. Revelation 22 shows it complete: river of life, tree of life, no more curse, God's throne with humanity dwelling in His presence forever.","theology":"Eden is not merely a past geographic location — it encodes humanity's TRUE VOCATION: to live in God's manifest presence, to bear His image, to extend His rule. The word means 'delight' because life with God IS delight. Sin exiles us from delight; redemption is the journey BACK. Genesis opens in Eden; Revelation closes with Eden restored. The whole biblical story is exile-and-return.","greek":"LXX: παράδεισος (paradeisos) — 'paradise.' Same word Jesus speaks to the thief on the cross: 'today shalt thou be with me in paradise' (Luke 23:43). Same word Paul uses for being caught up to 'paradise' (2 Cor 12:4). Revelation 2:7: 'the tree of life, which is in the midst of the paradise of God.'","cross":"Genesis 2-3; Isaiah 51:3 (Zion as Eden restored); Ezekiel 28:13-14, 31:8-9, 36:35; Joel 2:3; Luke 23:43; 2 Corinthians 12:4; Revelation 2:7, 22:1-5","_alias_of":"Eden","plain":"The lush garden-home God made for the first humans — a place of perfect abundance."},"ets":{"hebrew":"עֵץ","translit":"ets","strongs":"H6086","root":"Tree, wood — both the living plant and the cut material.","senses":["Tree (living)","Wood, timber, lumber","Stick, staff","Wooden object (cross, gallows)"],"visual":"A standing living tree — but also the wood it becomes when cut. The duality matters: the Tree of Life in Eden (living) and the tree on which Christ hung at Calvary (dead) are the same word in Hebrew (and the LXX uses ξύλον for both). The story comes full circle from Eden's tree to Calvary's tree.","ane":"ANE iconography: sacred trees in temple complexes represented divine wisdom and life. Asherah poles were sacred trees co-opted into Canaanite cult. Genesis takes the symbol and makes it about COMMUNION with YHWH.","kingdom":"Eden's two trees are the King's choice given to humanity: Life with Him, or autonomous knowledge of good and evil. Adam chose autonomy. Christ on the tree at Calvary undoes Adam's choice — His death on a tree opens the way back to the Tree of Life (Rev 22).","greek":"LXX: ξύλον (xylon) for both 'tree' and 'wood.' Same word used in NT for the cross (Acts 5:30, 1 Peter 2:24 — 'his own self bare our sins in his own body on the tree/xylon').","cross":"Genesis 2:9, 3:6; Deuteronomy 21:23 ('cursed is everyone that hangeth on a tree'); Galatians 3:13; 1 Peter 2:24; Revelation 22:2, 14","_alias_of":"tree","plain":"A tree. In Eden, two mattered: the Tree of Life and the tree of knowing good and evil."},"shamar":{"hebrew":"שָׁמַר","translit":"shamar","strongs":"H8104","def":"To keep, watch, guard. Adam was to 'keep' (shamar) the garden — same word later used for guarding the temple.","ane":"Eden = original temple. Adam = original priest-king.","_alias_of":"keep","plain":"To guard, watch over, and protect."},"avad":{"hebrew":"עָבַד","translit":"avad","strongs":"H5647","def":"To work, serve, cultivate, worship. Same word for 'work' the garden AND 'serve' YHWH.","ane":"Working the garden = worship. Adam's labor was liturgical.","_alias_of":"till","plain":"To work and tend the ground (the same word also means to serve God)."},"zera":{"hebrew":"זֶרַע","translit":"zera","strongs":"H2233","def":"Seed, offspring, descendants. Singular form used in Gen 3:15, 22:18 — Paul reads as Messianic singular.","cross":"Galatians 3:16","_alias_of":"seed","plain":"Offspring and descendants — the future carried in a single seed."},"qol":{"hebrew":"קוֹל","translit":"qol","strongs":"H6963","def":"Voice, sound. 'They heard the voice of YHWH walking in the garden' (Gen 3:8).","_alias_of":"voice","plain":"A voice or sound — the speaker made audible."},"davar":{"hebrew":"דָּבָר","translit":"davar","strongs":"H1697","def":"Word, matter, thing. God's word ENACTS — speech and event are unified.","_alias_of":"word","plain":"A spoken word. In the Bible, God's word actually makes things happen."},"halach":{"hebrew":"הָלַךְ","translit":"halach","strongs":"H1980","def":"To walk. Used metaphorically for life-pattern. 'Enoch walked with God' (5:24) = covenant intimacy.","_alias_of":"walk","plain":"In the Bible, how you 'walk' means how you live."},"yam":{"hebrew":"יָם","translit":"yam","strongs":"H3220","def":"Sea. In ANE myth, often a chaos-god. Genesis demythologizes.","ane":"Yam is the chaos-god in Ugaritic myth. Genesis makes him a created object.","_alias_of":"sea","plain":"The sea. Other nations feared it as a chaos-god; Genesis says God made it."},"shamayim":{"hebrew":"שָׁמַיִם","translit":"shamayim","strongs":"H8064","def":"Heaven(s). Grammatically dual. Sky AND the unseen dwelling of God.","_alias_of":"heaven","plain":"Where God dwells — and also the sky above."},"eretz":{"hebrew":"אֶרֶץ","translit":"eretz","strongs":"H776","def":"Land, ground, world.","_alias_of":"earth","plain":"The land and world we live on."},"tehom":{"hebrew":"תְהוֹם","translit":"tehom","strongs":"H8415","def":"Primal sea, abyss. Cognate with Babylonian Tiamat — but in Genesis just water under God's command.","ane":"In Babylonian Enuma Elish, Tiamat is a chaos-monster god killed by Marduk. Genesis demythologizes.","_alias_of":"deep","plain":"The deep waters / ocean. In Genesis, just water under God's control."},"or":{"hebrew":"אוֹר","translit":"or","strongs":"H216","def":"Brightness, illumination. Metaphor for truth, life, knowledge of God.","cross":"John 8:12","_alias_of":"light","plain":"Brightness. Also a picture of truth and life."},"choshech":{"hebrew":"חֹשֶׁךְ","translit":"choshech","strongs":"H2822","def":"Darkness — physical and spiritual absence of light.","cross":"1 John 1:5","_alias_of":"darkness","plain":"The absence of light — physical or spiritual."},"yom":{"hebrew":"יוֹם","translit":"yom","strongs":"H3117","def":"Day. Used in Genesis 1 for creation days.","_alias_of":"day","plain":"A day. In Genesis 1 it marks each stage of creation."},"mabbul":{"hebrew":"מַבּוּל","translit":"mabbul","strongs":"H3999","def":"Flood, deluge. Used only of the Genesis flood — a unique world-undoing event.","_alias_of":"flood","plain":"The world-covering flood in Noah's day."},"qeshet":{"hebrew":"קֶשֶׁת","translit":"qeshet","strongs":"H7198","def":"Bow (also a war bow). Set in clouds as covenant sign — God's bow hung up.","ane":"In ANE warrior imagery, hanging up the bow = ending warfare. YHWH disarms toward humanity in covenant.","_alias_of":"rainbow","plain":"The bow in the clouds — God's sign He'd never flood the whole earth again."},"tevah":{"hebrew":"תֵּבָה","translit":"tevah","strongs":"H8392","def":"Box, chest, ark. Same word for Moses' basket. Vehicle of salvation through judgment.","_alias_of":"ark","plain":"Either Noah's giant boat, or the sacred chest holding God's covenant items."},"mizbeach":{"hebrew":"מִזְבֵּחַ","translit":"mizbeach","strongs":"H4196","def":"Place of sacrifice. From zabach (to slaughter).","_alias_of":"altar","plain":"A raised place where sacrifices are offered to God."},"zevach":{"hebrew":"זֶבַח","translit":"zevach","strongs":"H2077","def":"Slaughtered offering. Bloodshed for atonement.","_alias_of":"sacrifice","plain":"An offering to God — often an animal's life given in place of the sinner's."},"melek":{"hebrew":"מֶלֶךְ","translit":"melek","strongs":"H4428","def":"King, sovereign ruler. Root: to reign, possess as ruler.","ane":"YHWH is repeatedly called Melek (King) over all creation. Earthly kings are deputies, not autonomous.","cross":"Psalm 24:7-10","_alias_of":"king","plain":"A ruler with full authority over his people and land."},"mamlakah":{"hebrew":"מַמְלָכָה","translit":"mamlakah","strongs":"H4467","root":"From malak (to reign) — the realm where reigning happens.","senses":["Kingdom — domain of a king's rule","Royal dominion","Sovereignty","Reign as a quality (not just territory)","The kingdom of God / heaven — His rule manifest"],"visual":"Wherever the king's word goes and is obeyed, that is his kingdom. Boundaries on a map, but ALSO every human heart that submits to him. The kingdom of God in the OT is wherever YHWH is acknowledged and worshipped; in the NT it bursts into the world with the King Himself.","ane":"Every ANE people understood kingdom — they all had kings. Hebrew thought adds: ALL kingdoms exist under YHWH's true kingdom. Earthly empires rise and fall (Daniel 2, 7) — but His kingdom is everlasting.","kingdom":"The CENTRAL theme of Scripture. Eden was the original kingdom-on-earth. Babel was the first counter-kingdom. Israel was YHWH's kingdom-people. Christ proclaimed the kingdom at hand. The Church is the kingdom-now. Revelation shows the kingdom-fully-come.","greek":"LXX: βασιλεία (basileia). Jesus' core message: 'the kingdom (basileia) of God is at hand' (Mark 1:15).","cross":"Exodus 19:6; 1 Chronicles 29:11; Psalm 22:28, 145:11-13; Daniel 2:44, 7:14, 7:27; Matthew 4:17, 6:10, 6:33; Revelation 11:15","_alias_of":"kingdom","plain":"God's rule — wherever God is in charge and His will is done."},"malak":{"hebrew":"מָלַךְ","translit":"malak","strongs":"H4427","def":"To be king, exercise kingship. Active exercise of royal authority.","cross":"1 Samuel 8","_alias_of":"reign","plain":"To rule as king — to actively hold and use power."},"kisse":{"hebrew":"כִּסֵּא","translit":"kisse","strongs":"H3678","def":"Throne, seat (of honor or authority).","ane":"In ANE coronation rituals, sitting on the throne = formal assumption of royal authority. YHWH's throne is in heaven (Ps 11:4); Eden was the original throne-room of His vice-regents.","_alias_of":"throne","plain":"A king's royal seat — the center of his power."},"sar":{"hebrew":"שַׂר","translit":"sar","strongs":"H8269","def":"Prince, chief, captain, ruler. A leader under the king.","cross":"Genesis 17:6","_alias_of":"prince","plain":"A high official just below the king — second in command."},"mashal":{"hebrew":"מָשַׁל","translit":"mashal","strongs":"H4910","def":"To rule, have dominion, govern. Different from radah — emphasizes governance/wisdom rather than conquest.","cross":"Genesis 1:18; Proverbs 16:32","_alias_of":"rule","plain":"To govern and make decisions for others."},"vice-regent":{"translit":"(ANE concept, not a single Hebrew word)","senses":["A deputy who rules a territory in the name of the absent King","A representative who carries the King's full authority","A statue placed by a king to declare his rule"],"visual":"A satrap holding a king's seal — wearing his ring, speaking his words","ane":"In Persian/Babylonian/Assyrian empires, vice-regents (satraps) governed provinces. They wore the king's seal, spoke with the king's voice, and were treated as the king himself when on duty. Their authority was DELEGATED — not autonomous.","kingdom":"Genesis 1:26-28 makes humanity collectively YHWH's vice-regent over the whole earth. We are not autonomous — we exercise HIS authority. Our power is delegated. The throne is His; our hands work it.","theology":"When humans rebel (Gen 3) we are NOT replacing the King — we are betraying the office. Christ comes as the TRUE vice-regent (the second Adam) who exercises God's rule perfectly.","cross":"Genesis 1:26-28; Psalm 8:6; Hebrews 2:6-9; 1 Corinthians 15:45","plain":"Someone who rules on behalf of the true king, carrying his authority."},"salvation":{"meaning":"Deliverance, rescue, healing, wholeness. Not just 'going to heaven' — the Bible's word covers every kind of rescue: from enemies, from sickness, from sin, from death, from yourself.","hebrew":"yeshuah (יְשׁוּעָה) — 'rescue, deliverance.' Root: yasha, 'to save, to deliver.'","greek":"soteria (σωτηρία) — 'rescue, wholeness, healing.' Where we get 'soteriology' — the study of salvation.","kingdom":"Jesus' name (Yeshua) literally MEANS 'YHWH saves.' Matthew 1:21: 'Thou shalt call his name JESUS: for he shall save his people from their sins.' The whole Bible storyline is one long rescue operation.","plain":"Being rescued and made whole — from danger, sickness, sin, and death."},"grace":{"meaning":"Unearned favor. Getting good things you didn't work for and don't deserve. The opposite of payment.","hebrew":"chen (חֵן) — 'favor, charm, grace.' Noah 'found grace in the eyes of the LORD' (Gen 6:8).","greek":"charis (χάρις) — 'unearned favor.' Where we get 'charity' and 'charisma.'","kingdom":"Ephesians 2:8-9: 'By grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast.' Grace is the engine of the gospel.","plain":"Getting good you didn't earn and don't deserve — God's free favor."},"glory":{"meaning":"Heavy weight of importance; visible splendor; the manifest presence of God. When God's glory shows up, it's substantial — like fire, like a cloud, like a face that shines.","hebrew":"kavod (כָּבוֹד) — literally 'weight, heaviness.' God's glory is His weighty significance made visible.","greek":"doxa (δόξα) — 'reputation, splendor.' Where 'doxology' comes from.","kingdom":"Isaiah 6 — Isaiah sees God's glory and falls. John 1:14 — 'we beheld his glory, the glory as of the only begotten of the Father.' Glory is the visible reality of God's presence.","plain":"God's weighty importance and visible splendor — His presence on display."},"holy":{"meaning":"Set apart, separated for a special purpose. Not just 'good' — DIFFERENT. Other. The temple was holy not because it was nice but because it was reserved.","hebrew":"qadosh (קָדוֹשׁ) — 'set apart, sacred.' Root qadash, 'to be separate.'","greek":"hagios (ἅγιος) — 'set apart, sacred.' Where 'hagiography' (saint-writing) comes from.","kingdom":"Leviticus 11:44 — 'Be ye holy; for I am holy.' Peter quotes this for the church (1 Pet 1:16). To be the LORD's is to be set apart.","plain":"Set apart and different — dedicated to God for a special purpose."},"repentance":{"meaning":"Turning around. Changing direction. Not just feeling sorry — actually facing the other way and walking that way.","hebrew":"shuv (שׁוּב) — 'to turn, to return.' Used 1,000+ times in the OT.","greek":"metanoia (μετάνοια) — 'change of mind.' But the OT background means change of LIFE, not just thought.","kingdom":"John the Baptist's first sermon: 'Repent ye: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand' (Matt 3:2). Jesus' first sermon: same words (Matt 4:17). Repentance is the gateway to the Kingdom.","plain":"Turning around — changing direction and walking God's way."},"redemption":{"meaning":"Buying back. Paying the price to free someone from slavery or debt. The redeemer is the one who pays.","hebrew":"ga'al (גָּאַל) — kinsman-redeemer (think Boaz buying Ruth). Also padah (to ransom).","greek":"lutroo (λυτρόω) — 'to redeem, to ransom.' From lutron, 'ransom price.'","kingdom":"1 Peter 1:18-19 — 'ye were not redeemed with corruptible things, as silver and gold... But with the precious blood of Christ.' Jesus is the Kinsman-Redeemer.","plain":"Buying someone back — paying the price to set them free."},"atonement":{"meaning":"AT-ONE-MENT. The covering of sin so God and humans can be united again. The bridge across the broken relationship.","hebrew":"kippur (כִּפֻּר) — 'covering, atonement.' Yom Kippur = Day of Atonement. Root kaphar: to cover.","greek":"hilasmos (ἱλασμός) — 'propitiation, atoning sacrifice.'","kingdom":"Leviticus 17:11 — 'the life of the flesh is in the blood... it is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul.' Christ's blood atones (Rom 3:25, 1 John 2:2).","plain":"Making things right again — covering sin so God and people can be 'at one.'"},"justification":{"meaning":"Being declared righteous. Not made righteous (sanctification does that) but DECLARED so — a legal verdict.","hebrew":"tsadaq (צָדַק) hiphil form — 'to declare righteous.'","greek":"dikaioo (δικαιόω) — same root as righteousness. A courtroom verdict.","kingdom":"Romans 5:1 — 'Being justified by faith, we have peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ.' The judge has banged the gavel: NOT GUILTY.","plain":"Being officially declared 'in the right' before God — a legal verdict."},"sanctification":{"meaning":"The lifelong process of being made holy. Justification is instant (God's verdict); sanctification is the slow work of becoming who you already are in Christ.","hebrew":"qadash (קָדַשׁ) — 'to set apart, to make holy.' Same root as holy.","greek":"hagiazo (ἁγιάζω) — 'to sanctify, make holy.'","kingdom":"1 Thessalonians 4:3 — 'This is the will of God, even your sanctification.' Christians are 'saints' (hagioi) — already set apart — yet being progressively sanctified.","plain":"The lifelong process of being made holy — becoming who God already declared you to be."},"propitiation":{"meaning":"An offering that turns away wrath. The thing that satisfies justice so the offender can be received.","hebrew":"kapporet (כַּפֹּרֶת) — the mercy seat on the Ark of the Covenant. The blood-sprinkled lid.","greek":"hilasterion (ἱλαστήριον) — same as the Hebrew kapporet.","kingdom":"Romans 3:25 — God set forth Christ 'to be a propitiation through faith in his blood.' 1 John 2:2 — 'he is the propitiation for our sins.' Christ IS the mercy seat.","plain":"An offering that satisfies justice and turns away anger, so you can be welcomed."},"saint":{"meaning":"A 'holy one' — set apart by God. EVERY Christian is a saint in NT use. Not a special elite.","greek":"hagios (ἅγιος) — 'set apart one.' Plural: hagioi.","kingdom":"Paul addresses ALL the believers at Rome, Corinth, Ephesus, Philippi as 'saints.' Sainthood is not earned — it's given the moment you're in Christ.","plain":"A 'holy one' set apart by God — which means every believer, not an elite few."},"disciple":{"meaning":"A learner. An apprentice. Not just a student of facts but a follower learning a way of life.","greek":"mathetes (μαθητής) — 'learner.' From manthano (to learn).","kingdom":"Jesus' Great Commission: 'Go ye therefore, and teach (literally: make disciples of) all nations' (Matt 28:19). The Kingdom advances by reproducing learners.","plain":"A follower and apprentice — learning a whole way of life, not just facts."},"apostle":{"meaning":"A 'sent one.' An ambassador with authority from the sender.","greek":"apostolos (ἀπόστολος) — 'one sent forth, ambassador.'","kingdom":"Jesus chose twelve apostles. Paul added later (1 Cor 15:8). They had unique authority because they had SEEN the risen Lord and were SENT directly by Him.","plain":"A 'sent one' — an ambassador carrying the sender's authority."},"evangelist":{"meaning":"A 'gospel-announcer.' Someone who brings the good news.","greek":"euangelistes (εὐαγγελιστής) — from euangelion ('good news').","kingdom":"Ephesians 4:11 lists 'evangelists' among Christ's gifts to the church. The four Gospel writers are also called evangelists.","plain":"Someone who announces the good news."},"elder":{"meaning":"A mature leader in the local church. Not just an old person — a person of proven character.","greek":"presbyteros (πρεσβύτερος) — 'older one, leader.' Where 'Presbyterian' comes from.","kingdom":"1 Timothy 3 and Titus 1 list qualifications — character, family management, knowing the faith. The Bible's leadership model is mature character, not age alone.","plain":"A mature, proven leader in the church."},"deacon":{"meaning":"A 'servant.' Practical helper. The first deacons were appointed to serve widows (Acts 6).","greek":"diakonos (διάκονος) — 'servant, minister.' Same word Jesus uses of himself.","kingdom":"Acts 6 institutes the role. Phoebe is called a 'deacon' in Romans 16:1 — women served in this office from the start.","plain":"A servant-leader who handles practical care in the church."},"prophet":{"meaning":"A spokesperson for God. Not primarily a fortune-teller — primarily a truth-teller who speaks God's word into a specific moment.","hebrew":"navi (נָבִיא) — 'spokesman, mouthpiece.'","greek":"prophetes (προφήτης) — 'one who speaks forth, one who speaks for another.'","kingdom":"Deuteronomy 18:15 — 'The LORD thy God will raise up unto thee a Prophet from the midst of thee, of thy brethren, like unto me' (Moses speaking about the Messiah).","plain":"A spokesperson for God — speaking His truth into a moment."},"priest":{"meaning":"A mediator between God and people. Offers sacrifices, prays for the people, teaches the law.","hebrew":"kohen (כֹּהֵן) — 'priest.' Aaron's line.","greek":"hiereus (ἱερεύς) — 'priest.'","kingdom":"Hebrews 4:14-16 — Jesus is our 'great high priest.' 1 Peter 2:9 — believers are 'a royal priesthood.' Every Christian has priestly access to God.","plain":"A go-between for God and people — offering sacrifices and praying for them."},"shepherd":{"meaning":"Pastor. The one who feeds, leads, protects, and tends a flock.","hebrew":"ra'ah (רָעָה) — 'to feed, to tend.'","greek":"poimen (ποιμήν) — 'shepherd, pastor.' Where 'pastor' comes from.","kingdom":"Psalm 23 — 'The LORD is my shepherd.' John 10:11 — Jesus the Good Shepherd. 1 Peter 5:4 — 'the chief Shepherd.'","plain":"A pastor — one who feeds, leads, and protects the flock."},"stranger":{"meaning":"Foreigner, sojourner, immigrant. The Bible's word for the outsider — and one of God's most-protected categories.","hebrew":"ger (גֵּר) — 'sojourner, resident alien.'","kingdom":"Leviticus 19:34 — 'But the stranger that dwelleth with you shall be unto you as one born among you, and thou shalt love him as thyself; for ye were strangers in the land of Egypt.' God's people remember they too were once outsiders.","plain":"A foreigner or immigrant — someone God specially commands His people to protect."},"widow":{"meaning":"Woman whose husband has died. In ANE, often left vulnerable. The Bible repeatedly places widows under God's special protection.","hebrew":"almanah (אַלְמָנָה).","kingdom":"James 1:27 — 'Pure religion and undefiled before God and the Father is this, To visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction.'","plain":"A woman whose husband has died — under God's special protection."},"orphan":{"meaning":"Child without a father — in ANE this meant economic and social vulnerability.","hebrew":"yatom (יָתוֹם) — 'fatherless.'","kingdom":"Psalm 68:5 — 'A father of the fatherless... is God in his holy habitation.' God himself takes up the role of father to the orphan.","plain":"A child without a father — vulnerable, and specially cared for by God."},"gentile":{"meaning":"Non-Jew. The 'nations' (plural) — anyone who's not part of God's covenant people Israel.","hebrew":"goyim (גּוֹיִם) — 'nations, peoples.'","greek":"ethnos (ἔθνος) — 'nation, gentile.' Plural ethne.","kingdom":"Acts 10 — Cornelius is the first major Gentile to receive the gospel. Galatians 3:28 — 'There is neither Jew nor Greek... ye are all one in Christ Jesus.' The Kingdom dissolves the wall.","plain":"Anyone who is not Jewish — the 'nations' outside Israel."},"remnant":{"meaning":"The surviving minority. The faithful few who endure when most fall away.","hebrew":"she'erit (שְׁאֵרִית) — 'remainder, what is left.'","kingdom":"Isaiah 10:21 — 'The remnant shall return.' Romans 9:27 — Paul cites this for the church. God always preserves a faithful remnant.","plain":"The faithful few who remain when most fall away."},"Father":{"meaning":"God as Father — not just creator but covenant-bound parent who loves, disciplines, provides.","hebrew":"Av (אָב); Avinu ('our Father').","greek":"Pater (πατήρ). Aramaic: Abba — daddy, intimate father.","kingdom":"Jesus called God 'Abba' (Mark 14:36) — the first-century equivalent of 'Daddy.' Paul says we cry 'Abba Father' through the Spirit (Rom 8:15).","plain":"God as a loving parent who provides, protects, and disciplines."},"Son":{"meaning":"Title of Jesus Christ. 'Son of God' means equal in nature with the Father; 'Son of Man' means truly human, and references Daniel 7's exalted heavenly figure.","hebrew":"Ben (בֵּן).","greek":"Huios (υἱός).","kingdom":"Mark 1:11 — at his baptism: 'Thou art my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.' The Father's audible affirmation at the start of Jesus' ministry.","plain":"A title of Jesus — fully God ('Son of God') and fully human ('Son of Man')."},"Christ":{"meaning":"Title (not a name). Hebrew Mashiach → Greek Christos → English Christ. Means 'anointed one' — the promised king-priest-prophet.","hebrew":"Mashiach (מָשִׁיחַ) — 'anointed.' Where 'Messiah' comes from.","greek":"Christos (Χριστός) — 'anointed one.'","kingdom":"Peter's confession: 'Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God' (Matt 16:16). Jesus is THE long-promised Anointed King.","plain":"A title meaning 'the Anointed One' — the promised king, priest, and prophet."},"Messiah":{"meaning":"Same word as Christ — Hebrew for 'anointed one.' Used of kings, priests, and prophets in the OT; specifically the promised Coming One.","hebrew":"Mashiach (מָשִׁיחַ).","kingdom":"John 1:41 — 'We have found the Messias, which is, being interpreted, the Christ.' Andrew's first word about Jesus.","plain":"The Hebrew word for 'Anointed One' — the promised Coming One. Same as Christ."},"Saviour":{"meaning":"Rescuer, deliverer. One who pulls you out of danger.","hebrew":"moshia (מוֹשִׁיעַ) — 'rescuer.'","greek":"soter (σωτήρ) — 'rescuer, savior.'","kingdom":"Luke 2:11 — 'For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord.' Angels announce it.","plain":"The Rescuer — the one who pulls you out of danger."},"Redeemer":{"meaning":"One who buys back. Pays the price to free the captive.","hebrew":"go'el (גֹּאֵל) — 'kinsman-redeemer.' Boaz to Ruth.","kingdom":"Job 19:25 — 'I know that my redeemer liveth.' Isaiah 41:14 — 'thy redeemer, the Holy One of Israel.' Jesus IS this Redeemer.","plain":"The one who buys you back and sets you free."},"Lamb":{"meaning":"Sacrificial lamb. Used 30+ times in Revelation as title of Christ.","hebrew":"kebes (כֶּבֶשׂ) — lamb of sacrifice.","greek":"arnion (ἀρνίον) — little lamb. amnos — lamb.","kingdom":"John 1:29 — 'Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world.' Revelation 5:6 — 'a Lamb as it had been slain.' Christ is the Passover Lamb.","plain":"A title for Christ — the sacrifice given in our place."},"Emmanuel":{"meaning":"'GOD WITH US.' One of the most loaded Hebrew names in scripture.","hebrew":"Immanu-el (עִמָּנוּ אֵל) — 'with us is God.'","kingdom":"Isaiah 7:14 — 'a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel.' Matthew 1:23 — fulfilled in Jesus.","plain":"A name meaning 'God with us.'"},"Almighty":{"meaning":"God as all-powerful. The unconquerable one.","hebrew":"El Shaddai (אֵל שַׁדַּי) — possibly 'God of the mountain' or 'God all-sufficient.' Used 48 times in OT.","greek":"pantokrator (παντοκράτωρ) — 'all-ruler.'","kingdom":"Genesis 17:1 — 'I am the Almighty God; walk before me, and be thou perfect.' Revelation 1:8 — 'Lord God Almighty, which was, and is, and is to come.'","plain":"God as all-powerful — the one no one can overcome."},"gospel":{"meaning":"GOOD NEWS. Originally an imperial term — announcement of a new emperor's birth or military victory. Christianity steals the word.","greek":"euangelion (εὐαγγέλιον) — 'good news.' Where 'evangel' and 'evangelism' come from.","kingdom":"Mark 1:14-15 — Jesus came preaching 'the gospel of the kingdom of God.' The good news is that the King has come.","plain":"'Good news' — the announcement of what God has done in Jesus."},"hope":{"meaning":"Confident expectation. Not 'wishing' — solid anticipation based on God's promises.","greek":"elpis (ἐλπίς) — 'confident expectation.'","kingdom":"1 Thessalonians 4:13 — Christians grieve, but 'not as others which have no hope.' We have a hope that survives death.","plain":"Confident expectation based on God's promises — not just wishful thinking."},"joy":{"meaning":"Deep gladness — independent of circumstances. Distinct from happiness (which depends on what's happening).","hebrew":"simchah (שִׂמְחָה).","greek":"chara (χαρά) — same root as charis (grace). Joy and grace are linguistically related.","kingdom":"Galatians 5:22 — joy is the second fruit of the Spirit. Nehemiah 8:10 — 'the joy of the LORD is your strength.'","plain":"Deep gladness that doesn't depend on your circumstances."},"patience":{"meaning":"Long-suffering. Endurance under stress. Not passive — actively choosing not to react.","greek":"makrothumia (μακροθυμία) — 'long-suffering, patience.' makros (long) + thumos (passion/anger). Slow to anger.","kingdom":"Galatians 5:22 — fruit of the Spirit. 2 Peter 3:9 — God 'is longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish.'","plain":"Staying steady and enduring under pressure without lashing out."},"church":{"meaning":"The 'called-out ones' — God's people. NOT primarily a building. The assembly of believers.","greek":"ekklesia (ἐκκλησία) — 'called-out assembly.' Where 'ecclesiastical' comes from.","kingdom":"Matthew 16:18 — Jesus' first use: 'I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.' Christ builds, not us.","plain":"God's 'called-out' people — the community of believers, not a building."},"baptism":{"meaning":"Immersion in water as public declaration of identification with Christ — his death, burial, and resurrection.","greek":"baptisma (βάπτισμα) — 'immersion.' From bapto, to dip.","kingdom":"Romans 6:3-4 — 'baptized into his death... that... we also should walk in newness of life.' Outward sign of inward reality.","plain":"Being immersed in water to publicly identify with Jesus' death and resurrection."},"communion":{"meaning":"The Lord's Supper. Sharing of bread and wine that Jesus instituted to remember his body and blood.","greek":"koinonia (κοινωνία) — 'fellowship, sharing.'","kingdom":"1 Corinthians 11:23-26 — 'this do in remembrance of me.' 'Ye do shew the Lord's death till he come.' Communion is both memorial and proclamation.","plain":"The Lord's Supper — bread and wine shared to remember Jesus' body and blood."},"prayer":{"meaning":"Communication with God. Not just asking — also listening, worshiping, confessing.","hebrew":"tefillah (תְּפִלָּה).","greek":"proseuche (προσευχή).","kingdom":"Jesus prayed often. 1 Thessalonians 5:17 — 'Pray without ceasing.' The most basic and powerful Christian practice.","plain":"Talking with God — asking, listening, worshiping, and confessing."},"fast":{"meaning":"Voluntary abstinence from food for spiritual purposes. Sharpens prayer and dependence on God.","hebrew":"tsom (צוֹם).","greek":"nesteia (νηστεία).","kingdom":"Jesus fasted 40 days (Matt 4:2). Acts 13:2 — leaders fasted before sending Paul on mission. Fasting is for breakthrough, direction, repentance.","plain":"Going without food for a time to focus on God and prayer."},"worship":{"meaning":"Ascribing worth. Bowing — physically and spiritually — to acknowledge God's supreme value.","hebrew":"shachah (שָׁחָה) — 'to bow down, prostrate.'","greek":"proskuneo (προσκυνέω) — 'to bow down, worship.' Literally 'to kiss toward.'","kingdom":"John 4:23-24 — 'true worshippers shall worship the Father in spirit and in truth.' Not just singing — life-orientation.","plain":"Honoring God's supreme worth — bowing to Him in heart and life."},"praise":{"meaning":"Speaking out the goodness of God. Public declaration of His worth.","hebrew":"halal (הָלַל) — 'to praise, to shine.' Where 'hallelujah' (praise YHWH) comes from. Also yadah, towdah, zamar.","kingdom":"Psalm 150 — 'let every thing that hath breath praise the LORD.' Praise is the universe's purpose.","plain":"Speaking out loud how good God is."},"tithe":{"meaning":"Tenth. Giving 10% of income/produce to God.","hebrew":"ma'aser (מַעֲשֵׂר) — 'tenth.'","kingdom":"Genesis 14:20 — Abraham tithes to Melchizedek (predates the law). Malachi 3:10 — 'bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse.' Jesus affirms tithing (Matt 23:23) but stresses justice/mercy/faith.","plain":"Giving a tenth of what you have back to God."},"incense":{"meaning":"Aromatic offering burned in the Temple. A picture of prayer rising to God.","hebrew":"qetoret (קְטֹרֶת).","kingdom":"Psalm 141:2 — 'Let my prayer be set forth before thee as incense.' Revelation 5:8 — golden bowls 'full of odours, which are the prayers of saints.'","plain":"Sweet-smelling smoke burned in worship — a picture of prayers rising to God."},"Satan":{"meaning":"The adversary. The Accuser. The chief enemy of God and humanity.","hebrew":"satan (שָׂטָן) — 'adversary, accuser.'","greek":"Satanas (Σατανᾶς); also diabolos (διάβολος, 'slanderer') = devil.","kingdom":"Revelation 12:9 — 'the great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world.' Defeated at the cross (Col 2:15).","plain":"The chief enemy of God and people — the Accuser."},"devil":{"meaning":"Slanderer, accuser. Same as Satan. The one who lies about God and accuses humans before God.","greek":"diabolos (διάβολος) — 'slanderer.' Where 'diabolical' comes from.","kingdom":"1 Peter 5:8 — 'your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour.' James 4:7 — 'resist the devil, and he will flee from you.'","plain":"The slanderer and accuser — another name for Satan."},"demon":{"meaning":"Evil spirit. Fallen angel. Servant of Satan.","greek":"daimon, daimonion (δαιμόνιον).","kingdom":"Jesus repeatedly cast out demons — a sign that the Kingdom of God had come (Matt 12:28). Believers have authority over demons through the name of Jesus.","plain":"An evil spirit that serves Satan."},"angel":{"meaning":"Messenger. Heavenly being who carries out God's missions.","hebrew":"malak (מַלְאָךְ) — 'messenger.'","greek":"angelos (ἄγγελος) — 'messenger.'","kingdom":"Angels announce Christ's birth (Luke 2), strengthen him in Gethsemane (Luke 22:43), announce his resurrection. Hebrews 1:14 — 'ministering spirits, sent forth to minister for them who shall be heirs of salvation.'","plain":"A messenger from heaven who carries out God's missions."},"law":{"meaning":"Instruction. The Torah is not primarily about rules — it's God's teaching for life.","hebrew":"Torah (תּוֹרָה) — 'instruction, teaching.' From yarah, 'to throw, to point' (like an arrow showing the direction).","greek":"nomos (νόμος) — 'law, custom.'","kingdom":"Psalm 1:2 — 'his delight is in the law of the LORD.' Jesus: 'I am not come to destroy [the law], but to fulfil' (Matt 5:17).","plain":"God's instruction for living — His teaching, more than just rules."},"commandment":{"meaning":"Direct instruction from God. Specific actionable guidance.","hebrew":"mitzvah (מִצְוָה) — 'commandment, instruction.' Plural mitzvot. Jewish tradition counts 613 in the Torah.","greek":"entole (ἐντολή).","kingdom":"John 14:15 — 'If ye love me, keep my commandments.' Love is shown by obedience.","plain":"A direct instruction from God."},"truth":{"meaning":"Reality as it actually is. In Hebrew, also faithfulness — what can be relied on.","hebrew":"emet (אֱמֶת) — 'firmness, truth, faithfulness.' Same root as faith.","greek":"aletheia (ἀλήθεια) — 'truth, reality.' Literally 'not hidden.'","kingdom":"John 14:6 — 'I am the way, the truth, and the life.' John 8:32 — 'the truth shall make you free.'","plain":"What is really real — and what can be fully relied on."},"fool":{"meaning":"Not someone of low intelligence — someone who lives as if there's no God. Morally and spiritually senseless.","hebrew":"nabal (נָבָל), kesil (כְּסִיל), evil (אֱוִיל) — three Hebrew words for kinds of foolishness.","greek":"moros (μωρός) — where 'moron' comes from.","kingdom":"Psalm 14:1 — 'The fool hath said in his heart, There is no God.' Foolishness is the practical denial of God.","plain":"Not someone unintelligent — someone who lives as if God isn't there."},"heart":{"meaning":"Center of the person — will, intellect, emotion, conscience. NOT primarily 'feelings' as in modern English.","hebrew":"lev (לֵב) or levav (לֵבָב) — center of decision.","greek":"kardia (καρδία).","kingdom":"Proverbs 4:23 — 'Keep thy heart with all diligence; for out of it are the issues of life.' Matthew 5:8 — 'Blessed are the pure in heart: for they shall see God.'","plain":"The control center of a person — will, thoughts, and emotions together."},"mind":{"meaning":"Thinking faculty. The reasoning self.","greek":"nous (νοῦς) — 'mind, understanding.' Also dianoia.","kingdom":"Romans 12:2 — 'be ye transformed by the renewing of your mind.' Discipleship rewires the way we think.","plain":"The thinking, reasoning part of you."},"hell":{"meaning":"Place of final judgment. Three biblical words behind English 'hell' — sheol (grave/underworld), hades (realm of dead), gehenna (final place of judgment).","hebrew":"sheol (שְׁאוֹל) — the grave, the realm of the dead.","greek":"hades (ᾅδης) — realm of the dead; gehenna — Valley of Hinnom, image of final judgment.","kingdom":"Jesus speaks of hell more than anyone in the Bible. Revelation 20:14 — 'the lake of fire. This is the second death.'","plain":"The place of final judgment after death."},"eternal":{"meaning":"Without end. Sometimes 'forever' or 'of the age to come.' Refers to the unending life of God and what He shares.","hebrew":"olam (עוֹלָם) — 'age, eternity, forever.'","greek":"aionios (αἰώνιος) — 'eternal, age-long.'","kingdom":"John 3:16 — 'whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting (aionios) life.'","plain":"Without end — the unending life God gives."},"everlasting":{"meaning":"Same as eternal — without end. The KJV's most common rendering of olam/aionios.","kingdom":"Isaiah 9:6 — 'The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace.' Daniel 12:2 — 'some to everlasting life.'","plain":"Without end — the same idea as eternal."},"resurrection":{"meaning":"Rising from the dead. The Christian hope is BODILY resurrection — not just spirits going to heaven.","greek":"anastasis (ἀνάστασις) — 'standing up again.'","kingdom":"1 Corinthians 15 is the resurrection chapter. Job 19:25-26 — 'in my flesh shall I see God.' Daniel 12:2. The hope is physical.","plain":"Rising from the dead — the Christian hope is a real, bodily one."},"Jerusalem":{"meaning":"The holy city. Capital of Israel under David. Site of Solomon's Temple. Where Jesus died and rose. Future site of the New Jerusalem.","hebrew":"Yerushalayim (יְרוּשָׁלַיִם) — 'foundation of peace' (one common etymology).","kingdom":"Psalm 122:6 — 'Pray for the peace of Jerusalem.' Revelation 21 — the New Jerusalem descends from heaven.","plain":"The holy city — capital of Israel, where Jesus died and rose."},"Zion":{"meaning":"The hill of Jerusalem where David built his palace and Solomon built the Temple. Becomes the symbol of God's chosen dwelling.","hebrew":"Tziyyon (צִיּוֹן).","kingdom":"Psalm 132:13 — 'the LORD hath chosen Zion; he hath desired it for his habitation.' Hebrews 12:22 — 'ye are come unto mount Sion.'","plain":"The hill of Jerusalem — a symbol of God's chosen dwelling place."},"Babylon":{"meaning":"Empire that destroyed Solomon's Temple and exiled Judah. In Revelation, symbol of the godless world system.","hebrew":"Bavel (בָּבֶל) — same as the Babel of Genesis 11.","kingdom":"Jeremiah 51 prophesies Babylon's fall. Revelation 18 — the fall of 'Babylon the great.' The original Babel of pride finds its final form, and its final fall.","plain":"The empire that destroyed the temple and exiled Judah; later a symbol of the godless world."},"Galilee":{"meaning":"Northern region of Israel. Jesus' home for ~30 years. Most of his ministry happened here.","hebrew":"Galil (גָּלִיל).","kingdom":"Matthew 4:15-16 — 'Galilee of the Gentiles; The people which sat in darkness saw great light.' Isaiah 9 fulfilled.","plain":"The northern region of Israel where Jesus grew up and did most of his ministry."},"Judea":{"meaning":"Southern region of Israel, around Jerusalem. The tribe of Judah's territory.","kingdom":"Bethlehem of Judea — birthplace of David and of Jesus (Mic 5:2, Matt 2:1).","plain":"The southern region of Israel, around Jerusalem."},"Samaria":{"meaning":"Central region between Galilee and Judea. After the Assyrian conquest, mixed-race population. Despised by Jews.","kingdom":"John 4 — Jesus deliberately meets the Samaritan woman. Acts 1:8 — the gospel goes to 'Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth.' Walls fall.","plain":"The central region of Israel, whose people Jews looked down on."},"forgive":{"meaning":"To cancel a debt. To release someone from what they owe. Both God forgiving us and us forgiving each other.","hebrew":"salach (סָלַח) — 'to forgive.' nasa (נָשָׂא) — 'to lift, bear away.'","greek":"aphiemi (ἀφίημι) — 'to send away, release.'","kingdom":"Matthew 6:14-15 — forgive others or your Father won't forgive you. Forgiveness flows.","plain":"To cancel what someone owes you and release them from it."},"deliver":{"meaning":"Rescue. Pull out of danger. Same root concept as 'save.'","hebrew":"natsal (נָצַל) — 'to snatch away.' palat (פָּלַט) — 'to escape, deliver.'","greek":"rhuomai (ῥύομαι).","kingdom":"The Exodus is THE deliverance event of the OT. The cross is THE deliverance event of the NT. Both shape the rest of scripture.","plain":"To rescue — to pull someone out of danger."},"anoint":{"meaning":"To pour oil on. Prophets, priests, and kings were anointed for their offices. 'Messiah/Christ' = the Anointed One.","hebrew":"mashach (מָשַׁח) — 'to anoint, smear.'","greek":"chrio (χρίω) — root of Christos.","kingdom":"1 Samuel 16:13 — Samuel anointed David. Isaiah 61:1 — 'The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me; because the LORD hath anointed me' — quoted by Jesus in Luke 4.","plain":"To pour oil on someone, setting them apart for a special role."},"consecrate":{"meaning":"To set apart for sacred use. To dedicate fully.","hebrew":"qadash (קָדַשׁ) — same root as holy.","kingdom":"Joshua 3:5 — 'Sanctify yourselves: for to morrow the LORD will do wonders among you.' Consecration before breakthrough.","plain":"To dedicate something fully to God for sacred use."},"cleanse":{"meaning":"To purify. To wash. In ritual sense: to make fit to approach God.","hebrew":"taher (טָהֵר) — 'to be clean, pure.'","greek":"katharizo (καθαρίζω) — where 'cathartic' comes from.","kingdom":"Psalm 51:7 — 'Wash me, and I shall be whiter than snow.' 1 John 1:9 — 'the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin.'","plain":"To wash and purify — to make fit to come near God."},"heal":{"meaning":"To make whole. Restore. Includes physical, emotional, and spiritual.","hebrew":"rapha (רָפָא) — 'to heal.' YHWH-Rapha — 'the LORD that healeth' (Exod 15:26).","greek":"iaomai (ἰάομαι), therapeuo (θεραπεύω).","kingdom":"Isaiah 53:5 — 'with his stripes we are healed.' Quoted in 1 Peter 2:24.","plain":"To make whole again — body, mind, or spirit."},"manna":{"meaning":"The miraculous bread God provided in the wilderness. 'What is it?' (Hebrew: man hu).","hebrew":"man (מָן) — 'what?'","kingdom":"John 6:32-35 — Jesus calls himself 'the true bread from heaven' — the manna prefigures Christ.","plain":"The miracle bread God provided in the wilderness. The name means 'what is it?'"},"tabernacle":{"meaning":"Tent of meeting. The portable sanctuary God commanded Moses to build for His presence.","hebrew":"mishkan (מִשְׁכָּן) — 'dwelling place.'","kingdom":"John 1:14 — 'the Word was made flesh, and dwelt (eskenosen — 'tabernacled') among us.' Jesus IS God's tabernacle.","plain":"The portable tent-sanctuary where God's presence dwelled with Israel."},"temple":{"meaning":"House of God. Solomon's, then Zerubbabel's/Herod's. Destroyed AD 70. Christians become the temple now.","hebrew":"beit ha-mikdash (בֵּית הַמִּקְדָּשׁ) — 'house of the holy.'","greek":"naos (ναός).","kingdom":"1 Corinthians 3:16 — 'ye are the temple of God.' 1 Corinthians 6:19 — 'your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost.'","plain":"God's house in Jerusalem. Now believers themselves are God's temple."},"sabbath":{"meaning":"Seventh day. Day of rest. Built into creation itself.","hebrew":"shabbat (שַׁבָּת) — 'cease, rest.'","kingdom":"Mark 2:27 — 'The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath.' Hebrews 4 — the Sabbath rest is fulfilled in Christ.","plain":"The seventh day — a day of rest built into creation itself."},"Passover":{"meaning":"Founding feast of Israel. Annual remembrance of the Exodus. The angel of death 'passed over' homes marked with blood.","hebrew":"Pesach (פֶּסַח) — 'passing over.'","kingdom":"1 Corinthians 5:7 — 'Christ our passover is sacrificed for us.' The Last Supper was a Passover meal.","plain":"Israel's founding feast, remembering when God rescued them from Egypt."},"Pentecost":{"meaning":"'Fiftieth' (day). Hebrew Shavuot. Celebrates the giving of the law at Sinai. Day the Spirit fell on the church.","greek":"Pentecoste (πεντηκοστή) — 'fiftieth.'","kingdom":"Acts 2 — the Spirit fell on the same day Israel commemorated receiving the Torah. Now the law is written on hearts.","plain":"A feast 50 days after Passover — the day God's Spirit filled the church."},"Pharisee":{"meaning":"Jewish religious party emphasizing strict obedience to the Torah and oral tradition.","hebrew":"perushim (פְּרוּשִׁים) — 'separated ones.'","kingdom":"Most of Jesus' criticism was aimed at Pharisees — but Paul WAS a Pharisee, and Pharisaism kept Judaism alive after AD 70. Nicodemus was a Pharisee.","plain":"A Jewish group focused on strict obedience to the law and tradition."},"Sadducee":{"meaning":"Jewish religious party of the priestly aristocracy. Rejected resurrection, angels, oral tradition.","kingdom":"Acts 23 — Paul exploits the Pharisee/Sadducee divide. Their party effectively died with the Temple in AD 70.","plain":"A Jewish priestly group that rejected resurrection and angels."},"Scribe":{"meaning":"Professional teacher/copyist of the Torah. Often associated with Pharisees.","hebrew":"sopher (סוֹפֵר) — 'one who counts/writes.'","greek":"grammateus (γραμματεύς) — 'letter-man.'","kingdom":"Ezra was a scribe. The scribes preserved scripture for centuries. Jesus said the trained scribe brings out treasures old and new (Matt 13:52).","plain":"A professional teacher and copyist of the law."},"publican":{"meaning":"Tax collector working for Rome. Hated by Jews as traitors and extortionists.","greek":"telones (τελώνης).","kingdom":"Matthew was a publican. Zacchaeus was a chief publican. Jesus chose them — and dined with them — to the religious leaders' fury.","plain":"A tax collector for Rome — despised as a traitor."},"Greek":{"meaning":"Non-Jew, especially Gentile of Hellenistic culture. In NT often used interchangeably with 'gentile.'","kingdom":"Romans 1:16 — 'to the Jew first, and also to the Greek.' Galatians 3:28 — 'There is neither Jew nor Greek.'","plain":"A non-Jew, especially of Greek culture — often used like 'Gentile.'"},"Jew":{"meaning":"From 'Judah.' Originally referred to the tribe of Judah, then the kingdom of Judah, then all Israelites after the exile.","kingdom":"Romans 11 — 'God hath not cast away his people which he foreknew.' God's covenant promises to the Jewish people remain.","plain":"A descendant of Judah/Israel — one of God's covenant people."},"leper":{"meaning":"Person with serious skin disease — often not what we now call leprosy but various conditions. Required ritual separation.","kingdom":"Jesus repeatedly TOUCHED lepers (unthinkable in his culture). Healing the leper was a messianic sign (Matt 11:5).","plain":"A person with a serious skin disease, required to live separately."},"seek":{"meaning":"To search for. To pursue. Biblical seeking is active, not passive.","hebrew":"darash (דָּרַשׁ), baqash (בָּקַשׁ).","greek":"zeteo (ζητέω).","kingdom":"Matthew 6:33 — 'seek ye first the kingdom of God.' Jeremiah 29:13 — 'ye shall seek me, and find me, when ye shall search for me with all your heart.'","plain":"To actively search for and pursue something."},"trust":{"meaning":"To rely on. To put your weight on. Same family of meaning as faith.","hebrew":"batach (בָּטַח) — 'to trust, to be confident.'","kingdom":"Proverbs 3:5 — 'Trust in the LORD with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding.' Isaiah 26:3 — 'Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee.'","plain":"To rely on and put your full weight on — close kin to faith."},"wait":{"meaning":"To expectantly anticipate. Not passive boredom — confident waiting.","hebrew":"qavah (קָוָה) — 'to wait, hope.'","kingdom":"Isaiah 40:31 — 'they that wait upon the LORD shall renew their strength.' Lamentations 3:25 — 'The LORD is good unto them that wait for him.'","plain":"To expect confidently — active anticipation, not idle boredom."},"abide":{"meaning":"To remain. To dwell. To stay continuously.","greek":"meno (μένω) — 'to stay, abide, remain.'","kingdom":"John 15:4-5 — 'Abide in me, and I in you. As the branch cannot bear fruit of itself... no more can ye, except ye abide in me.' Discipleship is remaining in Jesus.","plain":"To stay and remain — to keep dwelling somewhere."},"hear":{"meaning":"Listen — but in Hebrew thought, listening implies OBEYING. To hear is to act on.","hebrew":"shema (שָׁמַע) — 'hear, listen, obey.' Source of the great Shema prayer.","kingdom":"Deuteronomy 6:4 — 'Hear (Shema), O Israel: The LORD our God is one LORD.' Romans 10:17 — 'faith cometh by hearing.'","plain":"To listen — and in Hebrew, listening means obeying."},"stand":{"meaning":"To remain firm. In military and spiritual contexts: hold your ground.","kingdom":"Ephesians 6:13 — 'wherefore take unto you the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to withstand in the evil day, and having done all, to stand.'","plain":"To hold your ground and stay firm."},"run":{"meaning":"In NT, often used of the Christian life — running a race.","kingdom":"1 Corinthians 9:24 — 'so run, that ye may obtain' (the prize). Hebrews 12:1 — 'run with patience the race that is set before us.'","plain":"To run — often a picture of living the Christian life like a race."},"fight":{"meaning":"To struggle, contend, especially in spiritual battle.","kingdom":"1 Timothy 6:12 — 'Fight the good fight of faith.' 2 Timothy 4:7 — 'I have fought a good fight.'","plain":"To struggle and contend, especially in spiritual battle."},"overcome":{"meaning":"To conquer. To prevail over.","greek":"nikao (νικάω) — where 'Nike' (the Greek goddess of victory) comes from.","kingdom":"John 16:33 — 'I have overcome the world.' Revelation 21:7 — 'He that overcometh shall inherit all things.'","plain":"To conquer and win out over something."},"inherit":{"meaning":"To receive as a possession from a father. The Promised Land was Israel's inheritance.","hebrew":"nachal (נָחַל) — 'to inherit, possess.'","greek":"kleronomeo (κληρονομέω).","kingdom":"Galatians 4:7 — 'thou art... an heir of God through Christ.' Ephesians 1:14 — the Spirit is 'the earnest of our inheritance.'","plain":"To receive a possession handed down from a father."},"brothers":{"hebrew":"אַחִים","translit":"achim","strongs":"H251","plain":"More than one brother — or a close companion, ally, or fellow member of a group.","def":"Plural of ach (brother). Used literally of siblings (Joseph and his brothers) and figuratively of covenant kinship. Central to the Joseph narrative — the brothers who sold Joseph are the same ones Joseph saves.","kingdom":"The brothers of Joseph picture Israel's own story: rejection, suffering, and unexpected salvation through the one they abandoned. Redemption circles back to restore the broken family.","_note":"BSB uses 'brothers' throughout where KJV uses 'brethren.'"},"offspring":{"hebrew":"זֶרַע","translit":"zera","strongs":"H2233","plain":"Children, descendants, and the future line that carries the promise forward.","def":"Same Hebrew word as 'seed' (zera). BSB translates it as 'offspring' in most covenant promises. The entire story of Scripture traces whose offspring — whose seed-line — the Messiah will come through. Genesis 3:15 is the first prophecy: 'I will put enmity between you and the woman, and between your offspring and hers.'","kingdom":"The 'offspring' of the woman defeats the serpent. The 'offspring' of Abraham blesses all nations. The 'offspring' of David rules forever. Every generation of offspring is the next chapter of one long covenant promise.","cross":"Genesis 3:15; Genesis 12:7; Genesis 22:18; Galatians 3:16"},"descendants":{"hebrew":"זֶרַע","translit":"zera","strongs":"H2233","plain":"The people who come after — the next generations who carry the family name and covenant promise.","def":"Same as 'offspring' and 'seed' (zera). BSB uses 'descendants' for the generational lineage of covenant families. When God promises Abraham 'your descendants will be as numerous as the stars,' He is locking in a line of people who will carry the covenant forward.","kingdom":"Covenant lineage is not just biological — it is the conduit of promise. Every 'descendants' in Genesis is God tracking one thread through history toward the Messiah.","cross":"Genesis 13:16; Genesis 15:5; Genesis 22:17"},"household":{"hebrew":"בַּיִת","translit":"bayit","strongs":"H1004","plain":"Everyone under one roof — family, servants, and workers who belong to that household together.","def":"Bayit means house, but in the ancient world a household was far more than a building. It included your immediate family, extended relatives, servants, hired workers, and even guests under your protection. Abraham's 'household' was a small army (Gen 14:14). Joseph managed Pharaoh's entire household. The covenant included the whole household.","kingdom":"The household is the basic unit of God's covenant. When Cornelius's 'household' was saved (Acts 10), and when the Philippian jailer's 'household' was baptized (Acts 16), the household principle of Genesis was still in force.","cross":"Genesis 14:14; Genesis 39:4; Acts 10:2; Acts 16:31"},"livestock":{"hebrew":"מִקְנֶה","translit":"miqneh","strongs":"H4735","plain":"Animals on a farm or in a herd — cattle, sheep, goats. In the ancient world this WAS wealth.","def":"Miqneh comes from a root meaning 'to acquire/buy.' Livestock was the primary measure of wealth in the ancient Near East. Abraham was 'very rich in livestock' (Gen 13:2). The plagues of Egypt struck Egypt's livestock. The patriarchs measured their prosperity in flocks and herds.","ane":"In ancient Near Eastern culture, livestock was money, status, and military capacity. A man's herds told you his rank. Losing livestock to a neighbor was like losing stock value — it could shift power dynamics completely.","kingdom":"Livestock represent God's material blessing of the covenant families. When livestock multiply, it is a sign of God's favor. When they are struck (as in Egypt), it is covenant judgment."},"expanse":{"hebrew":"רָקִיעַ","translit":"raqia","strongs":"H7549","plain":"The vast open sky — the space God made between the waters above and below on Day Two of creation.","def":"Same Hebrew word as 'firmament' (raqia) — BSB translates it as 'expanse.' God stretched out a vast open space on Day Two (Gen 1:6-8), separating the waters above from the waters below. The expanse holds the sun, moon, and stars (Gen 1:14-17). The birds fly across it (Gen 1:20).","visual":"Picture a vast dome stretched across the earth — from horizon to horizon, holding the sky up. That is raqia — hammered-out space, a vaulted expanse.","ane":"Ancient Near Eastern peoples pictured the sky as a solid dome. Genesis uses the same picture but under God's authority — not a cosmic battle but orderly creation.","cross":"Genesis 1:6-8; Psalm 19:1; Ezekiel 1:22"},"ancestors":{"_alias_of":"fathers","plain":"The people who came before — your parents, grandparents, and the generations stretching back."},"being":{"hebrew":"נֶפֶשׁ","translit":"nephesh","strongs":"H5315","plain":"A living creature — a whole, breathing, alive person or animal. BSB often says 'living being' where KJV says 'living soul.'","def":"BSB translates nephesh as 'living being' in Genesis 2:7 — 'the man became a living being.' It means the whole person alive and breathing, not just an invisible soul floating inside a body. In Hebrew thought you don't HAVE a nephesh — you ARE one.","kingdom":"The resurrection hope of the Bible is not a soul escaping the body — it is a whole person, a living being, made whole again. Genesis 2:7 sets the foundation: God made a living being from dust plus His own breath.","cross":"Genesis 2:7; 1 Corinthians 15:45"},"forefathers":{"_alias_of":"fathers","plain":"The ancestors who came before — especially Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob."},"path":{"_alias_of":"way","plain":"A road or path — and how you live your life. The 'path' of the righteous vs. the path of destruction."},"face":{"hebrew":"פָּנִים","translit":"panim","strongs":"H6440","plain":"The face — and more than that: presence, surface, and front of something. 'The face of God' means His personal presence.","def":"Panim is always plural in Hebrew (literally 'faces'). It means face, but extends to presence, surface, and front. 'The Spirit of God hovered over the face of the waters' (Gen 1:2). 'Cain went out from the face of the LORD' (Gen 4:16 — he left God's presence). 'Face to face' means intimate, direct encounter.","visual":"When Jacob wrestled with God and survived, he named the place Peniel — 'face of God' — because he had seen God face to face. To seek God's face is to seek His personal presence, not just His power.","ane":"In the ancient Near East, to stand before a king's face meant access and favor. To be turned away or to leave someone's face meant rejection or exile. When Cain leaves 'the face of the LORD,' he is in exile from covenant presence.","kingdom":"The whole Bible is a story of humanity losing face-to-face access to God in Genesis 3 and gaining it back in Revelation 22: 'They will see His face.' The priestly blessing — 'The LORD make His face shine upon you' — is a prayer for God's welcoming presence.","cross":"Genesis 1:2; Genesis 4:16; Numbers 6:25; Revelation 22:4"},"hand":{"hebrew":"יָד","translit":"yad","strongs":"H3027","plain":"A hand — and in the Bible, a symbol of power, authority, and action. 'The hand of the LORD' means His active power.","def":"Yad means hand, but in biblical language it carries the weight of power and authority. 'Into your hand I have delivered them' = I have given you power over them. 'The hand of the LORD was heavy upon them' = God's power pressed down on them. The right hand is the place of honor and strength.","visual":"When Pharaoh gave Joseph his signet ring, he put it on Joseph's hand — transferring authority. When the Bible says God's hand did something, it means He personally and powerfully acted.","kingdom":"Christ sits at 'the right hand of God' — the position of supreme authority and honor. The hand that made the world (Psalm 95:5) is the same hand that rescues His people and holds them (Isaiah 41:10).","cross":"Genesis 41:42; Psalm 95:5; Isaiah 41:10; Acts 2:33"},"place":{"hebrew":"מָקוֹם","translit":"makom","strongs":"H4725","plain":"A location or spot — but in the Bible, often a special meeting place where heaven and earth connect.","def":"Makom is often just a location. But in Genesis it carries sacred weight: 'Jacob left Beersheba and set out for Haran. When he reached a certain place (makom), he stopped for the night...' That 'certain place' becomes Bethel — the House of God. Abraham arrives at 'the place' God showed him on Moriah. The makom is where God shows up.","visual":"An altar, a stone pillar, a well — the patriarchs mark the places where God met them. Place becomes sacred by encounter, not by human decoration.","kingdom":"God later chose a permanent makom — Jerusalem — for His name to dwell. The temple was the makom par excellence. Now, in Christ, believers are the place where God dwells.","cross":"Genesis 22:3-4; Genesis 28:11; Deuteronomy 12:5"},"dream":{"hebrew":"חֲלוֹם","translit":"chalom","strongs":"H2472","plain":"A dream — in the Bible, often a message from God delivered while someone is asleep.","def":"Chalom is used throughout Genesis as a primary channel of divine revelation. Jacob's ladder (Gen 28) is a dream. Joseph's two dreams (Gen 37) launch his entire story. The butler's and baker's dreams, Pharaoh's two dreams, and Joseph's interpretations drive the whole Egyptian narrative. God also speaks to Abimelech (Gen 20:3) and Laban (Gen 31:24) in dreams.","ane":"Dream interpretation was a major profession in ancient Egypt and Babylon. The biblical difference: Joseph insists interpretations belong to God (Gen 40:8), not to human skill. Dreams in the Bible authenticate God's messengers and reveal His plans.","kingdom":"Dreams in Genesis are God directing history — placing the right people in the right position to fulfill covenant promises. Joseph suffers unjustly because of his dreams and rises to power because of them. God's plan runs through the dreamer.","cross":"Genesis 28:12; Genesis 37:5-10; Genesis 40:5; Genesis 41:1; Joel 2:28; Acts 2:17"},"famine":{"hebrew":"רָעָב","translit":"ra'av","strongs":"H7458","plain":"A severe food shortage — no crops, no food. In Genesis, famines are often the events God uses to move His people.","def":"Ra'av means hunger and famine — the absence of food. Three major famines shape Genesis: the famine that drives Abram to Egypt (Gen 12:10), the famine in Isaac's day (Gen 26:1), and the seven-year famine that drives Jacob's sons to Egypt to buy grain from Joseph (Gen 41-47). Each famine is a divine hinge point.","kingdom":"The Joseph famine is no accident — it is the mechanism by which God fulfills His word to Abraham (Gen 15:13) that his descendants would be in Egypt. God uses shortage to position His people for rescue. The miracle is not that the famine didn't come — it is that Joseph was already there.","cross":"Genesis 12:10; Genesis 26:1; Genesis 41:27; Ruth 1:1"},"master":{"hebrew":"אָדוֹן","translit":"adon","strongs":"H113","plain":"A master, owner, or lord — someone with authority over others. The same root as 'Lord' (Adonai).","def":"Adon means master, owner, lord. It is the everyday word for someone in authority — Joseph becomes 'master' of Potiphar's household (Gen 39:2-3), then master of Egypt (Gen 45:9). It is also the root of Adonai (the divine title — 'my Lord'). So when a servant calls his earthly master 'adon,' and Israel calls God 'Adonai,' the same word is doing both jobs.","kingdom":"Every human master in Genesis is a limited picture of the true Adon — the LORD who owns everything and everyone. Joseph's mastery over Egypt saves the world, pointing forward to the Messiah whose authority brings true salvation.","cross":"Genesis 39:2-3; Genesis 45:9; Psalm 110:1"},"children":{"hebrew":"בָּנִים","translit":"banim","strongs":"H1121","plain":"Sons and daughters — your family's next generation. In the Bible, children carry the covenant forward.","def":"Banim is the plural of ben (son), but it often means all children. In covenant language, children are not just descendants — they are the carriers of the promise. 'Your children will be strangers in a foreign land' (Gen 15:13). 'All the children of Israel' becomes the standard phrase for the whole covenant people.","kingdom":"Children in the Bible are covenant inheritance, not just personal family. The barren wombs of Sarah, Rebekah, and Rachel all become sites of miraculous divine action because the covenant line must continue. God opens the womb to keep His promise moving forward.","cross":"Genesis 15:13; Genesis 21:7; Genesis 30:1"},"food":{"hebrew":"אֹכֶל","translit":"ochel","strongs":"H400","plain":"Food — what people eat to live. In Genesis, food carries big meaning: the forbidden fruit, the provision in famine, the grain Joseph stores.","def":"Ochel is simply food — anything eaten. But food carries enormous weight in Genesis: the fruit of the tree of knowledge (Gen 2-3), the forbidden food that broke everything; the food Noah was told to gather (Gen 6:21); and the grain Joseph stored across seven years that saved the world (Gen 41:35). Food appears at every crisis.","kingdom":"From the forbidden fruit to the bread and cup of the Lord's Supper, food in Scripture marks covenant moments. What you eat and with whom you eat it carries covenant significance — breaking bread together is a sign of covenant relationship.","cross":"Genesis 2:16-17; Genesis 41:35; Matthew 26:26"},"city":{"hebrew":"עִיר","translit":"ir","strongs":"H5892","plain":"A city — a walled settlement of people. In Genesis, cities often represent human power organized without God.","def":"Ir means city or town. The first city in the Bible was built by Cain (Gen 4:17). Babel is the first great city project — united humanity building to make a name for itself (Gen 11). Sodom and Gomorrah are destroyed cities. In Genesis, the city often represents human self-sufficiency and concentrated sin. Abraham, by contrast, lives in tents, looking for 'a city whose builder and architect is God' (Heb 11:10).","kingdom":"The Bible ends in a city — the New Jerusalem, which comes down from heaven. The city of God replaces the cities of man. Abraham was right to look for it.","cross":"Genesis 4:17; Genesis 11:4-5; Genesis 19:1; Hebrews 11:10; Revelation 21:2"},"flocks":{"hebrew":"צֹאן","translit":"tson","strongs":"H6629","plain":"Sheep and goats kept together as a herd — the core of a shepherd family's wealth and livelihood.","def":"Tson means a flock of small livestock — sheep and goats. The patriarchs lived as shepherds: Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, and all twelve sons tended flocks. Jacob's entire conflict with Laban centers on whose flocks are whose (Gen 30-31). Rachel was a shepherdess. The shepherd life deeply shapes how the Bible talks about leadership — God as Shepherd, kings as shepherds.","kingdom":"The flock is one of the Bible's central metaphors: Israel is God's flock, the Messiah is the Good Shepherd. The image comes out of this literal shepherd culture. Real flock-keeping (the risk, the care, the loss) gives the metaphor its weight.","cross":"Genesis 30:31-43; Psalm 23; John 10:11"},"silver":{"hebrew":"כֶּסֶף","translit":"keseph","strongs":"H3701","plain":"Silver — used as money, for gifts, and in covenant payments in the ancient world.","def":"Keseph means silver, which was the main currency of the ancient Near East before coins existed. It was weighed, not counted. Abraham paid 400 shekels of silver for the cave of Machpelah (Gen 23:16). Joseph was sold for 20 pieces of silver (Gen 37:28). Joseph's brothers are framed when his silver cup is found in Benjamin's sack (Gen 44). Silver runs through Genesis as both commerce and covenant.","ane":"Silver was the standard of exchange in ancient Canaan and Egypt. Keseph literally means 'the longed-for one' — it was precious and coveted. The price paid for Joseph (20 silver pieces) was the going rate for a young male slave in the ancient Near East.","kingdom":"The price of a slave in silver connects to Judas betraying Jesus for 30 pieces of silver — both are blood-money moments where God's chosen one is sold by his own people, yet the betrayal drives the plan of salvation forward.","cross":"Genesis 23:16; Genesis 37:28; Zechariah 11:12-13; Matthew 26:15"},"grain":{"hebrew":"בַּר","translit":"bar","strongs":"H1250","plain":"Grain — wheat, barley, or corn stored as food. In the Joseph story, grain is what saved the world.","def":"Bar means grain — threshed, pure grain. Joseph's genius was storing grain during seven years of abundance to survive seven years of famine (Gen 41:35-36). All the earth came to Egypt to buy grain from Joseph (Gen 41:57). The same man his brothers sold for silver became the one who held the world's food supply in his hands.","kingdom":"Joseph as keeper of grain for the nations is a picture of Christ — the Bread of Life who offers spiritual sustenance to all who come to Him. Egypt's grain came from one man's suffering; the world's salvation comes from one Man's.","cross":"Genesis 41:35; Genesis 41:57; John 6:35; John 12:24"},"well":{"hebrew":"בְּאֵר","translit":"be'er","strongs":"H875","plain":"A well — a dug pit with water at the bottom. In Genesis, wells are meeting places and covenant sites.","def":"Be'er means a well — hand-dug, vital for survival in a dry land. Wells in Genesis are named (Beersheba = 'well of the oath'), contested, and covenanted over. Abraham and Abimelech make a covenant at a well. Isaac re-digs his father's wells and they become disputed with Philistines. Jacob meets Rachel at a well. Moses meets his future wife at a well. The well is a place of meeting, covenant, and provision.","visual":"A stone circle in the ground with a rope and bucket. The one who controls the well controls life in a dry land. When Jacob single-handedly rolls the stone from the well for Rachel (Gen 29:10), it is a feat of both strength and love.","kingdom":"Jesus meets the Samaritan woman at a well — deliberately evoking the patriarchal well-meeting pattern. He offers water that wells can never provide: living water that springs up to eternal life (John 4).","cross":"Genesis 21:30-31; Genesis 29:2-10; John 4:14"},"camels":{"hebrew":"גְּמַלִּים","translit":"gemalim","strongs":"H1581","plain":"Camels — the large humped animals used for long desert travel and as a sign of great wealth.","def":"Gamal (singular) / gemalim (plural). Abraham was wealthy in camels (Gen 12:16). When Abraham's servant went to find a wife for Isaac, he took ten camels loaded with gifts (Gen 24:10). The camel caravan of Ishmaelites that Joseph was sold to was carrying trade goods to Egypt (Gen 37:25). Camels were expensive, prestigious animals — a camel-owner was a person of means.","ane":"In the ancient Near East, camels were the long-haul transport of the day — capable of carrying heavy loads across desert terrain. A train of ten camels showed that Abraham's servant came on serious, well-resourced business. Rebekah watered ten camels — a massive and impressive act of hospitality.","kingdom":"Abraham's servant's ten camels arrive at the well, Rebekah waters them all, and what follows is the marriage that continues the covenant line toward the Messiah. The camels are not incidental — they signal that heaven itself has arrived to seek a bride for the covenant son.","cross":"Genesis 24:10; Genesis 37:25"},"born":{"hebrew":"יָלַד","translit":"yalad","strongs":"H3205","plain":"To be born — to come into the world. In the Bible, every birth in a covenant family is a step in God's plan.","def":"Yalad means to bear, give birth, beget. It is the engine of the Genesis genealogies: 'And X lived Y years, and begot Z.' Each birth is not just biology — it is the covenant line advancing. The barren women in Genesis (Sarah, Rebekah, Rachel) make every birth in the covenant line a miracle, not a given.","kingdom":"When the New Testament opens with 'the genealogy of Jesus Christ,' it is using the same yalad logic of Genesis: every birth was moving history toward one birth — the one who would be born of a virgin (Isaiah 7:14), born in Bethlehem (Micah 5:2), and born again in resurrection.","cross":"Genesis 5; Matthew 1:1-16; Luke 3:23-38"},"birth":{"hebrew":"מוֹלֶדֶת","translit":"moledeth","strongs":"H4138","plain":"Birthplace, family of origin, or the event of being born. Also used for generations and lineage.","def":"Moledeth comes from yalad (to bear/beget) and means birth, native land, or kindred. Abraham left his 'land and birthplace' (moledeth) to follow God (Gen 12:1). The generations lists (toledoth) that structure Genesis use the same root — 'these are the generations of...' Birth and belonging, lineage and land are all tied together in one word-family.","kingdom":"Every follower of God is called to leave their comfortable moledeth — their birth-family, their hometown security — and follow where God leads. Abraham's departure from his birthplace is the model for the life of faith.","cross":"Genesis 12:1; Genesis 11:28; Ruth 2:11; Acts 7:3"},"morning":{"hebrew":"בֹּקֶר","translit":"boqer","strongs":"H1242","plain":"Morning — the beginning of a new day. In Genesis 1, each creation day ends and the next begins with 'evening and morning.'","def":"Boqer is morning — the time of light returning, of new beginnings. Genesis 1 marks each creation day with 'there was evening and there was morning' — the day is complete, a new one begins. Abraham rises 'early in the morning' (Gen 19:27; Gen 22:3) — the morning that follows an evening of decision is always the morning of obedience. Morning is when God acts, when people respond.","kingdom":"Morning in the Bible carries resurrection weight: 'Weeping may last for the night, but joy comes in the morning' (Psalm 30:5). The resurrection happened at early morning, the beginning of the eighth day — a new creation morning.","cross":"Genesis 1:5; Genesis 22:3; Psalm 30:5; John 20:1"},"evening":{"hebrew":"עֶרֶב","translit":"erev","strongs":"H6153","plain":"Evening — the end of the day, when light fades. In Genesis 1, each creation day is completed with 'evening and morning.'","def":"Erev is evening — the descent of darkness, the end of a day's work. The Hebrew day runs from evening to morning (not midnight to midnight), which is why 'evening and morning' marks each creation day. The Passover lamb was sacrificed 'between the evenings' (Exodus 12:6). Evening is also a time of encounter — Isaac goes out to meditate in the field 'at evening' when Rebekah arrives.","kingdom":"The first day of a new week — the morning of the resurrection — is preceded by the 'evening' of the Sabbath rest. The pattern of evening-then-morning built into creation points to darkness giving way to light, death giving way to life.","cross":"Genesis 1:5; Genesis 24:63; Exodus 12:6"},"Laban":{"hebrew":"לָבָן","translit":"lavan","strongs":"H3837","plain":"Laban — Jacob's uncle and father-in-law, who deceived Jacob repeatedly but couldn't stop God's plan.","def":"Lavan means 'white.' Laban was the brother of Rebekah (Gen 24:29) and later the father of Leah and Rachel. Jacob worked 14 years for Rachel — Laban cheated him by substituting Leah on the wedding night (Gen 29). Jacob worked another 6 years for flocks, during which Laban changed his wages 10 times (Gen 31:7). Yet God prospered Jacob despite every scheme. The story of Laban is the story of human scheming unable to derail divine covenant.","kingdom":"Laban represents the obstacles placed by human manipulation in the path of God's promise. Jacob, the deceiver, meets someone who out-deceives him — yet God ensures the covenant continues. No human trickery can abort a divine plan.","cross":"Genesis 24:29; Genesis 29:21-25; Genesis 31:7"},"Abimelech":{"hebrew":"אֲבִימֶלֶךְ","translit":"avimelech","strongs":"H40","plain":"Abimelech — a Philistine king who appears twice in Genesis: once with Abraham, once with Isaac.","def":"Avimelech means 'my father is king.' This is likely a title (like Pharaoh) rather than a personal name. Two Abimelech appearances shape Genesis: he takes Sarah into his household (Gen 20), receiving a divine warning in a dream; and he deals with Isaac after Isaac also (like his father) called his wife his sister (Gen 26). In both cases, the pagan king ends up acknowledging God and making a covenant. Beersheba is named from the oath made with Abimelech.","kingdom":"Abimelech is a pagan king who repeatedly recognizes that God is with the covenant family. His response — backing off, making peace, blessing the covenant heir — shows that the promise to Abraham ('all nations will be blessed through you') already works its way outward even in Genesis.","cross":"Genesis 20:2-18; Genesis 21:22-34; Genesis 26:6-31"},"formless":{"hebrew":"תֹּהוּ","translit":"tohu","strongs":"H8414","plain":"Formless, empty, chaotic — the state of the earth before God began shaping it on Day One.","def":"Tohu is emptiness, formlessness, chaos. Genesis 1:2 — 'the earth was formless (tohu) and void (bohu)' — is the condition God begins working with. Tohu describes something that has no useful shape or purpose yet. The same word is used in Isaiah for a wasteland, and by God's voice out of the whirlwind in Job: 'Where were you when I laid the earth's foundation?'","kingdom":"The entire creation narrative is God bringing order, beauty, and purpose out of tohu-bohu. This is what God does with human lives too — He takes what is shapeless and empty and speaks it into order.","cross":"Genesis 1:2; Isaiah 45:18; Jeremiah 4:23"},"void":{"hebrew":"בֹּהוּ","translit":"bohu","strongs":"H922","plain":"Empty, vacant, and without anything in it. Used with 'formless' in Genesis 1:2.","def":"Bohu appears only three times in the Bible, always paired with tohu. Genesis 1:2 — 'formless and void' (tohu va-bohu). The phrase describes absolute emptiness before creation. The same phrase recurs in Isaiah 34:11 and Jeremiah 4:23 as a picture of divine judgment returning land to pre-creation chaos.","kingdom":"Tohu-bohu is what sin does to what God made good. Judgment un-creates. Redemption re-creates. The Spirit that hovered over the void in Genesis 1:2 is the same Spirit who brings new creation in Christ.","cross":"Genesis 1:2; Isaiah 34:11; Jeremiah 4:23"},"seasons":{"hebrew":"מוֹעֲדִים","translit":"moadim","strongs":"H4150","plain":"Appointed times and seasons — set moments on God's calendar. The sun and moon mark them.","def":"Moadim (singular: moed) means appointed times, feasts, seasons. In Genesis 1:14, the luminaries are set to mark 'signs and seasons (moadim) and days and years' — the sun and moon are God's cosmic calendar. Later, God's entire festival calendar (Passover, Shavuot, Sukkot) uses the same word: these are YHWH's moadim — His appointed meetings with His people.","kingdom":"The sun and moon were created on Day Four specifically to govern God's sacred calendar. Every biblical feast is a moed — an appointment God keeps with His people. Jesus died at Passover (the Pesach moed), rose at Firstfruits, and sent the Spirit at Pentecost (Shavuot) — meeting every appointment on the covenant calendar.","cross":"Genesis 1:14; Leviticus 23:2; Luke 22:15"},"signs":{"hebrew":"אוֹת","translit":"ot","strongs":"H226","plain":"A sign or marker — something that points beyond itself to confirm a covenant, promise, or divine act.","def":"Ot means sign, mark, token. The luminaries are for 'signs' in Genesis 1:14. The rainbow is a covenant sign (Gen 9:12-13). Circumcision is a sign of the covenant (Gen 17:11). Signs point to something bigger than themselves — they are God's way of saying 'Remember this, I am here, this is real.' The plagues of Egypt are also called signs (Exodus 10:2).","kingdom":"From the stars to the rainbow to the cross, God's signs confirm His covenant faithfulness. When Jesus performs miracles, John calls them signs (semeia in Greek, translating ot) — they point beyond the miracle to who Jesus is.","cross":"Genesis 1:14; Genesis 9:12; Genesis 17:11; John 2:11"},"Potiphar":{"hebrew":"פּוֹטִיפַר","translit":"potifar","strongs":"H6318","plain":"Potiphar — the Egyptian official who bought Joseph as a slave, then threw him in prison after a false accusation.","def":"Potiphar was 'captain of the guard' for Pharaoh (Gen 37:36) — a senior military official. He bought Joseph from the Ishmaelite traders. God prospered everything Joseph touched, and Potiphar made Joseph overseer of his entire household (Gen 39:4). When Potiphar's wife falsely accused Joseph, Potiphar threw him in prison — but even the prison was still God's next step, not a dead end.","kingdom":"Potiphar is an instrument in God's plan even while trying to punish Joseph. The same hand that put Joseph into service put him into prison — and the prison had the butler who had the dream who led to Pharaoh. Every setback in Genesis advances the plot.","cross":"Genesis 37:36; Genesis 39:1-20"},"cubit":{"plain":"About 18 inches — the distance from your elbow to your fingertip. Ancient builders used this instead of measuring tapes.","def":"A cubit (Hebrew: ammah) was the standard unit of length in the ancient world — approximately 17.5 to 18 inches, based on the forearm length from elbow to middle fingertip. The royal cubit was slightly longer (~20.5 inches). Used throughout the Bible for measuring Noah's ark, the Tabernacle, Solomon's Temple, and Ezekiel's vision of the new temple. When the Bible says something was 300 cubits long, that's about 450 feet."},"cubits":{"_alias_of":"cubit","plain":"Plural of cubit — each about 18 inches long."},"shekel":{"plain":"A unit of weight used as money — roughly half an ounce of silver or gold. The most common currency in the Old Testament.","def":"Shekel (Hebrew: sheqel) means 'weight' — from the verb shakal, to weigh. Before coins existed, metal was weighed on scales. A shekel was about 11.4 grams (just under half an ounce). Silver shekels were the standard currency. The Temple shekel was a specific standard kept by the priests. Prices, wages, and purchases throughout the Bible are quoted in shekels. The 30 silver pieces paid for Joseph's betrayal and later for Judas's were shekels.","ane":"In ancient Canaan and Mesopotamia, weighed silver was the universal medium of exchange. Merchants carried sets of weights. Dishonest scales (using lighter weights when selling, heavier when buying) are specifically condemned throughout Proverbs and the prophets."},"shekels":{"_alias_of":"shekel","plain":"Plural of shekel — units of silver or gold used as money."},"ephah":{"plain":"A dry measure — roughly half a bushel. Used to measure grain, flour, and other dry goods.","def":"An ephah (Hebrew: eyphah) was the standard unit of dry volume in ancient Israel — approximately 22 liters or half a bushel (about 3/5 of a US bushel). Used for measuring grain, flour, barley, and other dry goods in marketplace transactions, priestly offerings, and famine rations. One ephah of flour was roughly what you'd bake into bread for a family for several days. The prophets condemned merchants who made their ephah small (giving less than they claimed) — Amos 8:5."},"talent":{"plain":"A very large unit of weight — about 75 pounds of silver or gold. Used for very large transactions and taxes.","def":"A talent (Hebrew: kikkar, Greek: talanton) was the largest unit of weight in the ancient world — approximately 34 kilograms (75 pounds). In terms of silver, one talent was equivalent to about 3,000 shekels. When the Bible says the Temple treasury held 'hundreds of talents,' it's describing tons of precious metal. The 'talent' in Jesus' Parable of the Talents (Matthew 25:14-30) was an enormous sum — one talent represented about 20 years of a common laborer's wages.","kingdom":"Jesus chose this massive unit deliberately in the parable — the master trusts his servants with enormous wealth. The question is not whether to do something impressive with it, but whether to do anything at all."},"talents":{"_alias_of":"talent","plain":"Plural of talent — each about 75 pounds of silver or gold, a vast sum."},"denarius":{"plain":"A Roman silver coin worth one day's wages for a laborer. The standard everyday currency of New Testament times.","def":"A denarius (plural: denarii) was the standard Roman silver coin — approximately 3.9 grams of silver. In first-century Palestine, one denarius was the standard daily wage for a field laborer (Matthew 20:2). The coin bore the image of Caesar — which is why Jesus used it in 'Render to Caesar what is Caesar's' (Matthew 22:20-21). The Good Samaritan paid two denarii for the injured man's inn stay — about two days' wages.","ane":"Roman coinage was the medium of the empire's economy. The image of Caesar on coins was itself a political and religious statement — Caesars were claimed to be divine. Jewish communities who handled denarii daily were handling coins that called their oppressor a god."},"denarii":{"_alias_of":"denarius","plain":"Plural of denarius — Roman silver coins, each worth a day's wages."},"garland":{"hebrew":"לִוְיַת","translit":"livyat","strongs":"H3880","plain":"A woven wreath of flowers or branches placed on the head as a public mark of honor — like a crown given to a winner or a bride.","def":"Livyat (from lavah — to encircle, to twine around) is a garland or wreath woven from flowers, olive branches, or precious materials. In the ancient Near East, garlands were placed on kings at coronation, on bridegrooms at weddings, on victors at competitions, and on honored guests at feasts. Proverbs 1:9 — 'They will be a garland of grace on your head' — is saying: your parents' wisdom will make you as visibly honorable as someone publicly crowned. People will see it on you.","kingdom":"In Revelation 4:4 and 4:10, the elders in heaven wear 'crowns of gold' (Greek: stephanoi — wreaths) and cast them before the throne. The garland of honor always points back to the one who bestowed it."},"garments":{"hebrew":"בְּגָדִים","translit":"begadim","strongs":"H899","plain":"Clothes — but in the Bible, garments often carry meaning beyond just clothing. They represent identity, honor, and covenant.","def":"The Hebrew word beged (plural: begadim) means garment or clothing. But garments in Scripture carry symbolic weight: to put on someone's garment was to receive their identity or authority (Elijah's mantle). To tear one's garments was to express grief or moral horror. Joseph's robe was stripped from him in betrayal and used to fake his death. Jesus' garments were divided by lot at the cross. Garments of righteousness and salvation are recurring images of covenant standing before God.","kingdom":"Revelation 19:8 says the Bride of Christ is given 'fine linen, bright and clean' — and then explains: 'the fine linen stands for the righteous acts of God's holy people.' Our eternal garment is not earned — it is given."},"garment":{"_alias_of":"garments","plain":"A piece of clothing — but in the Bible, garments often represent identity, honor, and covenant standing."},"robe":{"plain":"A long outer garment of dignity — the main outer clothing worn by priests, kings, and people of honor.","def":"Robes in the ancient Near East were the mark of rank. Priests wore designated robes for service. Kings were identified by their robes. Joseph's 'coat of many colors' (or 'long-sleeved robe') marked him as his father's favored heir — which is why his brothers stripped it off. The father in the prodigal son parable called for 'the best robe' to be put on his returning son — restoring his status in one gesture. When soldiers mocked Jesus with a scarlet robe (Matthew 27:28), they were mimicking the precise visual language of royal honor."},"robes":{"_alias_of":"robe","plain":"Plural of robe — the long outer garments of priests, kings, and honored people."},"tunic":{"plain":"A close-fitting undergarment worn next to the skin — the inner layer of clothing, like a shirt.","def":"A tunic (Greek: chiton, Hebrew: kutonet) was the basic inner garment — a close-fitting garment of linen or wool worn directly on the body. The coat or robe went over it. Jesus' tunic at the crucifixion was 'seamless, woven in one piece from top to bottom' (John 19:23) — this was an expensive, high-quality garment, not a poor man's rough clothing. The soldiers didn't tear it because it was too valuable."},"cloak":{"plain":"A large outer wrap used as a coat by day and a blanket by night — the most essential item of clothing for the poor.","def":"The cloak (Hebrew: simlah, Greek: himation) was the outer garment — a large rectangular piece of cloth wrapped around the body. For the poor it served as coat by day and blanket by night. This is why Exodus 22:26-27 commands: if you take someone's cloak as a pledge (security for a debt), return it before nightfall — they need it to sleep. When Jesus says 'if someone sues you for your tunic, give him your cloak also' (Matthew 5:40), he is describing stripping off literally everything a poor person owns — exposing the system of exploitation."},"mantle":{"plain":"A prophetic cloak — the symbol of a prophet's calling and authority. Elijah's mantle passed to Elisha at his departure.","def":"A mantle (Hebrew: aderet) was a large cloak, but in the prophetic tradition it became the symbol of prophetic authority. When Elijah threw his mantle over Elisha (1 Kings 19:19), he was calling Elisha to prophetic succession. When Elijah was taken up in the whirlwind, his mantle fell and Elisha picked it up — claiming the spirit and authority that went with it. The mantle represents the transferred calling of God."},"turban":{"plain":"A head covering wound from cloth — worn by priests as a sign of holiness and by kings as a sign of authority.","def":"The turban (Hebrew: mitsnefet for priestly turban, tsaniph for general turban) was the head covering of priests and officials. The high priest's turban had a gold plate attached to it reading 'HOLY TO THE LORD' (Exodus 28:36). In Zechariah 3:5, the restouring of Joshua the high priest includes putting a clean turban on his head — restoring his priestly honor. Isaiah 62:3: 'You will be a royal diadem and a turban of splendor in the hand of your God.'"},"veil":{"plain":"A covering for the face or head — and also the massive curtain in the Temple that separated ordinary people from the Most Holy Place.","def":"Veil appears in two key contexts. (1) Personal veil: worn by women in various cultures and contexts — Rebekah veiled herself before Isaac, Tamar used a veil to disguise herself. In 1 Corinthians 11 Paul discusses head covering and prophesying. (2) Temple veil: the massive curtain separating the Holy Place from the Most Holy Place in the Tabernacle and Temple — 60 feet tall, 30 feet wide, 4 inches thick in Herod's Temple. At Jesus' death, 'the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom' (Matthew 27:51) — from God's side down, not from human hands.","kingdom":"The torn veil is the central statement of the Crucifixion's effect on access to God. The barrier between humanity and God's presence — maintained since Adam's exile — was permanently removed. Hebrews 10:19-20 calls Jesus himself 'the new and living way opened for us through the curtain, that is, his body.'"},"sandals":{"plain":"Open-toed leather footwear fastened with straps. To carry or fasten someone's sandals was the most menial task — lower than a servant's work.","def":"Sandals (Greek: hypodema, Hebrew: na'al) were simple leather soles with straps tied around the foot. In Jewish culture, removing, carrying, or fastening sandals was the specific task of the lowest-ranked slave. This is why John the Baptist says: 'After me comes one who is more powerful than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry' (Matthew 3:11). He's not being falsely modest — he's using the precise cultural language of hierarchy: I am not even qualified to perform the most degrading task in relation to him."},"sandal":{"_alias_of":"sandals","plain":"A leather-strapped open shoe. To carry someone's sandal was the most menial servant's task."},"ephod":{"hebrew":"אֵפוֹד","translit":"ephod","strongs":"H646","plain":"A priestly vest worn by the high priest over his robe — it held the breastpiece with the twelve stones and the Urim and Thummim.","def":"The ephod was a garment worn only by priests, specifically the high priest. Made of gold, blue, purple, scarlet, and linen (Exodus 28:6), it was an apron-like vest worn over the blue robe. The high priest's ephod had two shoulder pieces, each set with a stone engraved with six of the twelve tribe names — Israel's names were carried on the shoulders of the priest as he served. The breastpiece (with its twelve gemstones) was attached to the ephod.","ane":"Other peoples in the ANE used ephod-like garments for their priests and divine statues. When Micah made an idol and hired a Levite, he also made an ephod (Judges 17:5). When David danced before the ark 'wearing a linen ephod' (2 Samuel 6:14), he was wearing the priestly garment — declaring himself priest-king before God."},"breastpiece":{"plain":"The square pouch worn by the high priest on his chest — set with twelve gemstones for the twelve tribes, and holding the Urim and Thummim for discerning God's will.","def":"The breastpiece (Hebrew: hoshen) was a folded square of the same material as the ephod, attached to it over the priest's heart. It was set with twelve precious stones in four rows of three, each engraved with one of the twelve tribes. The priest carried Israel's names over his heart when he entered God's presence — a picture of intercession. Inside the fold were kept the Urim and Thummim — the sacred objects used to discern God's will on specific decisions.","kingdom":"The high priest carrying Israel's names on his chest before God's presence is one of the Bible's richest pictures of intercession — the mediator presenting the people to God, holding them close to his heart. Hebrews says Jesus, our great high priest, is always interceding for us (Hebrews 7:25)."},"sackcloth":{"hebrew":"שַׂק","translit":"saq","strongs":"H8242","plain":"Rough, scratchy fabric made from goat or camel hair — worn during mourning, fasting, or repentance as a visible sign of grief and humility.","def":"Sackcloth (saq) was coarse, uncomfortable cloth woven from goat or camel hair. It was worn against the skin — sometimes as a loincloth, sometimes as a full tunic — during times of extreme grief, fasting, or national repentance. The physical discomfort was intentional: the body expressing what the soul felt. Jacob wore sackcloth when he thought Joseph was dead (Genesis 37:34). The men of Nineveh put on sackcloth in response to Jonah's preaching (Jonah 3:5-6). David wore sackcloth and fasted for his sick child (2 Samuel 12:16).","kingdom":"Sackcloth and ashes together were the ancient world's complete picture of broken humility — taking off fine clothes (identity and status) and putting on slave-cloth, sitting in the ash pile of burned offerings. God's response to genuine sackcloth repentance in Scripture is consistently mercy."},"ashes":{"plain":"The grey powder left after burning — used in mourning and repentance as a sign of humility and grief.","def":"Ashes (Hebrew: epher) were a symbol of grief, humility, and mortality. Job sat in ashes (Job 2:8). Tamar put ashes on her head in distress (2 Samuel 13:19). The phrase 'sackcloth and ashes' (Daniel 9:3, Matthew 11:21) describes the most complete expression of mourning and repentance. Ashes are what remains after everything is consumed by fire — the biblical image of nothingness. 'You are dust, and to dust you shall return' (Genesis 3:19) is the same thought.","kingdom":"In Isaiah 61:3, God promises to give his people 'a crown of beauty instead of ashes.' The exchange — ashes for beauty, mourning for joy — is one of the Bible's core pictures of redemption."},"loins":{"plain":"The region of the hips and lower abdomen — used in the Bible to mean reproductive power, strength, and your descendants.","def":"'Loins' (Hebrew: chalats/motnayim, Greek: osphys) refers to the hip and lower abdomen region. In Hebrew thought, this region was associated with reproductive power — 'those who came from his loins' means his biological descendants. 'Gird your loins' (also translated 'tuck your robe into your belt') meant pull up your long robe and tuck it into your belt so you can run or work — getting ready for action. 1 Kings 18:46: 'Elijah girded up his loins and ran before Ahab all the way to Jezreel.' Ephesians 6:14: 'Stand firm then, with the belt of truth buckled around your waist (loins)' — spiritual readiness."},"threshing":{"plain":"The process of separating grain from stalks by beating them — done on a threshing floor, a flat open area exposed to wind.","def":"Threshing was the process of loosening grain from the stalks after harvest. Grain was brought to a threshing floor — a flat, hard surface on a hilltop where wind could blow. Workers would beat the stalks (with flails or by having animals walk on them) to knock the grain loose. Then the stalks were tossed into the air — the grain fell back down (it was heavy) and the chaff blew away. This is the image behind Matthew 3:12: 'His winnowing fork is in his hand, and he will clear his threshing floor.' The threshing floor was also a site of worship and divine encounter — Ruth and Boaz's story centers on the threshing floor, as does Araunah's threshing floor where David built an altar (2 Samuel 24:18-25) — the same site that became Solomon's Temple.","kingdom":"John the Baptist's threshing-floor imagery for Jesus' ministry is not gentle: the one coming will separate grain from chaff absolutely. But the purpose of threshing is not to destroy — it is to gather the grain."},"winnowing":{"plain":"Tossing grain into the air after threshing so the wind blows away the lightweight husks (chaff) while the heavy grain falls back down.","def":"Winnowing followed threshing. Using a wooden fork or a shovel, workers would scoop up the threshed mixture and toss it into the air. The wind blew away the chaff (husks, straw) — light and worthless — while the good grain fell back to the floor. This is the image behind John the Baptist's words in Matthew 3:12: 'He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire. His winnowing fork is in his hand, and he will clear his threshing floor, gathering his wheat into the barn and burning up the chaff with unquenchable fire.' The separation is absolute and final."},"winnow":{"_alias_of":"winnowing","plain":"To toss grain into the wind so the chaff blows away and the good grain falls back down."},"chaff":{"plain":"The lightweight husks and straw left over after threshing grain — blown away by the wind as worthless.","def":"Chaff is what gets blown away in winnowing — the husks, broken straw, and dust that surround the grain but have no value themselves. It is the Bible's repeated image of what is temporary, insubstantial, and ultimately destroyed. Psalm 1:4: 'The wicked are not so, but are like chaff that the wind blows away.' Isaiah 17:13: 'Though the nations roar like the rushing of many waters, when he rebukes them they flee far away, driven before the wind like chaff on the hills.' In Matthew 3:12, chaff is burned with 'unquenchable fire' — the ultimate insubstantiality meets the ultimate permanence."},"gleaning":{"plain":"Picking up the leftover grain, grapes, or olives after harvesters have gone through a field — God commanded farmers to leave this for the poor and foreigners.","def":"Gleaning (Hebrew: laqat) was the practice of the poor going behind harvesters to pick up what was dropped or overlooked. God specifically commanded farmers not to harvest the corners of their fields, not to go back for forgotten sheaves, and not to strip their vineyards completely — these leftovers were for 'the poor and the stranger' (Leviticus 19:9-10, Deuteronomy 24:19-21). Ruth gleaning in Boaz's field (Ruth 2) demonstrates the system working exactly as designed — and then going beyond what the law required when Boaz orders his workers to deliberately drop extra for her.","kingdom":"The gleaning law is one of the Bible's most concrete pictures of structural provision for the vulnerable. God's justice is not only punitive — it is built into the economic design of the community."},"glean":{"_alias_of":"gleaning","plain":"To gather leftover grain or fruit from a field after the main harvest — a right given to the poor by law."},"sheaf":{"plain":"A bundle of cut grain stalks tied together — gathered after harvest, before threshing.","def":"A sheaf (Hebrew: alummah, Greek: drachme) was a bundle of cut grain stalks — wheat, barley, or other crops — tied together for transport to the threshing floor. The binding of sheaves was harvest labor. Joseph's first dream in Genesis 37:7 was of sheaves: his sheaf stood upright while his brothers' sheaves bowed to his. The Feast of Firstfruits involved waving a sheaf before the LORD (Leviticus 23:10-11) as an offering of the first of the harvest."},"sheaves":{"_alias_of":"sheaf","plain":"Plural of sheaf — bundles of cut grain tied together for harvest."},"yoke":{"plain":"A wooden bar fitted across the necks of two animals so they can pull a plow or cart together — and a powerful biblical symbol of servitude, burden, or partnership.","def":"A yoke (Hebrew: ol, Greek: zygos) was a carved wooden beam placed across the necks of two oxen, connecting them so they could pull together. A well-fitted yoke meant the animals could work effectively without being hurt. To 'break the yoke' meant freedom from servitude (Isaiah 9:4, Jeremiah 28:2). A 'heavy yoke' meant oppressive servitude — Solomon's heavy yoke of taxation (1 Kings 12:4). Jesus' 'easy yoke' (Matthew 11:29-30) contrasts with the Pharisees' heavy burden of legal requirements. 'Do not be unequally yoked' (2 Corinthians 6:14) uses the image of two different animals being paired — a mismatch that hurts both.","kingdom":"Jesus' invitation — 'Come to me...Take my yoke upon you' — is an invitation to a working partnership, not just rest. The yoke is easy not because there is no work, but because the one you are yoked with carries the weight."},"sickle":{"plain":"A curved cutting blade used to harvest grain or grapes — and in the Bible, an image of the final harvest of judgment.","def":"A sickle was a handheld curved blade for cutting grain stalks at harvest. At harvest time, workers would swing the sickle through the standing grain. Joel 3:13: 'Put in the sickle, for the harvest is ripe.' Revelation 14:14-19 uses the sickle three times in the great harvest vision of final judgment — 'thrust in your sickle and reap, for the hour to reap has come, for the harvest of the earth is fully ripe.' The double image (grain harvest = salvation, grape harvest = wrath) carries both judgment and gathering."},"firstfruits":{"hebrew":"בִּכּוּרִים","translit":"bikkurim","strongs":"H1061","plain":"The first and best portion of the harvest — given to God before anything else was eaten or used. A covenant act of trust that the rest would follow.","def":"Firstfruits (bikkurim) were the first ripened produce of the harvest — the very first grain, grapes, olives, figs to appear. God commanded Israel to bring these to the Temple before eating any of the crop. The act was theological: giving the first portion acknowledged that God owned the whole harvest and was its source. It was also an act of trust — you gave before you knew how the full harvest would turn out. Proverbs 3:9-10: 'Honor the LORD with your wealth, with the firstfruits of all your crops; then your barns will be filled to overflowing.'","kingdom":"1 Corinthians 15:20-23 calls Jesus 'the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep.' His resurrection is the first ripened fruit of the full harvest of resurrection that is coming. James 1:18 calls believers 'a kind of firstfruits of all he created.' The agricultural metaphor is cosmic: God is harvesting his creation."},"storehouse":{"plain":"A building where grain, oil, wine, or other supplies were kept — and in the Bible, often used as a picture of God's abundant provision.","def":"Storehouse (Hebrew: otsar, Hebrew: asam — different terms for different storage purposes) was where reserves were kept: grain, wine, oil, treasures. Malachi 3:10: 'Bring the whole tithe into the storehouse, that there may be food in my house. Test me in this,' says the LORD Almighty, 'and see if I will not throw open the floodgates of heaven and pour out so much blessing that there will not be room enough to store it.' God pictures himself as having a heavenly storehouse — Deuteronomy 28:12 calls it 'his good storehouse, the heavens.'"},"millstone":{"plain":"A large, heavy circular stone used to grind grain into flour — so heavy that being thrown into the sea with one tied around your neck was a picture of total destruction.","def":"Millstones were paired heavy circular stones — the lower one fixed, the upper one rotated on top to grind grain between them. They were essential household equipment; Deuteronomy 24:6 forbids taking a millstone as a loan pledge because the family would starve without it. Jesus uses the millstone as his image of ultimate judgment for anyone who causes a child to stumble (Matthew 18:6; Mark 9:42; Luke 17:2): it would be better to have a millstone hung around your neck and be thrown into the sea. The violence of the image signals how seriously God views harm to the vulnerable."},"winepress":{"plain":"A stone or wood vat where grapes were stomped to extract juice for making wine — and a powerful biblical image of judgment.","def":"A winepress was typically carved from stone — a large upper basin where grapes were treaded by foot, with a channel leading to a lower collecting vat. Treading grapes was hard, wet, staining work that turned the treader red. Isaiah 63:3 uses it for God's judgment: 'I have trodden the winepress alone...I trampled them in my anger and trod them down in my wrath; their blood spattered my garments and stained all my clothing.' Revelation 14:19-20 and 19:15 continue this imagery: 'He treads the winepress of the fury of the wrath of God Almighty.'"},"sanctuary":{"plain":"The holy place — God's dwelling among His people. In the Tabernacle and Temple it was the sacred inner space; also used for any place of God's protection.","def":"Sanctuary (Hebrew: qodesh, miqdash) was God's designated dwelling place among Israel. It had concentric rings of holiness: Outer Court (all Israelites), Holy Place (priests only), Most Holy Place (high priest once per year). The sanctuary was not primarily a human religious meeting hall — it was God coming to live in the midst of his people. Exodus 25:8: 'Have them make a sanctuary for me, and I will dwell among them.'"},"altars":{"_alias_of":"altar","plain":"Plural of altar — raised places for sacrifice and offerings, built wherever God appeared."},"cherubim":{"hebrew":"כְּרוּבִים","translit":"keruvim","strongs":"H3742","plain":"Mighty angelic beings who guard God's holy presence — not the chubby baby angels of popular art. These are fearsome creatures placed at the entrance to Eden and on the Ark of the Covenant.","def":"Cherubim (plural of cherub — but the singular is rarely used; Hebrew always uses the plural) are a class of divine beings who serve as guardians of God's sacred space. After the expulsion from Eden, God placed cherubim 'and a flaming sword flashing back and forth to guard the way to the tree of life' (Genesis 3:24). The Ark of the Covenant had two gold cherubim with outstretched wings, facing each other across the mercy seat (Exodus 25:18-22). God is described throughout the Psalms as 'enthroned between the cherubim' (Psalm 80:1, 99:1). Ezekiel 1 and 10 give the most detailed description: four-faced (lion, ox, eagle, man), four-winged, associated with fire and lightning.","kingdom":"The cherubim on the Ark stood over the mercy seat — the place where the high priest sprinkled blood on the Day of Atonement. The blood went on the mercy seat between the cherubim. Access to God's presence, guarded since Eden, was opened by blood. Every detail of the Ark's design was pointing to the cross."},"seraphim":{"hebrew":"שְׂרָפִים","translit":"serafim","strongs":"H8314","plain":"Six-winged angelic beings who stand before God's throne — they cover their faces before His glory and call out 'Holy, holy, holy.'","def":"Seraphim appear only once in the Bible in their full description — Isaiah 6:1-7. Isaiah sees the throne room of God, and 'above it stood the seraphim.' Each had six wings: with two they covered their faces (God's glory is too great to look at directly), with two they covered their feet (in the ancient Near East, covering one's feet before a superior was an act of deference), and with two they flew. They called to each other: 'Holy, holy, holy is the LORD Almighty; the whole earth is full of his glory.' The word seraph comes from a root meaning 'to burn' — these are burning ones.","kingdom":"The seraphim's cry — 'Holy, holy, holy' — is the only attribute of God that is stated three times consecutively. The triple repetition in Hebrew is the superlative of superlatives: God is holy in an absolute, incomparable way. Revelation 4:8 echoes this scene with four living creatures doing the same."},"hyssop":{"plain":"A small bushy plant used to apply blood and water in purification rituals — and associated with Passover and Jesus' crucifixion.","def":"Hyssop (Hebrew: ezov) was a small bushy herb used as a natural brush or sprinkler. At the first Passover, Israel was commanded to use a bunch of hyssop to apply the blood of the lamb to their doorposts (Exodus 12:22). It was used in the purification ritual for leprosy (Leviticus 14) and for cleansing from corpse defilement (Numbers 19). Psalm 51:7: 'Cleanse me with hyssop and I will be clean.' At the crucifixion, soldiers put a sponge soaked in sour wine on a hyssop stalk and offered it to Jesus (John 19:29). The detail connects the scene to Passover — the hyssop of application, the blood of the Lamb.","kingdom":"Hyssop is always an instrument, never the thing itself. It applies what purifies. The plant was never special — it was just what God chose to carry the blood from the basin to the doorpost. Small, common, and used by God for the most important act of that night."},"gall":{"plain":"An extremely bitter liquid — used as a painkiller or as an image of bitterness and suffering.","def":"Gall (Hebrew: rosh, Greek: chole) was a bitter substance — possibly from a bitter herb (like wormwood) or referring to bile (the digestive fluid from the liver). It was sometimes mixed with wine and offered as a painkiller or stupefying drink. Matthew 27:34: 'They offered him wine to drink, mixed with gall; but when he tasted it, he refused to drink it.' Jesus refused to dull the pain he was absorbing. The Old Testament uses gall and wormwood as images of deep bitterness: Lamentations 3:19, 'I remember my affliction and my wandering, the bitterness and the gall.'"},"frankincense":{"plain":"A precious white tree resin that burns with a sweet fragrance — used in worship and given as one of the Magi's gifts to Jesus.","def":"Frankincense (Hebrew: levonah, Greek: libanos) is a hard white resin obtained by cutting the bark of the Boswellia tree in Arabia, Somalia, and India. When burned, it produces a white smoke with a distinctive, clean, sweet fragrance. It was one of the four components of the Tabernacle incense (Exodus 30:34). It was added to grain offerings (Leviticus 2:1). The Magi brought frankincense as one of three gifts to the infant Jesus (Matthew 2:11) — scholars have traditionally seen this as a symbol of his priesthood (the priestly offering of prayer/intercession) alongside gold (king) and myrrh (death).","ane":"Frankincense was one of the most expensive trade goods of the ancient world — it came from the extreme south of the Arabian Peninsula (modern Yemen and Oman) and traveled by caravan. Temples throughout the Mediterranean world burned it. Its use in Israel's worship distinguished God's incense from other ancient cults through the specific formula and exclusive dedication."},"myrrh":{"plain":"A fragrant tree resin used in perfumes, anointing oil, and embalming — one of the most valuable substances in the ancient world, associated with both life and death.","def":"Myrrh (Hebrew: mor, Greek: smyrna) is a resin from the Commiphora tree — reddish-brown, bittersweet fragrant. It was used in the anointing oil formula (Exodus 30:23), in incense, in perfumes (Psalm 45:8, Song of Solomon 1:13), and in embalming. Joseph of Arimathea and Nicodemus wrapped Jesus' body with myrrh and aloes (John 19:39-40). The Magi brought myrrh as one of their three gifts — associated with burial and death from the beginning. Nicodemus brought '75 pounds' (!) of myrrh — the burial of a king.","kingdom":"The first gift of myrrh (Magi at birth) and the last application of myrrh (Nicodemus at burial) bracket Jesus' earthly life. He came to die. The fragrance that followed him was always both exquisite and mortal."},"wormwood":{"plain":"An extremely bitter plant — used as a symbol of bitter suffering, poison, and God's judgment.","def":"Wormwood (Hebrew: la'anah) is a bitter herb (Artemisia species) used as a metaphor for extreme bitterness and suffering. Proverbs 5:4 warns about the adulterous woman: 'but in the end she is as bitter as gall, as sharp as a double-edged sword.' Amos 5:7 accuses those who 'turn justice into bitterness (wormwood) and cast righteousness to the ground.' In Revelation 8:10-11, a star called 'Wormwood' falls into the waters and turns them bitter — judgment imagery for a third of earth's waters becoming undrinkable."},"balm":{"plain":"A fragrant, healing resin or ointment from trees in the Gilead region — famous enough that 'Balm of Gilead' became a byword for healing and comfort.","def":"Balm of Gilead (Hebrew: tsori) was a valuable aromatic resin from trees grown in the region east of the Jordan River. Used as a medicinal ointment, perfume, and trade good. When Jeremiah cries out over Israel's wound: 'Is there no balm in Gilead? Is there no physician there?' (Jeremiah 8:22), he's using the most famous healing substance of his day to ask why the people's spiritual wound isn't being healed. The 'Balm in Gilead' became a spiritual metaphor for Christ's healing — preserved in the African American spiritual tradition: 'There is a balm in Gilead to make the wounded whole.'"},"purification":{"plain":"The process of being made ritually clean — allowed back into God's presence or community after contact with death, disease, or sin.","def":"Purification (Hebrew: tohorah, Greek: katharismos) involved various rituals to restore 'cleanness' — the state of being permitted into God's presence and the community's life. Uncleanness could come from contact with the dead, skin diseases, bodily fluids, or certain foods. Purification involved washing, waiting periods, and sometimes sacrifice. John 2:6: the stone jars at Cana 'were standing there for the Jewish rites of purification, each holding 20-30 gallons' — Jesus turned all of that water into wine, a picture of the old purification system giving way to abundant covenant joy.","kingdom":"The New Testament consistently transfers purification from external ritual to internal transformation. Hebrews 9:13-14: 'The blood of goats and bulls...sanctify those who are ceremonially unclean...How much more, then, will the blood of Christ...cleanse our consciences from acts that lead to death.'"},"consecrated":{"_alias_of":"consecrate","plain":"Set apart and dedicated to God — declared holy and no longer available for ordinary use."},"defiled":{"plain":"Made ritually or morally unclean — no longer in the state required to approach God or participate in community worship.","def":"Defilement (Hebrew: tame — ritual uncleanness; chaneph — moral pollution) had two distinct senses in the Old Testament: (1) Ritual uncleanness — contact with certain things (corpses, skin diseases, bodily discharges) made a person 'unclean' and excluded from worship temporarily until purification rites were completed. (2) Moral defilement — idolatry, murder, and sexual sin defiled the person, the community, and even the land itself. Leviticus 18:24-25 says the land itself was 'defiled' by the sexual sins of the Canaanites and 'vomited them out.' Jesus cuts through the first category entirely in Mark 7:18-19: 'nothing that enters a person from the outside can defile them...he declared all foods clean.' What defiles is what comes from the heart."},"unclean":{"plain":"In the state of ritual or moral impurity — not permitted to enter the Tabernacle/Temple or participate in sacred assembly until made clean.","def":"Unclean (Hebrew: tame) was a ritual state — not necessarily moral failure — that excluded a person from sacred spaces and assembly. Contact with a dead body, certain skin conditions, bodily discharges, and other things made a person temporarily unclean. This was not shame or punishment but a system for protecting the holiness of God's dwelling. Leviticus 11-15 details the laws. Jesus regularly touched the 'unclean' — the leper, the dead, the woman with bleeding — and instead of becoming unclean himself, the unclean became clean at his touch.","kingdom":"The unclean laws pointed to a fundamental problem: humanity cannot approach a holy God in its natural state. The cross doesn't change the standard — it changes the standing."},"leprosy":{"plain":"A severe skin disease that made a person ritually unclean and excluded from community life — the biblical 'leper' was the most isolated person in ancient society.","def":"The Hebrew word tsara'at (Greek: lepra) covered a range of serious skin conditions — not necessarily identical to modern Hansen's disease (leprosy). Anyone diagnosed with tsara'at was declared 'unclean,' required to live outside the camp/city, wear torn clothes, and call out 'Unclean! Unclean!' whenever people approached (Leviticus 13:45-46). They were excluded from worship, community, family life, and trade. The disease was untreatable; only miraculous healing or priestly re-examination could restore a person to community.","kingdom":"When Jesus healed lepers and then told them to 'show themselves to the priests' (Luke 17:14), he was initiating the process of their restoration to full community life. The healing was not complete until they were declared clean by the priest and could go home. Jesus restored not just bodies but belonging."},"uncircumcised":{"plain":"Not circumcised — used to mean outside the covenant (literally or figuratively), or spiritually hard-hearted.","def":"Uncircumcised (Hebrew: arel) meant outside the covenant sign of Abraham — not belonging to God's covenant people. Gentiles were 'the uncircumcision' (Ephesians 2:11). But the prophets use 'uncircumcised' metaphorically: 'uncircumcised heart' (Leviticus 26:41, Jeremiah 9:26) means a heart that hasn't been cut — still hard and closed to God's covenant claim. Moses calls himself a man of 'uncircumcised lips' (Exodus 6:12, 6:30 KJV) — meaning he speaks with difficulty, his speech isn't 'cut' clean."},"intercession":{"plain":"Praying on behalf of someone else — standing in the gap between them and God, pleading their case.","def":"Intercession (Hebrew: palal — to pray, to intervene; Greek: entynchano — to meet with, to plead) is the act of bringing another person's need to God. Abraham intercedes for Sodom (Genesis 18:22-32). Moses intercedes for Israel repeatedly (Exodus 32:11-14, Numbers 14:13-19). Isaiah 53:12 says the Suffering Servant 'bore the sin of many, and made intercession for the transgressors.' Romans 8:26-27: the Holy Spirit intercedes for us. Romans 8:34 and Hebrews 7:25: Jesus 'always lives to make intercession' for those who come to God through him.","kingdom":"Intercession is the activity that never stops: the Spirit intercedes from within us, Jesus intercedes from the right hand of the Father, and we are called to intercede for others. The mechanism of prayer is not solo communication — it is woven into the fabric of the Trinity's own life."},"scapegoat":{"plain":"The goat that carried Israel's sins away into the wilderness on the Day of Atonement — the origin of our word 'scapegoat.'","def":"On the Day of Atonement (Yom Kippur — Leviticus 16), two goats were selected. One was sacrificed as a sin offering. The other — the 'scapegoat' (Hebrew: la-Azazel — 'for Azazel,' possibly meaning 'for removal' or referring to the wilderness) — had the high priest lay both hands on its head and confess over it all of Israel's sins. The goat was then led by a designated man into the wilderness and released, carrying Israel's sins away from the camp. The goat went to a land of 'separation' (Leviticus 16:22).","kingdom":"The two-goat ritual pictures two aspects of atonement: the blood of the first goat covers sin before God; the release of the second goat removes the sin from the community. Both are fulfilled in Christ — his blood covers, and his resurrection removes."},"Selah":{"hebrew":"סֶלָה","translit":"selah","strongs":"H5542","plain":"A musical notation in the Psalms — probably meaning 'pause,' 'lift up,' or 'interlude.' It marks a moment to stop, reflect, and let what was just sung sink in.","def":"Selah appears 74 times in the Psalms and 3 times in Habakkuk — always at musically or theologically significant points. The exact meaning is uncertain. The most likely interpretation: a musical direction to pause, to lift the voice, or to play an interlude. It functions like a comma of the soul — 'stop and think about that.' Selah follows the most weighty statements: after declarations of God's faithfulness, after cries from the depths, after covenant promises. Some scholars suggest it comes from salal (to lift up) or from calah (to pause).","kingdom":"Wherever Selah appears, the Psalm is telling you: don't rush past this. Let it land. Read the verse before every Selah as if someone just said something that requires a moment of silence."},"Hosanna":{"hebrew":"הוֹשִׁיעָה נָּא","translit":"hoshia na","strongs":"H3467","plain":"Originally a cry for help — 'Save us now, please!' — that became a shout of praise when directed at the one who actually came to save.","def":"Hosanna comes from the Hebrew hoshia na — literally 'save, please!' or 'save now!' It appears in Psalm 118:25 as a petition: 'LORD, save us! LORD, grant us success!' When the crowd cried 'Hosanna!' at Jesus' triumphal entry (Matthew 21:9), they were shouting this phrase from Psalm 118 — which was sung at Passover pilgrimages. They were shouting the ancient prayer for salvation at the one who came as the answer to that prayer. Over time, the petition-word became a praise-word because the savior had come.","kingdom":"The crowd at the Palm Sunday entry quoted Psalm 118 and shouted Hosanna — but they misread the kind of salvation Jesus was bringing. They wanted political liberation. He came to bring the salvation the word originally meant: rescue from death, sin, and separation from God."},"Amen":{"hebrew":"אָמֵן","translit":"amen","strongs":"H543","plain":"A word of affirmation meaning 'truly,' 'so be it,' 'this is certain.' When said at the end of a prayer it means 'I agree and trust this is real.'","def":"Amen (Hebrew: amen, from aman — to be firm, faithful, reliable) is an affirmation of truth and trust. It means 'this is solid, this is certain, I stand on this.' When said at the end of a blessing or prayer it means 'so be it — I trust this is true.' In the Old Testament it was a congregational response affirming a covenant statement (Deuteronomy 27:15-26). Jesus uses it uniquely: 'Truly (amen) I say to you' — placing amen at the BEGINNING of his statements rather than the end, declaring his own words to be certain before he says them. Revelation 3:14 calls Jesus 'the Amen, the faithful and true witness' — he is himself the embodiment of what the word means.","kingdom":"When you say 'Amen' at the end of a prayer you're doing more than finishing. You're saying: I believe God is who He says He is, that what was just prayed is real, and that I stand in agreement with it."},"lyre":{"plain":"A small harp-like stringed instrument — one of the main instruments in ancient Hebrew worship. David played one before Saul.","def":"The lyre (Hebrew: kinnor) was a portable stringed instrument with a wooden frame and strings plucked by hand or a plectrum. It was the instrument David played to soothe Saul (1 Samuel 16:23) and the instrument he played and danced before the ark (2 Samuel 6:5). The kinnor was carried by the Levitical musicians and was the instrument of joyful worship. Psalm 33:2: 'Praise the LORD with the lyre; make music to him on the ten-stringed harp.'"},"harp":{"plain":"A larger stringed instrument held upright — used in Temple worship by the Levites. In Revelation the elders and the 144,000 hold harps.","def":"The harp (Hebrew: nevel) was a larger stringed instrument than the kinnor/lyre — probably a larger angular harp with more strings, held upright and played with both hands. Together the kinnor and nevel were the core instruments of Levitical temple music. In Revelation 5:8 and 14:2, the heavenly worship includes harps — 'the sound of harpists playing their harps' and golden bowls of incense which are the prayers of the saints. The harp in heaven suggests that earthly worship is continuous with heavenly worship."},"tambourine":{"plain":"A small hand-held drum with jingling disks — used in celebration, dance, and worship throughout the Bible.","def":"The tambourine (Hebrew: tof) was a hand drum — a circular frame with a skin stretched over it, sometimes with metal rings that jingled. Used primarily in celebrations and processions. Miriam took a tambourine and led the women in dance after crossing the Red Sea (Exodus 15:20). Jephthah's daughter came out with tambourines to celebrate his return (Judges 11:34). Psalm 150:4: 'Praise him with tambourine and dancing.'"},"flute":{"plain":"A wind instrument made from reed, bone, or wood — associated with both mourning and celebration in ancient Israel.","def":"Flute (Hebrew: chalil, Greek: aulos) was a reed pipe or flute — an instrument associated with both celebrations and mourning depending on context. Matthew 11:17: 'We played the flute for you, and you did not dance; we sang a dirge, and you did not mourn.' The same instrument played festive music and funeral dirges. Matthew 9:23 mentions 'flute players' at a funeral. Revelation 18:22: 'The music of harpists and musicians, pipers (flutists) and trumpeters will never be heard in you again' — describing Babylon's cultural death."},"trumpet":{"plain":"A metal instrument used to call Israel to assembly, signal battle, and mark sacred moments — and in Revelation, the trumpets of heaven signal the unfolding of the end.","def":"The trumpet (Hebrew: shofar — ram's horn; also chatsotsrah — a metal trumpet) was used for multiple purposes: calling the assembly (Numbers 10:2), signaling battle (Judges 6:34), announcing the new moon and feasts (Numbers 10:10), and heralding the presence of God (Exodus 19:16-19 — the Lord descended on Sinai to the sound of a very loud trumpet). In Revelation, seven trumpets announce successive judgments (Revelation 8-11). The final trumpet (1 Corinthians 15:52, 1 Thessalonians 4:16) signals the resurrection of the dead.","kingdom":"The trumpet was always announcement — something is about to happen or has happened. Every trumpet blast in Scripture says: pay attention, the King is acting."},"exile":{"plain":"Being forced to leave your homeland and live in another country under foreign rule — the most severe punishment in ancient Israel, carried out by Assyria and Babylon.","def":"Exile (Hebrew: golah, galut) was the forced removal of a population from their land to a foreign country. The northern kingdom (Israel) was exiled to Assyria in 722 BC. The southern kingdom (Judah) was exiled to Babylon in 605-586 BC. The experience of exile — loss of land, temple, king, and national identity — reshaped Jewish theology and produced the prophetic literature of Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel, and Daniel. The return from exile (Ezra, Nehemiah) was understood as the fulfillment of covenant promise.","kingdom":"Paul and Peter describe all believers as 'strangers and exiles' on earth (1 Peter 2:11, Hebrews 11:13). The entire Christian life is a theology of exile — we are in the world but not of it, awaiting a return to our true country."},"inheritance":{"plain":"What is passed down from a parent to a child — property, land, or covenant standing. In the Bible, the whole earth is the inheritance God is giving his children.","def":"Inheritance (Hebrew: nachalah — permanent possession, an allotted portion) in ancient Israel meant the land allotted to each tribe and family by God through Joshua — a permanent possession that couldn't be permanently sold (Leviticus 25:23-28). It represented belonging, identity, and God's faithfulness. The Psalms use inheritance language for God himself: 'The LORD is my portion and my cup; you make my lot secure. The boundary lines have fallen for me in pleasant places; surely I have a delightful inheritance' (Psalm 16:5-6).","kingdom":"The New Testament expands inheritance to the whole new creation. Romans 8:17: 'and if children, then heirs — heirs of God and co-heirs with Christ.' Revelation 21:7: 'The one who is victorious will inherit all this, and I will be their God and they will be my children.' The land promise to Abraham was never only about Canaan."},"firstborn":{"plain":"The first child born to a mother — in ancient Israel, the firstborn son had a special inheritance, led the family, and belonged in a unique sense to God.","def":"The firstborn (Hebrew: bechor) held a specific legal and spiritual status. He received a double portion of the inheritance (Deuteronomy 21:17). He held family leadership. Most significantly: after the Passover (when God struck down Egypt's firstborn), all of Israel's firstborn males were declared to belong to God and had to be 'redeemed' (bought back with a sacrifice — Numbers 3:12-13). Every Israelite father redeemed his firstborn son from God's claim because the Levites were given as God's portion instead.","kingdom":"Jesus is called 'the firstborn over all creation' (Colossians 1:15), 'the firstborn among many brothers' (Romans 8:29), and 'the firstborn from the dead' (Revelation 1:5). All three dimensions of the firstborn status — inheritance, leadership, and belonging to God — are concentrated in him."},"synagogue":{"plain":"A Jewish gathering place for prayer, Scripture reading, and teaching — the institution that replaced the Temple after the exile. Jesus regularly taught in synagogues.","def":"Synagogue (Greek: synagoge — 'gathering place') developed during the Babylonian exile when Israel had no Temple. Communities began gathering for prayer and Scripture reading in local meeting houses. By the first century, virtually every Jewish community had a synagogue. The service included readings from the Torah and Prophets, a sermon, and prayers. The ruler of the synagogue (archisynagogos) oversaw the order and invited speakers. Jesus regularly attended and taught in synagogues (Luke 4:16: 'as was his custom, he went to the synagogue on the Sabbath day'). Paul's missionary strategy was always 'to the synagogue first.'"},"Sanhedrin":{"plain":"The supreme Jewish court in Jerusalem — 71 members including chief priests, elders, and scribes. They had authority over religious law and tried Jesus before sending him to Pilate.","def":"The Sanhedrin (from Greek: synedrion — 'sitting together') was the highest Jewish legal and religious authority. It had 71 members: the high priest presiding, along with chief priests, elders, and scribes. They ruled on religious law, controlled the Temple, and had authority over the Jewish community under Roman occupation. The Sanhedrin tried Jesus (Matthew 26:57-68, Mark 14:55-65), Stephen (Acts 6:12-7:60), and Paul (Acts 22-23). Roman authority limited their power: they could not execute anyone, which is why they sent Jesus to Pilate."},"leaven":{"plain":"Yeast or sourdough starter that makes bread rise — used in the Bible as a picture of something that spreads through and changes everything it touches, for good or bad.","def":"Leaven (Hebrew: chametz, Greek: zyme) was the fermenting agent — sourdough starter, yeast — that causes bread to rise. God commanded unleavened bread for Passover (no leaven in the house — Exodus 12:15-20) because Israel was leaving in haste and leaven represented the old Egyptian life being left behind. Jesus uses leaven twice: (1) negatively — 'Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees' (Matthew 16:6) — their hypocrisy spreads through and corrupts. (2) positively — 'The kingdom of heaven is like leaven that a woman took and hid in three measures of flour, till it was all leavened' (Matthew 13:33) — the kingdom spreads invisibly and transforms everything.","kingdom":"Paul uses leaven in 1 Corinthians 5:6-8: 'A little leaven leavens the whole lump. Cleanse out the old leaven...Christ, our Passover lamb, has been sacrificed. Let us therefore celebrate the festival, not with the old leaven...but with the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth.' The Passover command to remove leaven becomes a picture of the new life in Christ."},"unleavened":{"plain":"Without yeast — flat bread made quickly without rising. The bread of Passover and the Feast of Unleavened Bread — symbolizing the hasty departure from Egypt and a break from the old life.","def":"Unleavened bread (Hebrew: matzah) was bread baked without fermentation. At Passover, Israel ate unleavened bread because there was no time to let bread rise before fleeing Egypt (Exodus 12:39). The week-long Feast of Unleavened Bread followed Passover. Removing all leaven from the house was a physical act of covenant renewal — the old fermented life of Egypt was purged. Paul calls believers to live as 'unleavened' — a new batch, free from the old leaven of malice and evil (1 Corinthians 5:7-8)."},"vow":{"plain":"A solemn promise made directly to God — binding yourself to do or give something if God acts. Much more serious than a casual promise.","def":"A vow (Hebrew: neder) was a solemn promise made directly to God — binding in a way ordinary promises were not. 'When you make a vow to the LORD your God, do not be slow to pay it, for the LORD your God will certainly demand it of you and you will be guilty of sin. But if you refrain from making a vow, you will not be guilty' (Deuteronomy 23:21-22). Jacob vowed at Bethel (Genesis 28:20-22). Hannah vowed Samuel to God (1 Samuel 1:11). The Nazirite vow (Numbers 6) was a temporary vow of consecration. Ecclesiastes 5:4-5: 'Better not to vow than to vow and not fulfill it.'","kingdom":"Jesus doesn't forbid vows but raises the standard: 'All you need to say is simply 'Yes' or 'No'...anything beyond this comes from the evil one' (Matthew 5:37). The implication is that your ordinary speech should carry the weight of a vow — that you are the kind of person whose word is reliable."},"vows":{"_alias_of":"vow","plain":"Plural of vow — solemn promises made directly to God."},"tithes":{"plain":"Giving a tenth of your income or harvest to God — the standard of giving in ancient Israel and referenced in the New Testament.","def":"Tithes (Hebrew: ma'aser — tenth part) were a 10% giving of crops, livestock, and later income, designated for the support of the Levites and the needs of the Temple and community. Malachi 3:10: 'Bring the whole tithe into the storehouse, that there may be food in my house.' Abraham tithed to Melchizedek before the law existed (Genesis 14:20), establishing it as a pre-law covenant principle. Jesus acknowledged tithing while correcting its priority: 'You give a tenth of your spices...but you have neglected the more important matters of the law — justice, mercy and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter, without neglecting the former' (Matthew 23:23).","kingdom":"Paul doesn't use tithe language in the New Testament's giving instructions — he uses 'whatever you have decided in your heart to give' and 'whoever sows generously will also reap generously' (2 Corinthians 9:6-7). Some read this as liberating (the 10% floor is replaced by Spirit-led generosity); others read it as raising the standard (the NT calls for even more)."},"blameless":{"plain":"Without fault or defect — describing someone who is fully committed to God and cannot be legitimately accused of dishonesty or unfaithfulness.","def":"Blameless (Hebrew: tamim — whole, complete, without defect; Greek: amemptos — without blame) does not mean sinless or perfect. It means integrity — being the same person inside and out, not double-dealing, fully committed. Job is described as 'blameless and upright, one who feared God and turned away from evil' (Job 1:1) — yet he still needed God's mercy. Abraham is called to 'walk before me and be blameless' (Genesis 17:1) — a call to wholehearted covenant faithfulness.","kingdom":"Philippians 2:15: 'that you may be blameless and pure, children of God without fault in a warped and crooked generation.' Jude 24: 'to him who is able to keep you from stumbling and to present you before his glorious presence without fault.' The goal of salvation is not minimal compliance but full presentation before God without fault."},"upright":{"plain":"Living straight and honestly — oriented toward God and right living, without crookedness or deception.","def":"Upright (Hebrew: yashar — straight, level; yesharim — the upright ones) describes a person whose life is oriented correctly — toward God and justice. The contrast is with the 'crooked' (iqqesh) or the 'wicked' (rasha). Psalm 1 sets up the foundational contrast: the way of the righteous and the way of the wicked. Proverbs 2:7: 'He stores up sound wisdom for the upright; he is a shield to those who walk in integrity.' The uprightness referred to is not stiffness or self-righteousness — it is alignment with reality as God has ordered it."},"prudent":{"plain":"Wise in a practical sense — knowing how to act well in real situations, not just knowing good principles in theory.","def":"Prudent (Hebrew: bin — discerning, understanding; sakal — wise, prudent; Greek: phronimos — sensible, practical) describes the person who knows how to navigate life with wisdom. Proverbs values prudence highly: Proverbs 14:8: 'The prudence of the prudent is to understand their way.' Proverbs 12:23: 'A prudent man conceals knowledge, but the heart of fools proclaims folly.' Jesus calls his followers to be 'shrewd (prudent) as snakes and innocent as doves' (Matthew 10:16) — he values practical wisdom as part of faithfulness."},"reproof":{"plain":"A correction that points out error — given by a person or by God, with the goal of setting things right.","def":"Reproof (Hebrew: tokachath — argument, reproof, correction; Greek: elegchos — test, proof, conviction) is purposeful correction aimed at change. Proverbs 1:23: 'Repent at my reproof! I will pour out my thoughts to you, I will make known to you my teachings.' Proverbs 15:31-32: 'Whoever heeds life-giving correction will be at home among the wise. Those who disregard discipline despise themselves, but the one who heeds correction gains understanding.' 2 Timothy 3:16: Scripture is useful for 'reproof' — the Spirit uses Scripture to convict and correct."},"integrity":{"plain":"Being the same person in private as you are in public — what you say matching what you do, inside matching outside.","def":"Integrity (Hebrew: tom — wholeness, completeness; tamim — blameless) describes the quality of being undivided — no gap between your public face and your private life, no contradiction between your words and your actions. Job's integrity is specifically what Satan attacks: 'Does Job fear God for nothing?...Stretch out your hand and strike everything he has, and he will surely curse you to your face' (Job 1:9-11). When Job loses everything but maintains his integrity, he confounds the accusation that godliness is merely self-interest.","kingdom":"Psalm 15:2 describes the citizen of God's kingdom as one who 'speaks truth in his heart' — integrity starts on the inside before it reaches the mouth."},"steadfast":{"plain":"Firm, unwavering, and unshakeable — especially describing God's covenant love that never changes regardless of circumstances.","def":"Steadfast (Hebrew: chesed — loyal covenant love; emun/emunah — faithfulness; Greek: bebaios — firm, reliable) describes an unshakeable quality. When applied to God's love, it is often translated 'steadfast love' or 'lovingkindness' (chesed) — the love that is not moved by performance, failure, or circumstance. Psalm 136 repeats 26 times: 'His steadfast love endures forever.' Isaiah 54:10: 'Though the mountains be shaken and the hills be removed, yet my unfailing (steadfast) love for you will not be shaken.' When applied to believers, it means the unwavering trust and commitment that does not move regardless of opposition."},"contrite":{"plain":"Broken and crushed in spirit — the state of genuine humility before God, knowing you've done wrong and holding nothing back from him.","def":"Contrite (Hebrew: dakah — crushed, broken; nidkah — contrite) describes a person who has been broken open before God — no pretense, no self-defense, genuinely humbled. Psalm 51:17: 'My sacrifice, O God, is a broken spirit; a broken and contrite heart you, God, will not despise.' Isaiah 66:2: 'These are the ones I look on with favor: those who are humble and contrite in spirit, and who tremble at my word.' The contrite person is exactly the person God comes toward — the opposite of the proud.","kingdom":"Jesus begins the Beatitudes with 'Blessed are the poor in spirit' (Matthew 5:3) — the contrite, the spiritually bankrupt who know they have nothing to bring. The kingdom begins at this place of emptiness."},"meek":{"plain":"Humble and gentle — not weak or passive, but power under control. Moses was the meekest man on earth, and he also parted the Red Sea.","def":"Meek (Hebrew: anav — humble, afflicted; Greek: praus — gentle, tame) does not mean weak or spineless. In Greek culture, praus described a horse that had been trained — its power was real but under the direction of the rider. Moses is described as 'very meek, more than all the people on earth' (Numbers 12:3 ESV) — but Moses confronted Pharaoh, led a nation, and stood before God. Jesus describes himself as 'gentle (meek) and humble in heart' (Matthew 11:29). The Beatitude: 'Blessed are the meek, for they will inherit the earth' (Matthew 5:5) — quotes Psalm 37:11, promising the land to those who don't grasp for it.","kingdom":"Meekness and humility are not the opposite of strength — they are strength oriented correctly. The meek person doesn't have to prove anything, grasp anything, or protect their reputation. They can afford to be gentle because they know who they are."},"parable":{"plain":"A short story drawn from everyday life that teaches a spiritual truth — the standard teaching method of Jesus.","def":"A parable (Greek: parabole — a comparison placed alongside; Hebrew: mashal — a comparison, proverb, teaching story) is a short narrative drawn from familiar life used to illuminate a deeper truth. Jesus taught in parables extensively — Matthew 13:34: 'Jesus always used stories and illustrations like these when speaking to the crowds. In fact, he never taught them without using parables.' When disciples asked why he taught in parables, Jesus quoted Isaiah 6:9-10 — parables both reveal (to those with ears to hear) and conceal (from those who have closed their hearts). The parable is a mirror: you see in it what you are ready to see.","kingdom":"Every parable is an invitation to enter a story and find yourself in it. The question is always which character you identify with — and whether you're willing to have your identification challenged."},"oracle":{"plain":"A divine message or pronouncement — usually a prophecy or revelation from God delivered through a prophet.","def":"Oracle (Hebrew: massa — burden, utterance; Greek: logion — divine utterance) was a formal prophetic pronouncement. The word massa also means 'burden' — a prophetic oracle was something the prophet carried and was compelled to deliver. Many prophetic books open or divide with oracles: 'The oracle of the word of the LORD' (Malachi 1:1), 'The oracle concerning Babylon which Isaiah son of Amoz saw' (Isaiah 13:1). An oracle was not opinion or human wisdom — it was a word received from God, often against the prophet's own preferences (Jeremiah is a clear example)."},"trance":{"plain":"A state of being caught up in a spiritual vision — the person's normal consciousness suspended while they receive a divine revelation.","def":"Trance (Greek: ekstasis — 'out of standing,' being outside oneself) describes a state of visionary absorption. Peter 'fell into a trance' (Acts 10:10) and saw the vision of the sheet with clean and unclean animals. Paul describes being 'caught up to the third heaven' (2 Corinthians 12:2-4) — whether in body or out of body, he didn't know. John describes being 'in the Spirit' (Revelation 1:10, 4:2) as the setting for his visions. The prophets experienced similar states — Ezekiel describes being 'lifted up' and transported to Jerusalem (Ezekiel 8:3). These are not sleep-states or imagination — they are divinely initiated states of perception beyond ordinary consciousness."},"vain":{"plain":"Empty, hollow, and purposeless — not 'proud' (the modern meaning). Vanity in the Bible means things that look like they matter but ultimately don't last.","def":"Vain (Hebrew: shav — emptiness, worthlessness; also hevel — breath, vapor; Greek: mataios — futile, empty) means empty or futile — not 'conceited' as modern English uses the word. Ecclesiastes is built on hevel: 'Vanity of vanities, all is vanity' — better translated 'vapor of vapors, everything is vapor.' It's about impermanence and the limitations of life under the sun. To 'take God's name in vain' (Exodus 20:7) means to use it emptily — without weight or meaning, as if it were nothing.","kingdom":"The antidote to vanity in Ecclesiastes is not finding better earthly pursuits — it is fearing God (Ecclesiastes 12:13-14). What lasts is what is oriented toward the eternal."},"idolatry":{"plain":"Giving the devotion, trust, or worship that belongs to God to something else — whether a carved image, a concept, a relationship, or a goal.","def":"Idolatry (Greek: eidololatria — image-worship) was the primary sin the prophets confronted. In its concrete form it involved carved statues representing other gods — Baal, Molech, Asherah. But the prophets and Paul consistently extend the concept beyond statues. Colossians 3:5: 'Put to death...covetousness, which is idolatry.' 1 John 5:21: 'Dear children, keep yourselves from idols.' Any person, object, pursuit, or idea that occupies the place of ultimate devotion, trust, and identity that belongs to God is an idol — even good things become idols when they become ultimate things.","kingdom":"Augustine's formulation remains the best: 'You have made us for yourself, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.' Idolatry is the restless heart's attempt to find rest in the wrong place. Every idol ultimately betrays — it cannot bear the weight of what only God can carry."},"Baal":{"plain":"The storm and fertility god of the Canaanites — the main rival to the LORD throughout the period of the Judges and the monarchy. His name means 'master' or 'owner.'","def":"Baal (Hebrew: ba'al — lord, master, owner) was the chief deity of Canaan and Phoenicia — a storm and fertility god represented holding a lightning bolt. His worship involved cult prostitution, child sacrifice, and ecstatic religious practices. The confrontation between Elijah and the 450 prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel (1 Kings 18) was the central showdown of the covenant era: which deity controls the rain (the source of life), the LORD or Baal? The 400 prophets of Asherah who were also at Carmel (verse 19) show the two major Canaanite deities together.","ane":"In Canaanite mythology, Baal was the son of El and the king of the gods. He died and was resurrected seasonally — his death explained the dry season, his return explained the rain. Israel's temptation to Baal worship was not irrational — it was an agricultural culture that needed rain, and Baal was the regional specialist. The LORD's answer through Elijah was to shut the rain for three years and then open it in one moment at his word."},"Asherah":{"plain":"A Canaanite fertility goddess — the female counterpart to Baal. Asherah poles (carved wooden posts) were set up beside altars throughout Israel and strongly condemned by the prophets.","def":"Asherah was the mother goddess of Canaan — also called Astarte or Ashtoreth in different contexts. She was often represented by a carved wooden post (asherah pole) set up beside altars and in high places. The repeated formula in the Books of Kings and Chronicles: 'he did what was evil in the sight of the LORD...and made an Asherah' indicates how common this was in Israel and Judah. Josiah's reform specifically included cutting down Asherah poles (2 Kings 23:14).","ane":"Asherah was one of the most widely worshiped deities in the ancient Near East. Archaeological finds include hundreds of small female fertility figurines throughout Canaan and Israel — showing how widespread the cult was even among the covenant people. Some ancient inscriptions even reference 'the LORD and his Asherah' — evidence of syncretism where Asherah was being paired with YHWH."},"Molech":{"plain":"An Ammonite deity to whom children were burned as sacrifices — one of the most severely condemned practices in all of Scripture.","def":"Molech (also Moloch) was an Ammonite deity whose worship involved passing children 'through the fire' — burning them alive as an offering (Leviticus 18:21, 20:1-5). God calls this 'detestable' (to'evah — abomination) in the strongest terms. The Valley of Hinnom (Ge-Hinnom/Gehenna) outside Jerusalem was specifically where Israelite kings like Ahaz (2 Kings 16:3) and Manasseh (2 Kings 21:6) made their children 'pass through the fire.' This is why Jeremiah and Jesus use the Valley of Hinnom as their image for divine judgment — it was already the site of the worst human evil imaginable.","kingdom":"God's absolute prohibition of Molech worship is connected to his own fatherhood: 'I would never have commanded such a thing, nor did it enter my mind, that they should do this detestable thing' (Jeremiah 32:35). The God who does not withhold his own Son contrasts absolutely with the god who demands the death of yours."},"majesty":{"plain":"The overpowering greatness and dignity of God — the quality that produces awe and reverence, not just admiration.","def":"Majesty (Hebrew: hod — splendor; hadar — honor; gaon — elevation; Greek: megaloprepeia — befitting greatness; megalosyne — greatness) describes the overwhelming quality of God's greatness. It is not just 'impressive' but reality-altering — the quality that makes the seraphim cover their faces (Isaiah 6:2) and John fall as dead (Revelation 1:17). Psalm 96:6: 'Splendor and majesty are before him; strength and glory are in his sanctuary.' 2 Peter 1:17: at the Transfiguration, Jesus received 'honor and glory from God the Father when the voice came to him from the Majestic Glory.'"},"sovereignty":{"plain":"Having supreme authority over everything — the quality of being ultimate ruler, accountable to no one higher. Only God possesses this fully.","def":"Sovereignty (from Latin: superanus — above all) is not a Hebrew/Greek word but expresses a biblical concept: God's supreme, uncontested authority over all things. Psalm 115:3: 'Our God is in heaven; he does whatever pleases him.' Daniel 4:35: 'He does as he pleases with the powers of heaven and the peoples of the earth. No one can hold back his hand or say to him: 'What have you done?'' Romans 9:21: 'Does not the potter have the right over the clay?' God's sovereignty is not tyranny — it is the authority of the one who made everything, owns everything, and is working all things toward his good purposes.","kingdom":"The sovereignty of God is the foundation of Christian hope. If God is not sovereign, prayer is uncertain, promises are conditional on circumstances, and history has no guaranteed end. Because he is sovereign, every promise is certain and every prayer reaches the one who has the power to answer."},"diadem":{"plain":"A royal crown or headband — a symbol of kingship and authority.","def":"A diadem (Hebrew: tsaniph — a turban/headband; Greek: diadema — a royal band worn around the head) was a royal headpiece. Different from a garland (given to an honored guest) — the diadem was specifically a kingly crown. In Revelation 12:3, the dragon has 'seven diadems on his heads' — claiming seven royal authorities. Revelation 19:12: the rider on the white horse has 'many diadems on his head' — outranking every other authority. Isaiah 62:3: 'You will be a crown of splendor in the LORD's hand, a royal diadem in the hand of your God.'"},"decree":{"plain":"An official order from a king or ruler — binding on all subjects, not open for negotiation.","def":"A decree (Hebrew: paqad — command; gezerah — a decree cut/decided; Greek: dogma — that which seems right, an edict) was an authoritative legal command from a ruler that carried the force of law. Daniel's companions faced execution because they violated Nebuchadnezzar's decree to worship the golden image (Daniel 3). Esther's story turns on the unchangeable nature of Persian decrees (Esther 1:19, 8:8) — 'which cannot be revoked.' Luke 2:1: 'In those days a decree went out from Caesar Augustus that all the world should be registered.' God's decrees — the laws of creation, covenant commands, prophetic announcements — carry the authority of the universe's supreme ruler."},"statute":{"plain":"A specific law or rule — one of the detailed regulations in God's covenant with Israel, covering how to live in every area of life.","def":"Statute (Hebrew: choq — a decree, a prescribed limit; chuqqah — a statute, especially a cultic regulation) was one of the specific covenant regulations God gave Israel. The Psalms speak of delighting in God's statutes: Psalm 119:16: 'I delight in your statutes; I will not neglect your word.' Statutes covered worship practices, social laws, purity, feasts, and more. The word choq comes from chaqaq — to cut, engrave, decree — suggesting something carved in permanence."},"testimony":{"plain":"Evidence or witness — in the Bible, often referring to God's covenant law as his testimony about himself and his requirements.","def":"Testimony (Hebrew: eduth — testimony, witness; Greek: martyrion — witness, testimony) has two main uses. (1) Legal: a witness's statement of what they saw or know. (2) Covenant: the 'Testimony' often refers to the two stone tablets of the Ten Commandments (the Ark of the Covenant was the 'Ark of the Testimony' — Exodus 25:22), or to the law as God's witness about himself and his covenant. Psalm 119 uses 'testimonies' repeatedly for God's covenant instructions. Revelation 12:11: believers overcome 'by the word of their testimony' — their witness to what God has done."},"rebuke":{"plain":"A sharp correction or reproof — telling someone firmly that they are wrong. God rebukes, parents rebuke, and the wise receive rebuke as a gift.","def":"Rebuke (Hebrew: ga'ar — to rebuke, to threaten; Greek: epitimao — to charge, to rebuke) is a forceful correction. God rebukes the sea (Psalm 106:9), the nations (Psalm 9:5), and Satan (Zechariah 3:2, Jude 9). Jesus rebukes fever (Luke 4:39), winds (Mark 4:39), and Peter (Matthew 16:23). Proverbs 9:8: 'Do not rebuke mockers or they will hate you; rebuke the wise and they will love you.' 2 Timothy 4:2: 'Preach the word; be prepared in season and out of season; correct, rebuke and encourage — with great patience and careful instruction.'"},"divination":{"plain":"Seeking hidden knowledge or the future through spiritual means other than God — condemned throughout Scripture because it involves sources outside God's authority.","def":"Divination (Hebrew: qesem — lot-casting, consulting omens; Greek: manteia — prophetic power) is the practice of seeking information from the spiritual world through unauthorized means. It covers a wide range of practices — astrology, casting lots with spiritual intent, reading omens, consulting spirits. Deuteronomy 18:10-14 lists eight specific types that are forbidden (see Deuteronomy 18:10-12). The problem is not primarily the information sought but the source: any spiritual power that operates outside God's covenant is a power that has its own agenda.","kingdom":"There is a stark contrast throughout the Bible between genuine prophecy (God speaking through chosen vessels within covenant relationship) and divination (humans extracting information from spiritual powers outside that relationship). The difference is always traced to source, authorization, and accountability."},"sorcery":{"plain":"Using supernatural means — often substances, potions, or spells — to produce effects in the physical world or influence people, apart from God.","def":"Sorcery (Hebrew: mechashef — from kashaf, to whisper/to use spells; Greek: pharmakeia — use of drugs, sorcery) involved using materials, chants, or rituals to harness supernatural power for personal ends. The Greek word pharmakeia (from pharmakon — drug, poison) is the root of the modern word 'pharmacy' — sorcery in the ancient world often involved using substances with psychoactive or toxic effects as part of ritual practice. Galatians 5:20 lists pharmakeia as a 'work of the flesh.' Revelation 21:8: 'sorcerers' are among those excluded from the New Jerusalem.","ane":"Sorcery was one of the main professions in Babylon and Egypt — the Babylonian empire specifically trained specialists in it (Daniel 2:2). The context of Exodus is partly a contest between Moses and the Egyptian sorcerers (Exodus 7:11) — their sorcery has real but limited power; God's power is unlimited and needs no technique."},"witchcraft":{"plain":"Harnessing spiritual power for personal ends through forbidden means — associated in the Bible with rebellion and operating outside God's authority.","def":"Witchcraft (Hebrew: keshafim — witchcraft, sorcery; Greek: pharmakeia) covers practices involving the manipulation of spiritual powers through ritual, formula, or substance. 1 Samuel 15:23 uses it in a powerful comparison: 'For rebellion is as the sin of witchcraft (divination), and stubbornness is as iniquity and idolatry.' Saul's rebellion against God's command is called as serious as witchcraft — not because they're identical but because both involve substituting your own authority for God's.","kingdom":"The consistent biblical position is not that spiritual powers outside God don't exist or have no effect — it's that accessing them is dangerous, corrupting, and a fundamental act of unfaithfulness to the covenant. You are bringing yourself under the authority of powers that are not under God's authority."},"sanctified":{"plain":"Set apart and made holy — the process of becoming more like who God says you already are in Christ.","def":"Sanctified (Hebrew: qadash — made holy; Greek: hagiazo — set apart, made holy) describes both a positional reality and a progressive process. In one sense, all believers are sanctified — declared holy by God's act (1 Corinthians 6:11: 'you were sanctified'). In another sense, sanctification is ongoing — 'May God himself, the God of peace, sanctify you through and through' (1 Thessalonians 5:23). Both senses are true simultaneously: you are already sanctified in Christ, and you are being sanctified by the Spirit working in your life.","kingdom":"The tension between 'already sanctified' and 'being sanctified' is a tension the New Testament fully holds: 'You are not your own; you were bought at a price' (1 Corinthians 6:19-20) — so live that way. The command to be holy rests on the declaration that you already are."},"justified":{"plain":"Officially declared 'in the right' before God — a legal verdict, not a self-improvement process. You are justified by faith, not works.","def":"Justified (Greek: dikaioo — to declare righteous, to acquit) is a forensic term — legal language. It does not mean 'made righteous in behavior' (that's sanctification) but 'declared righteous in verdict.' Romans 3:24: 'and all are justified freely by his grace through the redemption that came by Christ Jesus.' Romans 5:1: 'Therefore, since we have been justified through faith, we have peace with God.' The verdict is final, declared on the basis of Jesus' righteousness credited to us — not our performance.","kingdom":"Luther's recovery of justification by faith was the crack in the medieval church that opened the Reformation. The question 'how is a person made right with God?' is answered differently by works-based systems (by performing, improving, satisfying requirements) and by the New Testament (by receiving what Christ accomplished — once, finally, as a gift)."},"adoption":{"plain":"Being taken into God's family as a full child with all the rights of inheritance — not a lesser status but full sonship.","def":"Adoption (Greek: huiothesia — placing as a son) is Paul's term for the believer's legal standing in God's family. Romans 8:15: 'You received the Spirit of adoption to sonship (huiothesia). And by him we cry, 'Abba, Father.'' Galatians 4:5: 'to redeem those under the law, that we might receive adoption to sonship.' In Roman law, adoption was a full legal transaction — the adopted child had all the rights of a biological child, including full inheritance rights. There was no second-tier status.","kingdom":"'Abba' (Mark 14:36, Romans 8:15, Galatians 4:6) is the Aramaic intimate term for father — used by children of their fathers in everyday speech. Paul says the Spirit prompts us to cry 'Abba, Father' — not just 'God' or 'Lord' but the intimate family name. This is the ground level of Christian identity: child of the Father."},"baptize":{"plain":"To immerse in water — the covenant act of initiation into Christ, declaring death to the old self and resurrection into new life.","def":"Baptize (Greek: baptizo — to immerse, to dip, to plunge) was the public act of entering the covenant community. John's baptism was 'a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins' (Mark 1:4) — a preparatory rite. Christian baptism was into the name of Jesus (Acts 2:38) or the Trinity (Matthew 28:19) — a declaration of ownership and identity. Romans 6:3-4: 'All of us who were baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into his death...buried with him through baptism into death in order that, just as Christ was raised from the dead through the glory of the Father, we too may live a new life.'","kingdom":"Baptism is a physical enactment of the gospel: going under the water is dying with Christ; coming up is rising with him. It is not what saves — Paul repeatedly distinguishes the sign from the reality (1 Corinthians 1:17, Romans 4:11). But it is the appointed declaration of what has happened."},"devout":{"plain":"Genuinely and consistently dedicated to God in daily life — the quality of someone whose religious commitment is real and practical, not just ceremonial.","def":"Devout (Greek: eulabes — taking hold of what is good, careful in religion; eusebeia — piety toward God; Greek: sebomai — to worship, to be devout) describes sincere, practiced godliness. Simeon in the Temple was 'righteous and devout' (Luke 2:25). Cornelius the Roman centurion was 'devout and God-fearing' (Acts 10:2) — 'he gave generously to those in need and prayed to God regularly.' The devout person is not religious for appearance or social advantage — their practice comes from genuine relationship with God."},"confess":{"meaning":"To acknowledge and agree with the truth about yourself before God. NOT the same as repentance — repentance is the TURN; confession is the AGREEMENT.","hebrew":"yadah (יָדָה) — to throw, give praise, confess. Used of the high priest confessing sins over the scapegoat (Lev 16:21).","greek":"homologeo (ὁμολογέω) — literally to say the same thing: to agree with God about what you did or what you believe. 1 John 1:9.","kingdom":"Repent = change direction. Confess = agree with God about the sin. You can confess without repenting (agreeing but not turning). True repentance normally includes confession. 1 John 1:9 — if we confess, He forgives and cleanses.","plain":"To say about yourself what God says. About sin: yes, I did it and it was wrong. About Jesus: yes, He is Lord. Confession is speaking truth aloud — not earning forgiveness but opening the door to it."},
"confession":{"plain":"Acknowledging and agreeing with the truth about yourself before God — admitting sin, or declaring what you believe.","def":"Confession (Hebrew: yadah — to throw, to give praise, to confess; Greek: homologeo — to say the same thing, to agree with) covers two distinct uses. (1) Confession of sin: agreeing with God about what you've done (1 John 1:9: 'If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just and will forgive us'). (2) Confession of faith: publicly declaring what you believe (Romans 10:9-10: 'with your mouth you confess and are saved'). The word means 'to say the same thing' — in confession of sin, you're agreeing with God's verdict; in confession of faith, you're agreeing with God's truth.","kingdom":"The two forms of confession are connected: confessing sin is agreeing that God is right about us; confessing faith is agreeing that God is right about Christ. Both are acts of agreement that orient us toward truth."},"prophetess":{"plain":"A woman who spoke God's word with divine authority — the Bible records several, including Miriam, Deborah, Huldah, Anna, and Philip's daughters.","def":"A prophetess (Hebrew: neviah; Greek: prophetis) was a woman through whom God spoke. Miriam (Exodus 15:20) is the first prophetess named — she led Israel in the Song of the Sea. Deborah (Judges 4-5) was both a prophetess and the judge of Israel. Huldah (2 Kings 22:14-20) gave the authoritative interpretation of the newly found law scroll to King Josiah — when the king sent his delegation, they went to a woman. Anna (Luke 2:36-38) prophesied over the infant Jesus in the Temple. Philip's four daughters prophesied (Acts 21:9). Isaiah's wife is called a prophetess (Isaiah 8:3). The gift was never limited by gender in the biblical record."},"overseer":{"plain":"A church leader responsible for the overall care and direction of a congregation — often translated 'bishop' in older versions.","def":"Overseer (Greek: episkopos — one who watches over, a supervisor) was a title for the leaders who shepherded local churches. It appears alongside 'elder' (presbuteros) and 'shepherd/pastor' (poimen) as overlapping descriptions of the same leadership role (Acts 20:17, 28; Titus 1:5-7). 1 Timothy 3:1-7 and Titus 1:6-9 give the qualifications: 'above reproach, faithful to his wife, temperate, self-controlled, respectable, hospitable, able to teach...a good manager of his own household.' The episkopos is a servant-leader responsible for the spiritual welfare of those in their care."},"Aramaic":{"plain":"The common spoken language of the Near East during the Babylonian and Persian periods — the everyday language Jesus likely spoke.","def":"Aramaic was a Semitic language closely related to Hebrew that became the dominant spoken language of the ancient Near East from the 7th century BC onward. After the Assyrian and Babylonian exiles, many Jews spoke Aramaic as their first language. Parts of the Bible are written in Aramaic (Daniel 2:4-7:28, Ezra 4:8-6:18, Jeremiah 10:11). Jesus' Aramaic words are preserved directly in the Gospels: 'Talitha koum' (Mark 5:41 — 'Little girl, arise'), 'Ephphatha' (Mark 7:34 — 'Be opened'), 'Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani' (Mark 15:34 — 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?'), 'Abba' (Mark 14:36)."},"immortality":{"plain":"The quality of not being able to die — in the Bible, something only God possesses fully, and that God gives to believers at the resurrection.","def":"Immortality (Greek: athanasia — deathlessness; aphtharsia — incorruption) is not the natural state of the human soul in biblical theology. God alone 'possesses immortality' in his own right (1 Timothy 6:16). Humans are mortal — subject to death — and receive immortality as a gift: 'For the perishable must clothe itself with the imperishable, and the mortal with immortality' (1 Corinthians 15:53). This will happen at the resurrection. The popular idea of an 'immortal soul' that survives death naturally (without resurrection) comes more from Greek Platonic philosophy than from Hebrew/NT thought.","kingdom":"The resurrection hope is bodily and future — immortality is not what you already have naturally but what you receive at the last day. 2 Timothy 1:10: Christ 'has destroyed death and has brought life and immortality to light through the gospel.'"},"citadel":{"plain":"The strongest fortified section of a city — the last place to fall, the innermost fortress where the king and valuables were kept.","def":"A citadel (Hebrew: bira — a palace-fortress; metsudah — stronghold) was the innermost and strongest defensive structure of an ancient city. When the outer walls fell, the citadel was the last refuge. The citadel of Zion (the Jebusite stronghold) was what David captured to make Jerusalem his capital (2 Samuel 5:7). God is called a 'citadel' metaphorically in the Psalms: 'The LORD is my rock, my fortress (metsudah) and my deliverer' (Psalm 18:2). Nehemiah rebuilt the walls and citadel of Jerusalem (Nehemiah 2:8)."},"cistern":{"plain":"A man-made underground tank for storing rainwater — the main source of water in areas without springs or rivers. In Proverbs, a picture of your own God-given resources.","def":"A cistern (Hebrew: bor — pit, cistern; also bor means dungeon or well) was a bottle-shaped underground cavity dug in rock or clay and plastered to hold water. In a region without abundant rivers, cisterns were life. Joseph was thrown into a cistern (Genesis 37:24). Jeremiah was lowered into a cistern-dungeon (Jeremiah 38:6). Proverbs 5:15: 'Drink water from your own cistern, running water from your own well' — a metaphor for faithfulness in marriage and contentment with your own resources.","ane":"Cisterns are found throughout ancient Israel — household cisterns and large communal ones. They were so important that failing to maintain one was a serious economic loss. Jeremiah 2:13 uses broken cisterns as God's charge against Israel: 'They have forsaken me, the spring of living water, and have dug their own cisterns, broken cisterns that cannot hold water.'"},"lamentation":{"plain":"A formal expression of grief — a poem or song of mourning over loss, death, or disaster.","def":"Lamentation (Hebrew: qinah — a dirge, a lamentation poem; evel — mourning) was a specific literary and ritual form for expressing grief. The Book of Lamentations is five poems of lamentation over the destruction of Jerusalem. Jeremiah is called 'the weeping prophet' for his sustained lament. The Psalms include multiple laments (Psalms 22, 44, 77, 88) — models for bringing raw pain honestly to God. Lament in the Bible is not forbidden grief or faithless despair — it is grief that still addresses God directly, still expects him to hear.","kingdom":"The lament psalms begin in darkness but most turn toward trust — not because circumstances changed but because the lamenter has spoken honestly to God and found he was heard. 'How long, O Lord?' is a prayer, not a rejection of faith."},"Persia":{"plain":"The great empire that conquered Babylon and allowed the Jewish exiles to return home — under kings like Cyrus, Darius, Xerxes, and Artaxerxes.","def":"Persia (Hebrew: Paras) was the empire that replaced Babylon as the dominant world power in 539 BC when Cyrus the Great conquered Babylon. The Persian Empire stretched from India to Greece and Egypt at its height. Three Persian kings are significant for Israel: Cyrus (Isaiah 44:28-45:1 — God calls him 'my anointed' before he was born, prophesying he would allow the temple to be rebuilt), Darius I (oversaw the completion of the second Temple — Ezra 6), and Ahasuerus/Xerxes (the king of the book of Esther). The return from exile under Persian permission was itself a fulfillment of covenant promise.","kingdom":"Isaiah 45:1 calling Cyrus 'my anointed (messiah)' is striking — a pagan king given the messianic title for the limited purpose of releasing God's people. God uses whomever he chooses as instruments of his purpose, regardless of whether they acknowledge him."},"transgress":{"plain":"To deliberately step over a line — willful rebellion against a known command. Stronger than sin or error.","def":"Transgress (Hebrew: pasha — to rebel, to break away, to transgress; Greek: parabainō — to go beside, to deviate) specifically implies willfulness — knowing the boundary and crossing it deliberately. Daniel 9:5: 'We have sinned and done wrong. We have been wicked and have rebelled; we have turned away from your commands and laws.' Isaiah 53:5: 'But he was pierced for our transgressions, he was crushed for our iniquities.' The suffering servant takes the penalty of willful rebellion.","kingdom":"The three Hebrew words for moral failure often appear together — sin (chatta't — missing the mark), iniquity (avon — twisted wrongdoing), and transgression (pesha — willful rebellion). Together they cover the full spectrum of human failure: accidental, habitual, and deliberate. Isaiah 53:5-6 says the servant bore all three."},"pardon":{"plain":"Forgiveness from a higher authority — being officially released from guilt and its consequences.","def":"Pardon (Hebrew: nasa — to lift, to carry away; salach — to forgive, to pardon specifically in a priestly/divine sense; Greek: aphesis — release, forgiveness) was an authoritative act of releasing guilt. God's pardon is distinct from human forgiveness — it carries official, binding, legal weight. Micah 7:18: 'Who is a God like you, who pardons sin and forgives the transgression of the remnant of his inheritance?' The distinction between salach (divine pardon) and nasa (lifting/carrying away) is that salach is used exclusively of God's pardon — only God can forgive in this way."},"hallowed":{"plain":"Made holy — set apart as sacred and to be treated as holy. 'Hallowed be your name' means 'may your name be treated as the holy thing it is.'","def":"Hallowed (Hebrew: qadash — to make holy; Greek: hagiazo — to set apart as holy) means declared and treated as sacred. The Lord's Prayer — 'Hallowed be your name' (Matthew 6:9) — is not describing what God's name already is but what we are asking to be true: 'May your name be acknowledged as the holy thing it is, by us and by the world.' To hallow God's name is to treat everything associated with God as sacred — to live in a way that represents him accurately and honors his character."},"phylacteries":{"plain":"Small leather boxes containing Scripture verses, worn on the forehead and arm during prayer — commanded in Deuteronomy and still worn by observant Jewish men today.","def":"Phylacteries (Greek: phylacterion — a safeguard; Hebrew: tefillin — prayer items) were small leather boxes holding strips of parchment with four passages from the Torah (Exodus 13:1-10, 13:11-16; Deuteronomy 6:4-9, 11:13-21). They were worn on the forehead between the eyes and on the left arm near the heart during morning prayer, following the literal interpretation of Deuteronomy 6:8: 'Tie them as symbols on your hands and bind them on your foreheads.' Jesus criticizes those who 'make their phylacteries broad' (Matthew 23:5) — making them larger than necessary to display their piety publicly."},"tassels":{"plain":"Fringes or knotted threads attached to the corners of a garment — commanded by God as a reminder to keep his commandments.","def":"Tassels (Hebrew: tsitsit — tassels, fringe; Greek: kraspedon — fringe, border) were knotted threads attached to the four corners of the outer garment. Numbers 15:38-40: 'Speak to the people of Israel and tell them to make tassels on the corners of their garments throughout their generations...so that you may look at it and remember all the commandments of the LORD.' The woman who touched 'the edge (kraspedon) of Jesus' garment' (Matthew 9:20) touched his tassels — which, in Jewish understanding, were associated with a rabbi's authority and divine power."},"heifer":{"plain":"A young cow that has not yet given birth — used in specific ancient purification and covenant rituals.","def":"A heifer (Hebrew: parah — young cow; eglah — a calf) was a young cow used in specific rituals. The most significant was the 'red heifer' (Numbers 19) — a rare, unblemished, never-yoked red cow burned outside the camp, whose ashes mixed with water produced the 'water of cleansing' for purification from corpse defilement. Hebrews 9:13: 'The blood of goats and bulls and the ashes of a heifer sprinkled on those who are ceremonially unclean sanctify them so that they are outwardly clean.' The red heifer ritual is one of the most discussed in Jewish tradition because its logic is not fully explained — it was a divine decree to be followed in trust."},"swaddling":{"plain":"Tightly wrapping a newborn baby in long strips of cloth — a standard practice in the ancient world to keep limbs straight and the baby secure.","def":"Swaddling clothes (Greek: sparganoo — to wrap in swaddling clothes; sparganon — swaddling bands) were long strips of cloth used to wrap a newborn tightly — legs straight, arms by the sides. The practice was universal in the ancient world, believed to support proper limb development. Luke 2:7: Mary 'wrapped him in swaddling cloths and placed him in a manger.' The swaddling cloths and the manger are the signs the angels give the shepherds (Luke 2:12) — not miraculous signs but signs of utter ordinariness and poverty. The one who holds the universe together is bundled in strips of cloth in an animal feeding trough.","kingdom":"The swaddling cloths of the birth and the linen cloths of the burial (John 20:6-7) bracket Jesus' earthly life. He was wrapped coming in and wrapped going out — fully human, fully mortal, fully in the story."},"manger":{"plain":"A feeding trough for livestock — a box cut into rock or made of wood where animals ate. This is where Mary laid the newborn Jesus.","def":"A manger (Greek: phatne — a feeding trough, a stall) was the stone or wooden box where grain or hay was placed for horses, cattle, or donkeys to eat from. It was located in the lower level of Palestinian houses (where animals were kept overnight for warmth and security) or in attached animal shelters. Luke 2:7: there was no room in the 'katalyma' (guest room) so Mary laid Jesus in a manger. The sign given to the shepherds — 'you will find a baby wrapped in cloths and lying in a manger' (Luke 2:12) — was specific enough to identify one particular trough in a town of troughs.","kingdom":"Isaiah 1:3: 'The ox knows its master, the donkey its owner's manger, but Israel does not know, my people do not understand.' The same word — manger — is where the owner provides food for the creatures who belong to him, and where the one who is the Bread of Life is first laid down."},"prudence":{"plain":"Practical wisdom — knowing the right thing to do in a given situation, not just knowing principles in theory.","def":"Prudence (Hebrew: ormah — shrewdness, prudence; sekel — good judgment, wisdom; Greek: phronesis — practical wisdom) is the wisdom that shows itself in good decisions and right timing. Proverbs highly values prudence: Proverbs 1:4: the purpose of Proverbs is to give 'prudence to the simple.' Proverbs 8:12: 'I, wisdom, dwell together with prudence; I possess knowledge and discretion.' Prudence is not cunning or self-serving cleverness — it is the practical expression of wisdom in the real world."}};
// repentance vocabulary — added for inline verse-text definitions
// appended directly to window.DEFINITIONS via Object.assign
Object.assign(window.DEFINITIONS, {
"repent":{"meaning":"Turn away from sin and turn toward God — not just feel bad, but actually change direction and life.","hebrew":"shuv (שׁוּב) — 'to turn back.' The prophetic command to Israel: return to YHWH. Spoken over 1,000 times in the OT.","greek":"metanoeo (μετανοέω) — to change one's mind and direction. The verb behind metanoia. META (change) + NOUS (mind).","kingdom":"'Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand' — Jesus' first public words (Matt 4:17; Mark 1:15). Repenting is how you enter and continue in the kingdom.","plain":"To turn completely — away from sin and toward God. A real change of direction, not just regret."},
"renew":{"meaning":"To be made new again — restored to freshness, especially the mind and heart.","hebrew":"chadash (חָדַשׁ) — to make new, renew. 'Create in me a clean heart... renew a right spirit' (Ps 51:10).","greek":"anakainoo (ἀνακαινόω) — to make new again; the inner self renewed daily (2 Cor 4:16). Kainos = new in quality, not just recent.","kingdom":"Romans 12:2 — 'Be transformed by the renewing of your mind.' The renewed mind discerns God's will and resists the world's mold.","plain":"Being made new inside — a fresh start God works in those who return to Him."},
"renewal":{"meaning":"The ongoing work of God making the inner person new — Spirit-powered, not self-generated.","hebrew":"chadash — newness; related to chodesh (new moon), each month a picture of renewal.","greek":"anakainosis (ἀνακαίνωσις) — renewal. Romans 12:2; Titus 3:5 'renewing of the Holy Spirit.'","kingdom":"Titus 3:5 — 'the washing of regeneration and renewing of the Holy Spirit' — renewal is the Spirit's work at salvation and throughout the life.","plain":"God making you new on the inside — not once only, but as an ongoing reality."},
"forgiveness":{"meaning":"The cancellation of a debt — releasing a person from what they owe because of sin.","hebrew":"selichah (סְלִיחָה) — forgiveness. 'A God ready to forgive' (Neh 9:17). Nasa (נָשָׂא) — to lift and carry away guilt.","greek":"aphesis (ἄφεσις) — release, sending away. 'Forgiveness of sins' (Luke 24:47; Acts 2:38).","kingdom":"Acts 13:38 — 'through this man is preached unto you the forgiveness of sins.' Forgiveness is the entry gift of the kingdom.","plain":"God releasing you from the guilt and penalty of sin — the debt canceled, the record cleared."},
"purify":{"meaning":"To make clean from defilement — made fit to approach God through and through.","hebrew":"taher (טָהֵר) — to be clean, make pure. Ps 51:7 'wash me and I shall be whiter than snow.'","greek":"hagnizo (ἁγνίζω) — to purify oneself; katharizo (καθαρίζω) — to cleanse. 1 John 3:3 'purifies himself as He is pure.'","kingdom":"James 4:8 — 'Purify your hearts, ye double-minded.' A command to those drawing near God.","plain":"To be cleaned completely — inside and out — by the blood of Christ and the work of the Spirit."},
"humble":{"meaning":"Low before God — not self-promoting, not self-reliant. To make yourself small so God is great.","hebrew":"anav (עָנָו) — meek, humble, lowly. Moses: 'very meek above all men' (Num 12:3).","greek":"tapeinoo (ταπεινόω) — to humble, bring low. Noun form tapeinophrosyne — humility of mind (Phil 2:3).","kingdom":"Matthew 23:12 — 'whosoever shall humble himself shall be exalted.' James 4:10 — 'Humble yourselves before the Lord and he will lift you up.'","plain":"To lower yourself before God — accepting that you depend on Him, not on yourself."},
"humility":{"meaning":"The settled posture of a heart that knows it depends entirely on God and does not grasp for status.","hebrew":"anavah (עֲנָוָה) — meekness, humility. Proverbs 22:4: 'By humility and the fear of the LORD are riches and honor and life.'","greek":"tapeinophrosyne (ταπεινοφροσύνη) — humility of mind, lowliness (Phil 2:3; Col 3:12).","kingdom":"Philippians 2:3-8 — Christ himself 'humbled himself and became obedient to death.' Humility is Christlike, not a weakness.","plain":"A settled awareness that you are not God, you need Him, and you will not compete with Him for the place that is His."},
"restore":{"meaning":"To bring back to an original or better state — healing what was broken, returning what was lost.","hebrew":"shuv (שׁוּב) in hiphil — to cause to return, restore. Shalam (שָׁלַם) — to make whole, complete.","greek":"apokathistemi (ἀποκαθίστημι) — to restore, set right again. Used of healing (Mark 3:5) and end-times restoration (Acts 3:21).","kingdom":"Acts 3:21 — 'the times of the restitution of all things.' Psalm 23:3 — 'He restores my soul.' Restoration is God's goal.","plain":"God bringing back what was broken, lost, or damaged — including people, relationships, and all of creation."},
"restoration":{"meaning":"The full return to rightness — healing of what was broken, recovery of what was lost, completion of all God promised.","hebrew":"shuv — return; tikun (תִּקּוּן) — correction, setting right (Talmudic but rooted in OT).","greek":"apokatastasis (ἀποκατάστασις) — restoration, reestablishment (Acts 3:21).","kingdom":"Acts 3:19-21 — 'Repent and turn back... until the time of the restoration of all things.' Restoration is the destination of history and the purpose of repentance.","plain":"Everything made right again — God's final goal for creation and for every person who turns back to Him."},
"reconcile":{"meaning":"To restore a broken relationship — to bring two estranged parties back into peace and fellowship.","greek":"katallasso (καταλλάσσω) — to reconcile, change from enmity to friendship. A marketplace word: exchanging hostility for agreement.","kingdom":"2 Corinthians 5:18-20 — 'God was in Christ, reconciling the world to himself... We are ambassadors for Christ: be reconciled to God.'","plain":"Being brought back into right relationship with God after sin had made you His enemy — through Christ's death."},
"reconciliation":{"meaning":"The state of restored peace and fellowship after separation and hostility.","greek":"katallagē (καταλλαγή) — reconciliation (Rom 5:11; 2 Cor 5:18-19). From kata + allasso — complete exchange.","kingdom":"Romans 5:10-11 — 'when we were enemies, we were reconciled to God by the death of his Son.' The cross achieves reconciliation; the gospel announces it.","plain":"The relationship with God restored — from enemy to child — through the death of Jesus Christ."},
"mourn":{"meaning":"To grieve deeply — in the kingdom context, the blessed grief that breaks over sin and its weight.","hebrew":"abal (אָבַל) — to mourn, lament. Used of national grief and personal loss.","greek":"pentheo (πενθέω) — to mourn, grieve deeply. 2 Corinthians 7:10 — 'godly sorrow worketh repentance.'","kingdom":"Matthew 5:4 — 'Blessed are they that mourn: for they shall be comforted.' The mourning Jesus calls blessed is grief over sin that produces real turning.","plain":"To grieve deeply before God — especially over sin. This grief is blessed because it leads to real change and to comfort."},
"forsake":{"meaning":"To leave entirely — to walk away and not return. Sin must be forsaken; God promises never to forsake His people.","hebrew":"azab (עָזַב) — to leave, forsake, abandon. Used of Israel forsaking God (Jer 2:13) and the promise God will never forsake the righteous (Ps 37:25).","greek":"egkataleipo (ἐγκαταλείπω) — to abandon completely. Hebrews 13:5 — 'I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee.'","kingdom":"Isaiah 55:7 — 'Let the wicked forsake his way.' Forsaking sin is the active side of repentance. God's refusal to forsake His people is the security of every believer.","plain":"To completely walk away from something and not go back. Forsake sin; know God will never forsake you."},
"convert":{"meaning":"To turn — to change sides and direction. The same root event as repentance, seen from the outside.","hebrew":"shuv (שׁוּב) — return, turn; often translated 'convert' in KJV. Same word as repent in the OT.","greek":"epistrepho (ἐπιστρέφω) — to turn toward, return, be converted (Acts 3:19; 9:35).","kingdom":"Acts 3:19 — 'Repent ye therefore and be converted, that your sins may be blotted out.' Conversion is the visible, outward evidence of inward repentance.","plain":"Turning completely — from one direction to the other. Not a label but a real U-turn from sin to God."},
"conversion":{"meaning":"The act and experience of turning from sin and self to God — the complete change of direction.","greek":"epistrophe (ἐπιστροφή) — conversion, turning (Acts 15:3).","kingdom":"Acts 15:3 — Paul and Barnabas 'declaring the conversion of the Gentiles' — conversion is God's purpose for all people and the evidence the gospel is working.","plain":"The full turn of a person's life toward God — not a minor adjustment but a complete change of direction."},
"sanctify":{"meaning":"To set apart for God — to consecrate, make holy. Both a status God declares and a process He works.","hebrew":"qadash (קָדַשׁ) — to make holy, consecrate, set apart. Used of priests, altars, days, and people.","greek":"hagiazo (ἁγιάζω) — to sanctify, make holy. John 17:17 'Sanctify them through thy truth; thy word is truth.'","kingdom":"1 Thessalonians 5:23 — 'the very God of peace sanctify you wholly.' Sanctification is God's act, not human effort alone; the Word and Spirit are the instruments.","plain":"To be set apart by God, for God — made holy through the truth of the Word and the work of the Spirit."}
});


// NT cultural vocabulary — misunderstood Western translations
Object.assign(window.DEFINITIONS, {
"condemnation":{"meaning": "A LEGAL VERDICT of guilty \u2014 not a feeling of shame. The court has ruled.", "greek": "katakrima (\u03ba\u03b1\u03c4\u03ac\u03ba\u03c1\u03b9\u03bc\u03b1, G2631) \u2014 the penalty/sentence that follows a verdict of guilt. Katakrino (G2632) \u2014 to pass sentence against. A courtroom word.", "kingdom": "Romans 8:1 \u2014 'There is THEREFORE NOW no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus.' This is not emotional reassurance. It is a reversal of a legal verdict. The case was retried at the cross and the sentence fell on Christ.", "plain": "The judge's verdict of guilty \u2014 and the sentence attached to it. Romans 8:1 says that verdict has been overturned for those in Christ."},
"mystery":{"meaning": "A secret that was hidden but is NOW REVEALED \u2014 not something puzzling or hard to understand.", "greek": "mysterion (\u03bc\u03c5\u03c3\u03c4\u03ae\u03c1\u03b9\u03bf\u03bd, G3466) \u2014 from Greek mystery cults (Eleusinian, Orphic), where it meant secrets known only to initiates. Paul co-opts the word: God's hidden plan for the world, NOW made public through the gospel.", "kingdom": "Colossians 1:26-27 \u2014 'the mystery hidden for ages and generations but now revealed to his saints... Christ in you, the hope of glory.' The mystery is not a puzzle. It is the announced secret. Matthew 13:11 \u2014 'the mysteries of the kingdom.'", "plain": "God's hidden plan that has now been made public. The secret is out \u2014 and the secret is Christ."},
"believe":{"meaning": "To commit, trust, entrust yourself to \u2014 not just 'think something is true in your head.'", "greek": "pisteuo (\u03c0\u03b9\u03c3\u03c4\u03b5\u03cd\u03c9, G4100) \u2014 same root as pistis (faith) and pistos (faithful). In commercial papyri: to entrust goods to someone you trust. John uses pisteuo EIS (believe INTO) \u2014 entering a relationship, not just agreeing about facts.", "kingdom": "John 3:16 \u2014 'whoever believes (pisteuo) in him.' James 2:19 \u2014 'Even the demons believe (pisteuo) \u2014 and shudder.' Demons have correct facts. That is not saving faith. Believing INTO Christ is commitment and entry into him.", "plain": "To trust completely and entrust yourself \u2014 not just agree in your head. The demons agreed; what they lack is commitment and surrender."},
"witness":{"meaning": "An eyewitness who testifies to what they personally experienced \u2014 not someone who tells others about Jesus secondhand.", "greek": "martys (\u03bc\u03ac\u03c1\u03c4\u03c5\u03c2, G3144) \u2014 the word our word martyr comes from. A witness is one who saw and testifies. Acts 1:8: 'You will be my witnesses' \u2014 you who saw him, touched him, ate with him after the resurrection.", "kingdom": "Hebrews 12:1 \u2014 'a great cloud of witnesses (martyron).' Revelation 2:13 \u2014 Antipas, 'my faithful martyr (martys).' The witness who dies for their testimony is where 'martyr' comes from. Being a witness is costly personal testimony.", "plain": "One who personally saw, experienced, and now testifies to what is real. The early church's witness cost many of them their lives \u2014 and that's where the word martyr came from."},
"mediator":{"meaning": "The authorized legal go-between who stands between two parties and represents both \u2014 not just a messenger.", "greek": "mesites (\u03bc\u03b5\u03c3\u03af\u03c4\u03b7\u03c2, G3316) \u2014 from mesos (middle) + the suffix for 'one who.' A legal intermediary, agent, arbiter. Attested in papyri for commercial legal transactions.", "kingdom": "1 Timothy 2:5 \u2014 'For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus.' He does not take sides \u2014 he is the authorized agent of both. Hebrews 9:15 \u2014 'mediator of a new covenant.'", "plain": "The one person who stands in the middle between God and humanity with full authorization to represent both sides. Jesus alone holds that position."},
"advocate":{"meaning": "One called to stand alongside you \u2014 your defense attorney before God AND the Comforter sent to stand with you in daily life.", "greek": "parakletos (\u03c0\u03b1\u03c1\u03ac\u03ba\u03bb\u03b7\u03c4\u03bf\u03c2, G3875) \u2014 para (alongside) + kaleo (to call). A legal defense attorney called to stand beside the accused. Also: comforter, helper, counselor. John 14:16,26; 15:26; 16:7 \u2014 the Holy Spirit. 1 John 2:1 \u2014 Jesus himself is our parakletos with the Father.", "kingdom": "English translators have split the word into 'Comforter' (KJV), 'Advocate' (NRSV), 'Helper' (ESV), 'Counselor' (NIV) \u2014 none fully captures it. He is the Spirit who stands with you in court AND in daily life as the one who comes alongside.", "plain": "Your defense attorney before God (when you sin, 1 John 2:1) AND the one who walks alongside you in daily life (the Spirit, John 14:16). Both dimensions are in one word."},
"obedience":{"meaning": "To listen UNDER \u2014 to hear with the posture of a willing servant who then does what is heard.", "greek": "hupakoe (\u1f51\u03c0\u03b1\u03ba\u03bf\u03ae, G5218) \u2014 from hupo (under) + akouo (to hear/listen). Literally: listening from under, listening in submission. Romans 1:5 \u2014 'obedience of faith.' Philippians 2:8 \u2014 Christ 'became obedient (hupekoos) to the point of death.'", "kingdom": "Romans 5:19 \u2014 'by the one man's obedience the many will be made righteous.' Christ's obedience reverses Adam's disobedience. The kingdom citizen is one who has trained their hearing to listen from under God's authority.", "plain": "Listening with full intention to do what you hear. Not reluctant compliance \u2014 the root means coming under the voice and acting from that position."},
"anointed":{"meaning": "Set apart, consecrated, and empowered for a specific role by the pouring of oil \u2014 the meaning behind both Messiah and Christ.", "hebrew": "mashiach (\u05de\u05b8\u05e9\u05b4\u05c1\u05d9\u05d7\u05b7, H4899) \u2014 anointed one. Prophets, priests, and kings were all anointed. The Messiah is the one who holds all three offices perfectly.", "greek": "Christos (\u03a7\u03c1\u03b9\u03c3\u03c4\u03cc\u03c2, G5547) \u2014 the exact Greek translation of mashiach. 'Jesus Christ' = 'Jesus the Anointed One.' Christ is not his last name \u2014 it is his title and identity.", "kingdom": "Luke 4:18 \u2014 'The Spirit of the Lord is upon me BECAUSE HE HAS ANOINTED ME.' Jesus publicly claimed the anointing of Isaiah 61 in his first sermon. The Anointed One has come; the kingdom has arrived.", "plain": "Set apart by God, empowered by the Spirit, for the specific mission. 'Christ' is a title meaning The Anointed One \u2014 prophet, priest, and king in one person."},
"power":{"meaning": "NT Greek has TWO completely different words both translated 'power' \u2014 and they mean different things.", "greek": "dunamis (\u03b4\u03cd\u03bd\u03b1\u03bc\u03b9\u03c2, G1411) \u2014 inherent energy, raw ability, the root of dynamite and dynamic. Acts 1:8: 'you will receive POWER (dunamis) when the Holy Spirit comes.' Exousia (\u1f10\u03be\u03bf\u03c5\u03c3\u03af\u03b1, G1849) \u2014 authority, the legal RIGHT to act; delegated rule. Matthew 28:18: 'All AUTHORITY (exousia) has been given to me.'", "kingdom": "A police officer has authority (exousia) to make arrests. If the criminal is stronger, the officer lacks the physical power (dunamis). Jesus has both: all authority AND all power. His followers receive the Spirit's dunamis (Acts 1:8) to carry out the exousia of their commission (Matt 28:18-20).", "plain": "Dunamis = the ability to do something (energy, power). Exousia = the right and authority to do something. You need both \u2014 and Jesus delegates both to his people."},
"logos":{"meaning": "The divine rational principle behind all reality \u2014 AND the personal creative Word of God \u2014 who became a person: Jesus.", "greek": "logos (\u03bb\u03cc\u03b3\u03bf\u03c2, G3056) \u2014 word, reason, rational ordering principle. Greek philosophers (Heraclitus, the Stoics) used logos for the rational force ordering the universe. Jewish Scripture used it for God's active creative word (Ps 33:6 'by the WORD of the LORD the heavens were made').", "kingdom": "John 1:1-14 \u2014 'In the beginning was the Logos... and the Logos became flesh and dwelt among us.' John shatters both frameworks: to Greeks, the logos was impersonal. To Jews, God's Word was activity, not a separate Person. John says: the rational ordering principle of the universe is a Person who moved into the neighborhood.", "plain": "The full expression of God's mind, character, and creative power \u2014 who is a Person. 'The Word became flesh' (John 1:14) \u2014 everything God thinks and says and is took human form in Jesus."}
,
"Gehenna":{"def":"Valley of Ben-Hinnom (ge-hinnom) south of Jerusalem — once a site of child sacrifice to Molech, later a burning trash dump outside the city. Jesus used it 11 times as the image of final destruction (Matt 5:22; Mark 9:43-47). Greek geenna (G1067).","plain":"Jesus's word for the place of final judgment — drawn from the garbage fire outside Jerusalem where nothing good survives. Not the same as Sheol (the grave) — Gehenna is the fate of the wicked after judgment."},
"Sheol":{"hebrew":"שְאוֹל","translit":"she'ol","strongs":"H7585","def":"The Hebrew realm of the dead — where all the dead go, not just the wicked. Dark, silent, a land of no return (Job 10:21; Ps 88:3-5). The OT offers limited revelation about what happens there.","plain":"'The grave' or underworld in the Old Testament. All the dead go there — it is the place of the dead, not specifically a place of punishment. Greater clarity on the afterlife comes in the New Testament."},
"Hades":{"greek":"ᾅδης (G86)","def":"The Greek NT equivalent of Hebrew Sheol — the realm of the dead. In Luke 16:23 the rich man suffers in Hades; Jesus holds its keys (Rev 1:18). In Revelation, Hades itself is thrown into the lake of fire at the final judgment (Rev 20:14).","plain":"The realm of the dead in Greek — the NT's word for Sheol. Not the same as Gehenna (hell-fire). In the end, even Hades gets destroyed."},
"mammon":{"def":"Aramaic mamona — money, wealth, property. Jesus personifies it as a competing master: 'You cannot serve God and mammon' (Matt 6:24). Wealth functions as an idol because it generates trust, security, and loyalty the way only God should.","plain":"Money — but specifically money as a spiritual power that competes with God for your allegiance. Jesus treats it as a rival lord, not just a neutral tool."},
"hosanna":{"hebrew":"הושִיעָה נָּא","translit":"hoshi'a-na","strongs":"H3467","def":"'Save us, please!' — from Psalm 118:25, the royal processional psalm sung when the king entered the temple. The crowd's shout at the Triumphal Entry (Matt 21:9) was a loaded messianic acclamation — they were quoting the psalm that welcomed Israel's deliverer.","plain":"A cry of 'Save us now!' that also welcomes a deliverer. When the crowd shouted it at Jesus entering Jerusalem, they were quoting the Passover psalm that announced the king. Praise and urgent petition in one shout."},
"selah":{"hebrew":"סֶלָה","translit":"selah","strongs":"H5542","def":"A liturgical or musical term appearing 71 times in Psalms and 3 times in Habakkuk. Exact meaning uncertain. Likely a pause instruction — 'lift up' (the voice or the melody) or a signal to stop and reflect. The Septuagint translates it diapsalma (interlude).","plain":"A musical pause marked in the Psalms. Nobody knows exactly what it meant, but the effect is: stop, be still, and absorb what was just said before moving on."},
"amen":{"hebrew":"אָמֵן","translit":"amen","strongs":"H543","def":"From emunah (faithfulness, truth). 'So be it' — affirming that what was said is reliable and true. Jesus's use of 'Amen I say to you' (Matt 5:18) was shocking: rabbis cited Scripture or God; Jesus cited himself, claiming his own word carries divine authority.","plain":"'Truly' or 'so be it' — a declaration that something is reliable. When Jesus said 'Amen I say to you,' he used his OWN authority as the guarantee, which no rabbi ever did."},
"hallelujah":{"hebrew":"הַלְלוּיָהּ","translit":"hallelu-yah","def":"'Praise YHWH!' — hallelu (plural imperative: you all praise!) + Yah (short form of YHWH). An imperative calling a group to join in praising God. Appears at the close of Psalms 104-106, 111-117, 146-150, and four times in Revelation 19 as the shout of heavenly multitudes.","plain":"A command to everyone: 'Praise God!' Not just an expression of worship — an invitation to others to join in. The hallelujah psalms were sung at Passover, and Revelation brings them into heaven itself."},
"abba":{"def":"Aramaic abba — the intimate form of 'father,' similar to 'papa' or 'dear father.' Jesus uses it in Gethsemane (Mark 14:36); the Spirit enables believers to cry 'Abba, Father' (Rom 8:15; Gal 4:6). Addressing God this way was a radical departure from the formal reverence typical in 1st-century Jewish prayer.","plain":"'Papa' or 'Father' in Aramaic — the intimate word a child uses for their father. Jesus used it in his darkest moment of prayer, and says his followers can use it too. It reframes your relationship to God as family, not just servant."},
"maranatha":{"def":"Aramaic — either maran atha ('Our Lord has come') or marana tha ('Come, O Lord!'). Found in 1 Cor 16:22 and the early church's eucharistic liturgy (Didache 10:6). The oldest recorded Christian prayer, likely prayed while sharing the Lord's Supper. Echoed in Rev 22:20: 'Come, Lord Jesus.'","plain":"'Come, Lord' in Aramaic — one of the earliest Christian prayers, prayed at the Lord's Supper. A cry for Christ's return and an affirmation that he is already Lord."},
"corban":{"hebrew":"קָרְבָּן","translit":"korban","strongs":"H7133","def":"An offering declared sacred and dedicated to God. Jesus condemns its abuse in Mark 7:11: people declared money 'corban' (vowed to the temple) to avoid supporting elderly parents — a legal loophole that nullified the 5th commandment. They kept the money but claimed it was God's.","plain":"A vow dedicating something to God. Jesus condemned using it as a loophole — declaring your money corban so you did not have to give it to your parents, while still using it yourself. Religious tradition weaponized to dodge a clear command of God."},
"paradise":{"greek":"παράδεισος (G3857)","def":"From Old Persian pairi-daeza (walled garden or park) into Greek paradeisos. The Septuagint uses it for the Garden of Eden. In the NT: (1) the realm of the righteous dead (Luke 23:43 — 'today you will be with me in paradise'); (2) the third heaven (2 Cor 12:4); (3) the eschatological restoration (Rev 2:7 — the tree of life in the paradise of God).","plain":"'Garden' — the word for Eden, and then for where the righteous go after death, and finally for the restored world at the end. The whole biblical story moves from garden lost to garden restored."}
});
